window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("deployment", {"topics":[{"key":"deployment/customer-vpc-on-prem-air-gapped","sources":["Official docs: Helm","Official docs: Kubernetes","Official docs: Sigstore (cosign)"],"why":"- **SaaS assumes the customer sends you data.** For a bank's transaction records, a hospital's clinical\n  notes, a defence contractor's documents or a government's citizen data, the contract, regulator or\n  classification policy says the data stays inside a perimeter they control. An agent that reads those\n  records through tools is processing them, wherever its model sits.\n- **Encryption promises do not answer the rule.** \"We encrypt it and promise not to train on it\" does\n  not satisfy a rule about *where processing happens* and *who can be compelled to hand it over*.\n- **Copying your SaaS stack fails three ways.** It quietly depends on the open internet; you no longer\n  own the platform; and you lose your eyes (no dashboards, no logs, no hot-fix at 2 a.m.). For an agent,\n  losing traces means losing the only record of why it chose a tool.\n- **So it is a different product shape:** a signed, self-contained bundle, one configuration surface\n  for every environment decision, and the customer's platform team holding the keys. Each step inward\n  trades your operability for their control.\n\n### The hidden internet dependencies\n| Dependency | Why it breaks |\n|---|---|\n| Container images from public registries | outbound pull a review will question, an air gap breaks |\n| Model weights fetched from a hub at start-up | same, and often 100+ GB |\n| Python packages installed at build time inside their network | no package index reachable |\n| Licence checks and feature flags served from your SaaS | call-home traffic |\n| Traces shipped to an observability vendor | data leaving the perimeter |\n| Hosted LLM API behind the agent loop | every step is an outbound call carrying customer data |\n| Tools that call public SaaS APIs (search, web fetch) | no route out, and a data leak if there were |\n\nPlatform you no longer own: their Kubernetes version, storage classes, certificate authority, egress\nproxy and identity provider.","how":"### Three targets, one spectrum\n| | Customer VPC | On-prem | Air-gapped |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Where it runs | their AWS, Azure or GCP account | their datacentre or colocation | their facility, no network path out |\n| Managed services | yes (their IAM, storage, DB, KMS) | none | none |\n| Models | managed service over private endpoint, or open-weight on GPUs in the VPC | open-weight on their GPUs | open-weight, shipped in |\n| Updates | registry replication | mirror they populate | physical media or diode |\n| Your access | optional cross-account role | usually none | none |\n| Agent tools | their internal APIs plus managed services | their internal APIs only | their internal APIs only, no web tools |\n\n**Customer VPC (often sold as \"bring your own cloud\").**\n- **Their identity and data stores.** Containers run in a VPC/VNet they own; identity is their IAM;\n  storage, databases and KMS keys are theirs. The agent's tool credentials come from their IAM roles.\n- **Model calls stay private.** Calls go to the cloud's managed model service (Bedrock, Azure OpenAI,\n  Vertex AI) over a private endpoint, or to open-weight models on GPU instances in the same VPC.\n- **Vendor access is narrow.** If any, a cross-account role with a time-bound, logged session and an\n  `ExternalId` condition to prevent the confused-deputy problem.\n- **Control plane / data plane split.** Many vendors host a **control plane** (licensing, version\n  catalogue, configuration, upgrade orchestration) while the customer hosts the **data plane** (every\n  component that touches customer data: the agent loop, tools, retrieval, memory, traces).\n- **Why the split works.** The requirement is data custody, not metadata, so you keep operability for\n  everything else. It must be optional *because* some customers reject any outbound connection.\n\n**On-prem.**\n- **No managed services.** You get a Kubernetes distribution (OpenShift, Rancher, vanilla upstream, k3s)\n  at whatever version they run, their storage classes and their ingress controller.\n- **Their hardware and trust.** GPUs come through a device plugin or the NVIDIA GPU Operator; there is\n  an internal CA; identity is their directory through OIDC or LDAP.\n- **Egress through a proxy.** Outbound traffic, if allowed at all, goes through a proxy that often\n  terminates TLS with its own certificate.\n- **Deliverable.** A Helm chart or operator plus images in a registry they can reach, usually a mirror\n  they populate from your registry.\n\n**Air-gapped.**\n- **Everything on-prem implies, plus no network path.** Artifacts cross on physical media or through a\n  one-way transfer device (a data diode or a cross-domain solution).\n- **Inside:** a mirrored container registry, a package mirror, their own time source and their own CA.\n- **Nothing can call home.** No licence server, no telemetry, no automatic update, no hosted model, no\n  web-search tool.\n- **Updates and support.** Updates are a new bundle carried in; support is a log bundle carried out.\n\n### The delivery pipeline\n1. **Build deterministically.** Pin every dependency, build images from a locked manifest, and ensure\n   nothing at container start needs the network.\n   - Model weights (LLM, embedding model, reranker) are baked into an image or seeded into a persistent\n     volume from the bundle.\n   - `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` and `TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE=1` turn Hugging Face libraries into pure local loaders.\n   - Tokenizer files are shipped, not fetched.\n2. **Sign and attest.** Sign images and the bundle manifest (cosign is the common choice), ship an SBOM\n   (SPDX or CycloneDX), and publish SHA-256 hashes. Done *because* the receiving side cannot ask you \"is\n   this file right?\" and *because* the security review will ask how they know the artifact is yours and\n   unmodified.\n3. **Package.** Images exported with `docker save` or `skopeo copy` into a tarball or OCI layout, the\n   chart, the weights, a wheelhouse built with `pip download` for anything installed on-site, the docs,\n   the agent's eval set, and a verify script.\n4. **Transfer.** Registry-to-registry replication for VPC and connected on-prem; media or diode for the\n   air gap. A 70B-parameter model in 16-bit precision is roughly 140 GB of weights (2 bytes per\n   parameter), so the transfer is a planned activity, not a download.\n5. **Install.** `helm install` with a values file holding every environment decision: registry host,\n   storage class, ingress class, CA bundle, proxy, model endpoint, tool endpoints, identity provider,\n   resource limits. Hard-coded assumptions are the single biggest source of failed installs.\n6. **Verify.** Smoke tests that exercise the real path inside their network: a model call, a retrieval,\n   an authenticated request, a write to their database, and one end-to-end agent task that calls a tool.\n7. **Operate.**\n   - Logs, metrics and agent traces stay inside and flow to *their* backends (ship Prometheus and\n     OpenTelemetry collector configs that point at them).\n   - A `support-bundle` command gathers redacted logs, config, version info and failed agent traces into\n     a tarball they can send you.\n   - Upgrade is a new bundle plus `helm upgrade`; rollback is the previous chart version, which only\n     works if your schema migrations (including agent memory and checkpoint tables) are backward\n     compatible.\n\n### Signing for an air gap: key pair vs keyless\n- **Key pair (simplest).** A long-lived key pair whose public key ships out of band; verification needs\n  nothing but the key, as long as you skip the transparency-log upload when signing (or ship the\n  signature bundle), since cosign v2 otherwise looks the entry up in Rekor online.\n- **Most air-gapped sites choose key pairs,** trading key-rotation work for simpler verification.\n- **Keyless (cosign's default).** Ties signatures to a short-lived certificate (Fulcio) and a\n  transparency log (Rekor).\n- **Keyless offline is possible but heavy.** It verifies offline only if you also ship the signature\n  bundle with its log inclusion proof and the Sigstore trusted root, and keep that root current: extra\n  machinery many air-gapped sites decline.\n\n### What changes about the agent itself\n- **Licensing.** A signed offline licence file with an expiry and a grace period, checked locally.\n- **Feature flags.** Move from a remote service into the values file, including which tools are enabled.\n- **Trust the customer's CA.** Every HTTP client, the model client and each tool adapter, reads their CA\n  bundle (`SSL_CERT_FILE`, `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE`) *because* their proxy re-signs traffic.\n- **Secrets.** Tool credentials come from their secret store, not yours.\n- **Time bombs fail loudly.** Certificate expiry, licence expiry and token lifetimes must fail loudly and\n  early *because* nobody will be watching a dashboard when they fail silently.\n- **The model is whatever you shipped.** On-prem and air-gapped customers run open-weight models on vLLM\n  or similar, so your evaluation suite must have run on that exact model and prompt set before the\n  bundle was cut. Tool-calling accuracy is often what drops first when you leave a frontier model.\n- **Tool set shrinks to what is inside.** Web search and public SaaS tools are removed or pointed at\n  internal equivalents, and the system prompt and tool descriptions are re-evaluated for the new set.","example":"### Offline preflight for an agent's model and tool hosts\nAn init container that blocks the agent if the model or any tool endpoint names a host outside the\nvalues-file allowlist, so a leftover public tool fails at install, not mid-task with customer data in\nthe request.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Preflight: every model and tool endpoint must be an approved internal host.\"\"\"\nimport os, sys\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\n\nALLOWED = set(os.environ[\"AGENT_ALLOWED_HOSTS\"].split(\",\"))  # from the Helm values file\n\nAGENT_CONFIG = {\n    \"model\": {\"base_url\": os.environ[\"MODEL_BASE_URL\"]},          # e.g. in-cluster vLLM service\n    \"tools\": {\n        \"search_tickets\": \"https://tickets.internal.example/api\",\n        \"lookup_customer\": \"https://crm.internal.example/v2\",\n        \"web_search\": os.environ.get(\"WEB_SEARCH_URL\", \"\"),       # must be empty inside an air gap\n    },\n}\n\ndef check():\n    problems = []\n    host = urlparse(AGENT_CONFIG[\"model\"][\"base_url\"]).hostname\n    if host not in ALLOWED:\n        problems.append(f\"model host {host!r} not allowed\")\n    for name, url in AGENT_CONFIG[\"tools\"].items():\n        if not url:\n            continue                                             # tool disabled for this site\n        h = urlparse(url).hostname\n        if h not in ALLOWED:\n            problems.append(f\"tool {name}: host {h!r} not allowed\")\n    for p in problems:\n        print(\"PREFLIGHT FAIL\", p)\n    return not problems\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(0 if check() else 1)    # run as an init container; the agent never starts on failure\n```\n\n### Bundle manifest tool: build and verify\n- **Where it runs.** `build` on your connected side; `verify` on the customer's side before anything\n  enters their mirror registry, so a corrupted 140 GB copy fails before the install, not during it.\n- **Authorship too.** The manifest is then signed with `cosign sign-blob` in key-pair mode, with no\n  transparency-log entry or with the signature bundle shipped, so `cosign verify-blob --key` works\n  offline and the customer checks authorship as well as integrity.\n- **Three distinct rejections.** A file that is missing (truncated transfer); a file whose hash differs\n  (corruption or tampering); a file present but not in the manifest (added after signing, which an\n  installer that globs the directory would otherwise load).\n- **Streaming hash.** Hashing reads 1 MB blocks *because* a 140 GB weights file cannot be read into memory.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Build and verify an offline install bundle: `build <dir> <version>` or `verify <dir>`.\"\"\"\nimport hashlib, json, sys, pathlib\n\ndef sha256(path, chunk=1 << 20):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(chunk), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\nSKIP = {\"manifest.json\", \"manifest.json.sig\"}   # the manifest and its cosign signature\n\ndef payload(root):\n    return [p for p in root.rglob(\"*\") if p.is_file() and str(p.relative_to(root)) not in SKIP]\n\ndef build(bundle_dir, version):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    files = payload(root)\n    manifest = {\"version\": version, \"artifacts\": {\n        str(p.relative_to(root)): {\"sha256\": sha256(p), \"bytes\": p.stat().st_size} for p in files}}\n    (root / \"manifest.json\").write_text(json.dumps(manifest, indent=1))\n    total = sum(a[\"bytes\"] for a in manifest[\"artifacts\"].values())\n    print(f\"{len(files)} artifacts, {total / 1e9:.1f} GB\")\n\ndef verify(bundle_dir):\n    root = pathlib.Path(bundle_dir)\n    manifest = json.loads((root / \"manifest.json\").read_text())\n    listed = manifest[\"artifacts\"]\n    present = {str(p.relative_to(root)) for p in payload(root)}\n    missing = [r for r in listed if r not in present]\n    altered = [r for r in listed if r in present and sha256(root / r) != listed[r][\"sha256\"]]\n    extra = sorted(present - set(listed))\n    for label, items in ((\"MISSING\", missing), (\"ALTERED\", altered), (\"UNLISTED\", extra)):\n        if items:\n            print(label, items)\n    ok = not (missing or altered or extra)\n    print(\"OK\" if ok else \"REJECT BUNDLE\")\n    return ok\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    cmd, path = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]\n    if cmd == \"build\":\n        build(path, sys.argv[3])\n    else:\n        sys.exit(0 if verify(path) else 1)\n# Typical layout: images/app-1.8.3.tar, charts/assistant-1.8.3.tgz, models/<name>/, wheels/, docs/\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: sizing the weights in an air-gap agent delivery\nWeights are parameters × bytes per parameter, so precision choice decides the media and transfer plan.\nLink speeds are illustrative; a diode or cross-domain solution is often slower and is set by the site.\n\n| Model | Precision | Bytes/param | Weights | At 1 Gbps | At 10 Gbps |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7B | 16-bit | 2 | ~14 GB | ~2 min | ~11 s |\n| 70B | 16-bit | 2 | ~140 GB | ~19 min | ~2 min |\n| 70B | 8-bit | 1 | ~70 GB | ~9 min | ~56 s |\n| 70B | 4-bit | 0.5 | ~35 GB | ~5 min | ~28 s |\n\n- **Read it as:** 140 GB × 8 = 1,120 Gb, and 1,120 Gb ÷ 1 Gbps ≈ 1,120 s ≈ 19 min, before hashing,\n  media handling and the customer's inbound scanning.\n- **Each extra precision ships as another full copy**, so bundling 16-bit and 8-bit doubles the media;\n  ship the one your agent eval suite (including tool-calling cases) approved.\n- **An agent ships more than one model.** The embedding model and any reranker are small next to the\n  LLM, but missing either one breaks retrieval at start-up.\n\n### Offline-first CI gate: deny egress during the install test\nRun the install test, including one end-to-end agent task, in a namespace whose pods may only reach each\nother and cluster DNS. Any hidden download or public tool call now fails in your CI instead of the\ncustomer's cluster. NetworkPolicy is enforced only if the cluster's CNI plugin supports it.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\n  name: offline-install-test\nspec:\n  podSelector: {}                 # every pod in this namespace\n  policyTypes: [\"Egress\"]\n  egress:\n    - to:\n        - podSelector: {}         # same-namespace traffic only\n    - to:\n        - namespaceSelector:\n            matchLabels:\n              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system\n      ports:\n        - { protocol: UDP, port: 53 }\n        - { protocol: TCP, port: 53 }\n```","patterns":["**Offline-first CI gate with a real agent task.** On every release candidate, install the bundle into a namespace with the deny-egress NetworkPolicy above and run the smoke tests plus a scripted agent task that calls each enabled tool. Monitor DNS and connection-timeout errors in pod logs; any public hostname in them is a release blocker.","**One values file for every environment decision.** Expose registry host, storage class, ingress class, CA bundle, proxy, model endpoint and tool endpoints as chart values (Bitnami-style charts use `global.imageRegistry` for the registry). Lint with `helm template` against each customer's values file before shipping.","**Per-model agent evals in the bundle.** Ship the eval set and a runner so the customer or your FDE can re-run task-completion and tool-call accuracy on their GPUs after install; track scores per site and bundle version.","**Mirror relocation with skopeo.** Use `skopeo copy` to move images into an OCI layout for the media, then into the customer's mirror, keeping digests unchanged so signatures still verify. Monitor signature-verification failures at admission.","**Expiry warnings well ahead of the date.** Have the licence and certificate checks log and surface a warning weeks before expiry and again during the grace period. Monitor days-to-expiry per site from support bundles.","**Bounded version support and expand-then-contract migrations.** Publish how many bundle versions you support and require sites to upgrade into that window, so back-ports stay finite. Add columns and tables first, remove old ones a release later, so `helm rollback` to the previous chart still finds a schema it understands. Test rollback in CI."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is a web of outbound calls: the model API, tool endpoints, an\n  embedding model, a vector store, a tracing backend. Inside a customer perimeter every one of those must\n  resolve to something the customer hosts, or the loop fails on its first step.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model client's base URL, the tool registry's endpoint hosts,\n  framework telemetry and tracing exporters, `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` for local weights, and the Helm values\n  file that wires all of them to the customer's services.\n- **What you control.** Which open-weight model the agent is evaluated on, which tools may exist inside,\n  where traces go, and an offline preflight that fails install if any tool or model host is outside the\n  allowed set.\n- **What you can skip.** Data-diode hardware and cross-domain solution internals. Know that artifacts\n  cross on media or one-way links and that the customer's site sets the speed.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents for regulated enterprises.** A bank's support agent runs in the bank's VPC, calling Bedrock or\n  Azure OpenAI over a private endpoint and tools on internal APIs, so no record leaves their account.\n- **On-prem document agents.** A hospital's RAG assistant runs open-weight models on the hospital's GPUs,\n  with retrieval over notes that may never reach a public cloud.\n- **Air-gapped analyst agents.** A defence or government site receives the agent, its models and its eval\n  set on media, and sends back only redacted support bundles.\n- **Bring-your-own-cloud agent platforms.** A vendor keeps a control plane for licensing and upgrades\n  while each customer hosts the data plane that runs agent loops and tools.","mini_project":"**Make a tool-calling agent pass an offline install gate (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove a small agent (one model endpoint, two tools, one retrieval step) runs with zero\n  internet access.\n- **Steps:** containerise the agent with weights baked in and `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1` set; serve a small\n  open-weight model (0.5-3B) in-cluster; add the host preflight as an init container.\n- **Then gate it:** deploy to a local k3s cluster (it enforces NetworkPolicy out of the box) or kind with\n  a policy-enforcing CNI, in a namespace with the deny-egress NetworkPolicy; run a scripted task that\n  calls both tools; then add a public web-search tool and watch the preflight fail.\n- **Done when:** the clean install completes the task with no DNS errors for public hosts, the polluted\n  install is refused before the agent starts, and you have a one-page list of every outbound dependency\n  you removed."},"exercise":"**Goal:** prove an agent you have already built can run with no network path out.\n- **Inputs:** any small agent of yours (one model call, one or two tools), Docker, and the two scripts\n  above.\n- **Constraints:** the model must be served locally (a 0.5-3B open-weight model is enough); nothing may\n  be fetched at container start.\n- **Deliver:** a table of every outbound dependency and how you removed it; a bundle directory with a\n  manifest; one command that runs a scripted agent task.\n- **Acceptance:** the task completes with agent and model on a `docker network create --internal`\n  network (or in one container under `--network none`); `verify` rejects the bundle\n  after you flip one byte in a weights file and after you add a stray file; pointing one tool at a public\n  host makes the preflight exit 1 before the agent starts.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a hospital wants your clinical-notes agent on-prem. You validated it on a 70B model in\n16-bit; their server has two 48 GB GPUs. Media goes through a scanning process that takes three days,\nand they expect a fix bundle every month. Plan the delivery and say what you must re-test.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- notes 140 GB of 16-bit weights cannot fit in 96 GB, so proposes 8-bit (~70 GB, little KV-cache room)\n  or 4-bit (~35 GB), and ships only the one precision that passed;\n- re-runs the agent eval suite, especially tool-call accuracy, on exactly that model and precision;\n- keeps weights out of monthly bundles unless they change, so each fix is images and chart only, and\n  tells them even an urgent fix lands three days after it ships;\n- requires reversible migrations, a tested rollback, expiry warnings and a support-bundle command.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Deterministic build\"] --> S[\"Sign images and manifest\"]\n    S --> P[\"Package bundle\"]\n    P --> T{\"Target?\"}\n    T -->|\"VPC or connected\"| R[\"Registry replication\"]\n    T -->|\"air-gapped\"| M[\"Media or data diode\"]\n    R --> V[\"Verify hashes and signature\"]\n    M --> V\n    V -->|\"reject\"| P\n    V -->|\"ok\"| MR[(\"Customer mirror registry\")]\n    MR --> I[\"helm install with values file\"]\n    I --> ST[\"Agent smoke task inside network\"]\n    ST --> O[\"Operate: their logs, support bundle\"]","caption":"Follow the agent bundle from your connected build side across the boundary; nothing on the right may\nreach back out."},"interview":[{"q":"How do customer-VPC, on-prem and air-gapped deployments differ, and why does a customer choose one?","a":"- **Customer VPC:** your software in their cloud account, with their IAM, storage and KMS; chosen when\n  custody matters but they already trust a cloud, so managed model services stay available.\n- **On-prem:** their hardware and Kubernetes with no managed services; chosen with no cloud footprint, a\n  ban on public cloud, or GPUs needed next to their systems.\n- **Air-gapped:** no network path at all; chosen only when a regulator or classification policy demands\n  it, because every update and support exchange becomes physical."},{"q":"You are moving a tool-calling agent from your SaaS into a customer's air-gapped site. What changes in the agent itself?","a":"- **The model:** a hosted frontier model becomes an open-weight model on their GPUs, so re-run the agent\n  eval suite, especially tool-selection and argument accuracy, on exactly that model and precision.\n- **The tools:** public tools (web search, SaaS APIs) are removed or re-pointed at internal services, and\n  the prompt and tool descriptions are re-evaluated for the smaller set.\n- **The operations:** traces go to their collector, a preflight rejects non-internal hosts, and failed\n  runs leave the site only through a redacted support bundle."},{"q":"Your chart installs cleanly in your lab but pods sit in ImagePullBackOff at the customer. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the event first:** `kubectl describe pod` shows which image reference failed and why\n  (unreachable host, auth, unknown manifest).\n- **Likely causes:** a hard-coded public registry host instead of their mirror, images not yet loaded\n  into the mirror, or a missing pull secret.\n- **Fix at the source:** make the registry a chart value, verify the bundle was imported by digest, and\n  add the offline CI gate so the next release cannot regress."},{"q":"When would you not offer a vendor-hosted control plane?","a":"- **When policy forbids any egress.** Even metadata-only outbound calls are a review item, and some\n  customers reject them on principle, so the split must be optional.\n- **When the air gap is physical.** There is no path to a control plane, so licensing, configuration and\n  upgrades must all work locally from the bundle and values file.\n- **Cost of dropping it:** upgrades and configuration become per-site work, so support load grows with\n  customer count."},{"q":"A customer wants a 70B model delivered into an air-gapped site. What numbers do you plan around?","a":"- **Weights:** 70B × 2 bytes in 16-bit ≈ 140 GB; an 8-bit copy is ~70 GB, so precision choice changes the\n  media plan.\n- **Time:** at 1 Gbps the copy alone is ~19 minutes, before hashing, the customer's scanning and import\n  into their mirror, which often dominate.\n- **Quality and hardware:** confirm their GPUs fit the chosen precision and that your eval suite passed\n  on exactly that model and precision."},{"q":"An on-prem agent works in your smoke test but fails intermittently on real tasks at the customer. How do you investigate without access?","a":"- **Get the evidence out:** ask the customer to run the support-bundle command, which carries redacted\n  agent traces, tool error counts and versions, since you cannot log in.\n- **Look for the boundary:** a tool timing out through their proxy, a certificate error from a tool\n  adapter that skipped their CA bundle, or the open-weight model producing malformed tool arguments.\n- **Close the gap:** add the failing case to the bundled eval set and CI gate, then ship a fix bundle."}],"tradeoffs":["**Data custody vs observability.** The customer keeps custody of data and uses their own cloud discounts and controls, but you lose direct observability and fast iteration on prompts and tools. Flips toward hosted SaaS plus a strong DPA when the customer has no platform team to operate what you ship.","**Control-plane split vs zero egress.** The split keeps upgrades and configuration manageable across many customers, but any outbound connection is a review item. Flips to fully self-contained the moment the customer's policy forbids egress.","**On-prem vs elastic capacity.** On-prem removes cloud dependency and per-token fees, but GPU capacity is fixed on a procurement cycle measured in months, and multi-step agents consume many model calls per task. Flips when the workload is bursty or needs frontier-model quality.","**Air-gap isolation vs support speed.** The strongest isolation possible, but every bug fix is a physical delivery and bugs surface weeks later through log bundles. Accept it only where mandated, and budget support time accordingly.","**Open-weight portability vs quality.** Shipping open-weight models makes the agent deployable anywhere, but your quality bar must be re-established per shipped model, since the hosted model your SaaS uses is not an option inside."],"pitfalls":["**Runtime downloads.** Symptom: works in CI, then the pod crash-loops at the customer with DNS resolution or connection-timeout errors for a public hostname. Cause: hub weights, tokenizer files, `pip install` on start, NLTK or tiktoken data. Diagnose: run the install under the deny-egress policy and grep pod logs for public hostnames. Fix: bundle them and set the offline flags.","**A tool that still points outside.** Symptom: timeouts on one tool and odd answers, because a web-search or SaaS tool left in the registry fails mid-task and the agent retries or improvises. Diagnose: per-tool error counts in the traces. Fix: the start-up preflight above, plus removing the tool from the prompt.","**Hard-coded registry host, DNS domain or storage class in the chart.** Symptom: `ImagePullBackOff` or persistent volume claims stuck in `Pending` on a healthy cluster. Diagnose: `kubectl describe` the pod or claim and compare the image reference or class with their values file. Fix: make them values.","**Not trusting the customer's CA.** Symptom: every outbound call fails certificate verification, because their egress proxy re-signs TLS. Diagnose: `openssl s_client` through the proxy shows their issuer, not a public one. Fix: mount their CA bundle.","**Expiry or missing logs inside an air gap.** Symptom: the system stops on a licence or certificate date nobody can renew online, or incidents are debugged from screenshots in email. Diagnose: can a support bundle answer \"days to expiry\" and \"which tool failed and why\"? Fix: early warnings and a support-bundle command, with redacted agent traces, that the customer knows how to run.","**Version sprawl and one-way migrations.** Symptom: a support matrix nobody can keep in their head, since fixes are back-ported to every site's bundle, and a failed upgrade that cannot return to the previous chart version, turning a bad hour into a bad week. Diagnose: versions per site from support bundles and a rollback test in CI. Fix: a bounded version window and expand-then-contract migrations."],"when":"- **Customer VPC when** the blocker is data custody within a cloud the customer already runs; it keeps\n  managed model services (and a strong tool-calling model) and most of your operability.\n- **On-prem when** the customer has no cloud footprint, needs GPU proximity to their systems or has a\n  contractual prohibition on public cloud.\n- **Air-gapped only when** a regulator or classification policy mandates it, and price the support model in.\n- **Measure before committing:** install time per customer, bundle size, supported versions, mean time\n  to deliver a fix, and agent eval scores on the shipped model.\n- **Interviews:** list what breaks without a network and how delivery changes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Removes every runtime network dependency, moves environment decisions into chart values, builds the signed bundle and the support-bundle command, and keeps migrations reversible.","architect":"Decides which deployment tiers the product supports, where the control-plane/data-plane boundary sits, and which models and dependencies each tier is allowed to assume.","fde":"Runs the install on the customer's cluster, negotiates registry, CA, proxy and identity details with their platform and security teams, and operates the sneakernet for air-gapped sites."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants your RAG assistant inside an air-gapped facility. Today the product calls a\nhosted frontier model, sends traces to a SaaS observability tool, downloads its embedding model from a\nhub at start-up and validates a licence against your server. What has to change, in what order, and\nwhat do you tell the customer they are giving up?","a":"First replace the hosted model with an open-weight model served inside (vLLM or similar) and\nre-run the evaluation suite on it, because that decides whether the product is still good enough to\nship. Then remove the start-up download by baking or seeding the embedding weights and setting the\noffline flags; replace the licence call with a signed offline licence file with expiry and grace; and\nredirect traces to a collector targeting the customer's own backend. Package images, chart, weights\nand wheels into a signed bundle with a hash manifest and a verify step, and add a support-bundle\ncommand. Tell the customer they lose frontier-model quality, automatic updates and vendor\nobservability; fixes arrive as bundles on a schedule, and they must operate the stack."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do many vendors split a customer-VPC deployment into a vendor-hosted control plane and a customer-hosted data plane?","options":["Because cloud providers require the control plane to run in the vendor's account","Because the customer's requirement is custody of data, so keeping only non-data functions vendor-side preserves operability while meeting the requirement","Because it removes the need for the customer to grant the vendor any IAM access","Because Kubernetes cannot run both planes in the same cluster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The split keeps licensing, configuration and upgrades manageable across customers while every component that touches data stays in the customer's account. It is a design choice, not a platform rule, and it must be optional because some customers forbid any outbound connection."},{"q":"An on-prem install crash-loops; the pod log shows a DNS resolution failure for a public model-hub hostname. What is the root cause and fix?","options":["The customer's cluster lacks a GPU operator, so the model cannot load","The Helm chart has the wrong storage class configured","The application downloads model or tokenizer files at start-up; weights must be bundled and the libraries run in offline mode","The customer's CA certificate is not trusted by the container"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A DNS failure for a hub hostname means code is trying to fetch artifacts at runtime. The fix is in the build: ship the weights and tokenizer files in the bundle and set the offline environment flags. A CA problem would show as a certificate verification error, not a resolution failure."},{"q":"Which artifact lets an air-gapped customer confirm a bundle is complete, unmodified and genuinely from you without contacting you?","options":["A README listing the included files and their versions","A licence file with an expiry date","A manifest of SHA-256 hashes signed with the vendor's key, plus signed images","A telemetry ping sent once the installation completes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hashes prove integrity and a signature over the manifest proves origin, and both can be checked offline. A README is unverifiable, a licence file proves entitlement rather than integrity, and a telemetry ping is impossible without a network path."}]},{"key":"deployment/private-endpoints","sources":["Official docs: AWS PrivateLink","Official docs: Azure Private Link","Official docs: Google Cloud Private Service Connect"],"why":"- **The subnet has no internet route on purpose.** A bank's application subnet still needs a hosted\n  model API, object storage and a SaaS observability tool, and every one has a public hostname that\n  resolves to a public IP. An agent adds more: every tool backend is another hostname.\n- **A NAT gateway opens everything.** The same path that reaches the model API also reaches a paste\n  site, which is exactly what the data-loss team was preventing. For an agent that can be steered by\n  prompt injection into calling a URL, that open path is a real exfiltration route.\n- **IP allowlists are brittle.** Cloud services use large, shared and changing address pools, so the\n  allowlist ends up permitting a huge slice of the cloud.\n- **Peering is too broad.** It is usually not offered for multi-tenant services; where it is, it connects\n  entire networks, requires non-overlapping CIDR ranges and exposes far more than one API.\n- **A private endpoint says exactly what security wants:** this subnet may reach this one service over\n  a path we control. Once every consumer arrives privately, the provider can disable public network\n  access, so a leaked API key is useless from the internet.","how":"The three large clouds implement the same idea under different names:\n\n| Cloud | Name | Consumer object | Publishing your own SaaS |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AWS | PrivateLink | interface VPC endpoint | endpoint service fronted by a Network Load Balancer |\n| Azure | Private Link | private endpoint | Private Link service behind a Standard Load Balancer |\n| Google Cloud | Private Service Connect | endpoint | service attachment |\n\nThe mechanism has four parts.\n\n### 1. A network interface in the consumer's subnet\n- **What is created.** One or more network interfaces, each with a private IP from the consumer's\n  subnet; packets to that IP travel over the provider's internal network to the service.\n- **Overlapping CIDRs are fine.** The consumer only ever sees an IP in its own range, so address spaces\n  never have to be compatible. This is the main reason it beats peering for a SaaS whose thousands of\n  customers all use 10.0.0.0/8.\n- **One interface per availability zone**, because the interface lives in a zone; a single-zone endpoint\n  is a single point of failure and pushes cross-zone traffic. For an agent, a zone outage then stops\n  every model call mid-task.\n\n### 2. A service published on the provider side\n- **First-party services** (model APIs, storage, key management): the cloud publishes them for you.\n- **Your own SaaS:** you publish it yourself (see the table above). An agent platform or a hosted tool\n  server sold to enterprises is often offered this way.\n- **Decide who may connect** by allowlisting customer accounts or requiring manual acceptance of each\n  connection request, because otherwise anyone who learns the service name could attach to it.\n\n### 3. DNS that points the normal hostname at the private IP\n- **Why DNS matters.** Applications and SDKs use the service's ordinary hostname; if it still resolves to\n  a public IP, the endpoint is never used.\n- **The override.** The endpoint comes with a private DNS zone that overrides the public record inside\n  the consumer network, so the same hostname resolves to the interface's private IP.\n- **Hostname unchanged on purpose**, because TLS certificates still validate and no SDK needs\n  reconfiguring. The agent's model client keeps its default base URL.\n- **Callers outside the VPC** (on-prem over a VPN, or another network) need their DNS queries forwarded\n  into the cloud resolver, otherwise they resolve publicly.\n\n### 4. Policy on both ends\n- **Connectivity is not authorization.** Calls still need IAM credentials or API keys.\n- **Endpoint policy:** which principals and actions may use this endpoint. For an agent's model endpoint\n  this can limit the agent's role to invoking models, not managing them.\n- **Service-side rule:** a resource policy or network rule saying \"only accept requests arriving via\n  endpoint X\". The combination is what makes stolen credentials useless off-network.\n\n### Following one request\nPut the four parts together for one agent step calling a model API from a pod:\n1. **Resolve.** The SDK asks the VPC resolver for `bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com`; the private\n   zone answers with the endpoint interfaces' private IPs (say 10.20.1.15 and 10.20.2.15, one per zone).\n2. **Connect.** The pod opens TCP 443 to 10.20.1.15. The **security group on the endpoint interface**\n   must allow 443 from the pod's subnet, separately from the pod's own egress rules.\n3. **Carry.** The provider network takes the flow to the service's load balancer. The service never sees\n   a route into your VPC; it sees a connection tagged with the endpoint it came through, which is what\n   resource policies such as \"only via endpoint X\" match on.\n4. **Authenticate.** TLS is negotiated against the ordinary hostname, so the provider's public\n   certificate validates; then the API key or IAM signature is checked as usual.\n\n- **Customer identity for your own SaaS.** On AWS, enabling Proxy Protocol v2 on the NLB is how your\n  application learns which customer endpoint a connection came from, *because* the source IP alone is\n  not a reliable customer identifier.\n- **One-way by design.** Only the consumer initiates connections; the provider cannot open any back\n  through the endpoint, so security approves it more easily than a VPN or peering. A hosted agent\n  therefore cannot use it to call tools inside the customer's network.\n\n### Gateway endpoints (AWS only)\n- **Older, different mechanism** available only for S3 and DynamoDB: a route-table entry (a prefix list)\n  instead of an interface.\n- **Cost and reach.** Free, but only from inside that VPC, not from on-prem or peered networks.\n- **Elsewhere.** Azure and Google Cloud have no direct equivalent; their closest analogues are service\n  endpoints and Private Google Access respectively.","example":"### Deploy-time check that SDK hostnames resolve privately\n- **Setting.** A RAG agent in a no-NAT subnet, with interface endpoints for the model runtime API and\n  object storage, and its own vector service behind a private endpoint.\n- **Why check.** It is the earliest signal that private DNS is off, which otherwise shows up only as\n  timeouts (no egress) or silent internet traffic (if egress exists).\n\n```python\nimport ipaddress, socket, sys\n\n# Hostnames the application's SDKs actually call (region-specific in real deployments).\nREQUIRED_PRIVATE = [\n    \"bedrock-runtime.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com\",\n    \"vectors.internal.example.com\",   # our own SaaS exposed through a private endpoint\n]\n\ndef resolved_ips(host):\n    infos = socket.getaddrinfo(host, 443, proto=socket.IPPROTO_TCP)\n    return sorted({ipaddress.ip_address(info[4][0]) for info in infos}, key=str)  # v4 and v6 mix\n\nfailures = []\nfor host in REQUIRED_PRIVATE:\n    try:\n        ips = resolved_ips(host)\n    except socket.gaierror as e:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: does not resolve ({e})\"); continue\n    public = [str(ip) for ip in ips if not ip.is_private]\n    if public:\n        failures.append(f\"{host}: resolves to public {public}; private DNS not active here\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"ok   {host} -> {[str(ip) for ip in ips]}\")\n\nif failures:\n    print(\"\\n\".join(\"FAIL \" + f for f in failures)); sys.exit(1)\n```\n\n- **Run it inside the workload,** because resolution depends on the caller's resolver.\n- **Storage may use a gateway route** rather than a private IP; check the route table for those.\n- **Derive the list from the agent's config** (model region, every tool's base URL) into\n  `REQUIRED_PRIVATE`, so adding a tool cannot skip the check.\n\n### Agent tool adapter that reports which half broke\nWhen a tool call fails inside a private subnet, the agent should get a precise, non-retryable error\nrather than a generic timeout it will retry until the step budget runs out.\n\n```python\nimport ipaddress, socket\n\ndef diagnose(host, port=443, timeout=3.0):\n    \"\"\"Return a short error string an agent loop can log and surface, or None if reachable.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        ips = {ipaddress.ip_address(i[4][0]) for i in socket.getaddrinfo(host, port)}\n    except socket.gaierror:\n        return f\"{host}: DNS lookup failed (no record or no resolver path)\"\n    if any(not ip.is_private for ip in ips):\n        return f\"{host}: resolves publicly; private DNS zone missing for this VPC\"\n    ip = sorted(map(str, ips))[0]     # str sort: mixed IPv4/IPv6 objects cannot be compared\n    try:\n        socket.create_connection((ip, port), timeout=timeout).close()\n    except OSError:\n        return f\"{host}: private IP {ip} unreachable on {port}; check endpoint security group\"\n    return None\n\ndef call_tool(tool, host, do_request):\n    problem = diagnose(host)\n    if problem:                       # network faults are not fixed by retrying the model step\n        return {\"tool\": tool, \"error\": problem, \"retryable\": False}\n    return {\"tool\": tool, \"result\": do_request()}\n```\n\n### The endpoint in Terraform, with the security group that usually gets forgotten\nOne interface per zone, private DNS on, 443 admitted from the workload subnets (validated with\nTerraform 1.9.8, AWS provider 5.100).\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_vpc_endpoint\" \"bedrock_runtime\" {\n  vpc_id              = aws_vpc.main.id\n  service_name        = \"com.amazonaws.eu-west-1.bedrock-runtime\"\n  vpc_endpoint_type   = \"Interface\"\n  subnet_ids          = [aws_subnet.app_a.id, aws_subnet.app_b.id]   # one per AZ\n  security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.endpoint.id]\n  private_dns_enabled = true                                          # the DNS half\n}\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"endpoint\" {\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [aws_subnet.app_a.cidr_block, aws_subnet.app_b.cidr_block]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: three symptoms, three different causes\n| Symptom seen | What the lookup from the pod shows | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Flow logs show bytes via NAT to the API | public IP | private DNS off or zone not linked to this VPC | enable private DNS, link the zone |\n| Timeouts, hostname resolves to 10.20.1.15 | private IP | endpoint security group blocks 443 | admit 443 from the workload subnet |\n| Works in cluster, fails from data centre | public IP on-prem only | no DNS forwarding from on-prem | forward the domain to the cloud resolver |\n\n### Worked scenario: one agent task, many private paths\nAn illustrative support agent task in a no-NAT subnet; the counts show why one missing endpoint stalls\nthe whole task.\n\n| Dependency | Calls per task (illustrative) | Path | If it is missing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model API | 8 (one per step) | interface endpoint, two zones | step 1 times out, task fails |\n| Object storage (attachments) | 2 | gateway endpoint | file tool hangs |\n| Vector store (your SaaS) | 3 | your PrivateLink endpoint service | retrieval empty or timeout |\n| Token or identity service | 1 | interface endpoint | auth hangs while other calls succeed |\n\nWith the adapter above, each missing path is one named, non-retryable error, not 8 model retries.","patterns":["**Fail closed at the network layer.** Remove the NAT route from subnets that only need endpointed services, so a call that resolves publicly fails loudly instead of leaking. Monitor VPC flow logs for any bytes to the service via NAT; the target is zero. For agents this also caps where an injected instruction can send data.","**Restrict the service to the endpoint.** For S3, a bucket policy that denies requests unless the `aws:SourceVpce` condition key matches your endpoint id; for Azure services, set public network access to disabled. Monitor denied requests: a spike means a caller is still on the public path.","**Shared endpoint hub.** With dozens of services across many VPCs, host endpoints once in a central VPC and share the private zones, because per-endpoint, per-zone charges add up. Monitor endpoint count and cost per VPC.","**Hybrid DNS forwarding.** Give on-prem resolvers a conditional forward for the service domains to a cloud inbound resolver (Route 53 Resolver inbound endpoints on AWS, Azure DNS Private Resolver on Azure). Monitor the DNS check above run from an on-prem host.","**Manual acceptance for your SaaS endpoint service.** Set `acceptance_required = true` and an explicit principal allowlist on the Terraform `aws_vpc_endpoint_service`, so unknown accounts never attach. Review the connection list on a schedule.","**DNS check as a deploy gate.** Run the resolution script as a pre-start job in each environment and block rollout on failure; alert if it ever flips from private to public."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent in a regulated customer's subnet calls a model API on every\n  step, plus object storage, a vector store and tool APIs. With no internet route, each of those needs a\n  private path, or the loop times out on step one or silently leaks over NAT.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model SDK's ordinary hostname (for example\n  `bedrock-runtime.<region>.amazonaws.com`), the Terraform `aws_vpc_endpoint` resource, the endpoint's\n  security group, and the private DNS zone that makes the hostname resolve privately.\n- **What you control.** A deploy-time check that every hostname the agent and its tools call resolves to\n  a private IP, timeouts and error messages in tool adapters that distinguish DNS from connectivity, and\n  whether your own agent SaaS is published over PrivateLink.\n- **What you can skip.** Provider-network internals and load-balancer routing. Know the four parts\n  (interface, published service, DNS override, policy) and the one-way property.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents in a bank's no-egress subnet.** The agent reaches Bedrock, Azure OpenAI or Vertex AI and object\n  storage only through private endpoints, with no NAT gateway.\n- **Selling an agent platform to enterprises.** Your agent API or hosted tool server is published as an\n  endpoint service, consumed without a VPN or public exposure.\n- **Shrinking the blast radius of prompt injection.** With NAT removed, an injected attacker URL has no\n  route.\n- **Hybrid agents.** On-prem workers reach a cloud model API privately via DNS forwarding.","mini_project":"**Prove an agent's dependencies are private (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn an agent's model and tool configuration into a deploy gate that fails on any public or\n  unreachable dependency.\n- **Steps:** write an agent config with the model hostname and three tool base URLs; generate\n  `REQUIRED_PRIVATE` from it; run the check and the `diagnose` adapter from a container; wrap one tool in\n  `call_tool` in a minimal agent loop; point a hosts-file entry at a public IP, then at an unreachable\n  private IP.\n- **Done when:** the gate exits non-zero for both broken cases with the right named cause, passes for the\n  clean config, and the loop logs one non-retryable error instead of repeated timeouts."},"exercise":"**Goal:** turn the Terraform example into a reusable endpoint module whose guarantees are tested without\nan AWS account.\n- **Inputs:** a list of interface services, workload subnet ids with their CIDRs (one per zone) and one\n  S3 bucket.\n- **Deliver:** a module that creates one interface endpoint per service with private DNS on, a shared\n  security group admitting 443 only from the workload subnets, an S3 gateway endpoint, and a bucket\n  policy denying requests whose `aws:SourceVpce` is not that endpoint.\n- **Acceptance:** `terraform validate` passes; a `terraform test` file using `mock_provider \"aws\"`\n  (Terraform 1.7 or later) asserts every endpoint has `private_dns_enabled = true` and spans every\n  subnet, and the bucket policy names the gateway endpoint (run it with `command = apply`, since ids\n  are unknown at plan).","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an agent in a customer's Azure Kubernetes cluster calls Azure OpenAI through a private\nendpoint. Security then disables public network access. Next morning the cluster works, but the nightly\neval job on hosted CI runners and a desktop tool used by analysts on-prem (linked by site-to-site VPN)\nboth get 403s.\n\nExplain both failures and fix them without re-enabling public access.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- sees the CI failure as the intended effect: hosted runners now arrive on the public path, so the evals\n  move to a self-hosted runner inside the network or reach it over VPN;\n- traces on-prem to DNS: the private zone answers only inside, so on-prem resolvers need a conditional\n  forward to an inbound resolver (Azure DNS Private Resolver), and the VPN must route the endpoint's IP;\n- verifies with a lookup from each caller, and proves a valid key from outside is now denied.","diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant P as Agent pod in private subnet\n    participant R as VPC resolver\n    participant E as Endpoint interface\n    participant L as Provider load balancer\n    participant S as Model API\n    P->>R: resolve the ordinary API hostname\n    R-->>P: private IPs, one per zone\n    P->>E: TCP 443 to private IP\n    Note over E: security group must admit 443\n    E->>L: carried on provider network, tagged with endpoint id\n    L->>S: forward request\n    Note over S: TLS on normal hostname, then IAM or API key, then only-via-endpoint policy\n    S-->>P: response on the same path","caption":"One agent step calling a model API: the private DNS answer and the endpoint's security group are the\ntwo steps that most often break."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a private endpoint, and why is it preferred over a NAT gateway or IP allowlist?","a":"- **Definition:** a network interface with a private IP in the consumer's subnet, plus a private DNS\n  override, that carries traffic to one managed service over the provider's network.\n- **Versus NAT:** NAT opens the whole internet to the subnet; the endpoint reaches exactly one service.\n- **Versus allowlists:** provider IP pools are shared and change, so allowlists end up admitting a large\n  slice of the cloud."},{"q":"DNS resolves to a private IP but every call times out. Walk me through the debugging.","a":"- **Confirm the path:** from inside the pod, check the resolved IP is the endpoint interface and try a\n  TCP connect to 443.\n- **Check the endpoint's security group first:** it must admit 443 from the workload subnet, and it is\n  separate from the pod's egress rules.\n- **Then check** pod egress policy, network ACLs on the subnet, and that the endpoint has an interface in\n  the zone the pod is calling."},{"q":"Your agent works in staging but in the customer's no-NAT subnet every task fails on its first step. How do you approach it?","a":"- **List what step one calls:** the model API, then usually retrieval and an identity token; each needs an\n  endpoint or gateway route.\n- **Run the resolution check from the agent pod:** a public answer means private DNS is missing; a private\n  answer with timeouts points at the endpoint security group.\n- **Stop the retry spiral:** adapters return a named network error, so traces show the real cause."},{"q":"When would you choose a VPN or peering instead of private endpoints?","a":"- **Many services or both directions:** endpoints carry traffic only to one published service, so broad\n  or bidirectional flows are simpler over peering or a transit network.\n- **Non-cloud sources needing routes into the VPC:** a VPN joins networks; endpoints deliberately do not.\n- **Cost:** endpoints bill per endpoint, per zone and per GB, so dozens of services across many VPCs\n  favour a shared hub or a different topology."},{"q":"How do you make a stolen API key useless from outside the customer network?","a":"- **The endpoint alone does not:** it adds a private path but leaves the public one open.\n- **Restrict at the service:** disable public network access, or use a resource policy that only accepts\n  requests via this endpoint (for S3, the `aws:SourceVpce` condition).\n- **Prove it:** call from outside with a valid key and expect a denial; alert on denied-request spikes."},{"q":"You are offering your agent SaaS over PrivateLink to 300 enterprise customers. What do you design for?","a":"- **Publishing:** an endpoint service behind a Network Load Balancer, with manual acceptance or an\n  account allowlist so only contracted customers attach.\n- **Tenant identity:** Proxy Protocol v2 tells your app which endpoint a connection came from, because\n  source IPs overlap across customers using the same private ranges.\n- **Operations:** availability in every zone customers use, and a runbook for their DNS and security-group\n  setup, since most onboarding failures are on their side."}],"tradeoffs":["**Narrow path vs cost.** You gain a narrow, one-directional path to exactly one service, but pay per-endpoint, per-zone hourly and per-GB charges. With dozens of services across many VPCs, a central shared-endpoint VPC becomes cheaper.","**No public access vs convenience.** Disabling public access blocks leaked credentials from the internet, but also blocks your own engineers' laptops and CI runners (including agent eval jobs) unless they reach it over VPN or a private runner.","**No CIDR coordination vs flexibility.** Endpoints avoid the CIDR coordination peering or VPNs need, but only carry traffic to the published service. If workloads need many services or bidirectional flows, such as a hosted agent calling tools inside the customer, peering or a transit network is simpler.","**Path assurance vs data assurance.** Private networking reassures a security team about the path, but does not change who processes the data or where. If the concern is residency or training use, you need contractual and regional controls instead."],"pitfalls":["**Endpoint without private DNS.** Symptom: the SDK times out in a no-egress subnet, or flow logs show traffic still leaving through the NAT gateway. Cause: the private DNS zone is not enabled or not linked to this VPC. Diagnose: resolve the hostname from inside the pod; a public IP confirms it. Fix: enable private DNS and link the zone.","**Forgetting hybrid callers.** Symptom: \"works from the cluster, fails from the data center\", with the hostname resolving to a public IP there. Cause: on-prem and peered-VPC callers need DNS forwarding to the cloud resolver. Diagnose: run the DNS check from an on-prem host. Fix: a conditional forward.","**Default security group on the interface.** Symptom: the hostname resolves to a private IP but every connection times out, which teams misdiagnose as a DNS or provider outage. Cause: it does not admit 443 from the workload subnet. Diagnose: a TCP connect to that IP on 443 from the pod, as `diagnose` does. Fix: admit 443 from the workload subnets.","**Endpointing the API but not its dependencies.** Symptom: authentication or uploads hang while plain calls succeed. Cause: the identity provider, token service or a separate file-upload host has no private path. Diagnose: list every hostname in one task's trace and check each.","**Letting the agent retry network faults.** Symptom: the task burns its step budget re-trying one tool. Diagnose: count identical consecutive tool calls per trace. Fix: classify network errors in the adapter and return them as non-retryable.","**Single-zone interface or auto-accept for any principal.** Symptom: model calls fail during a zone incident plus cross-zone data charges normally; or unknown accounts in your endpoint service's connection list, which an audit will flag. Diagnose: count endpoint subnets per zone and review connections. Fix: one interface per zone, manual acceptance."],"when":"- **Use when** a customer's workload in a cloud network must call a managed service and policy says \"no\n  internet egress\" or \"no public endpoints for data services\". That covers most regulated buyers calling\n  hosted model APIs, storage and key management in their own account.\n- **Offer your SaaS this way**: it is the standard way to serve enterprise customers without a VPN.\n- **Prefer it over VPN or peering** when the flow is one-directional to a single service.\n- **Do not use it** as a substitute for authorization, encryption or data-processing terms.\n- **Measure:** flow logs (zero bytes to the service via NAT) and the service rejecting non-endpoint traffic.\n- **Interviews:** explain the DNS override and the one-way property; that separates experience from slideware.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the endpoint, security group and private DNS resources in Terraform, and adds a deploy check that every SDK hostname resolves to a private address from inside the workload.","architect":"Decides where endpoints live (per VPC or a shared hub), which services must disable public access, and how on-prem DNS forwarding is designed so hybrid callers stay private.","fde":"Walks the customer's network team through publishing or consuming the endpoint, requests account allowlisting, and debugs the inevitable DNS resolution difference between clusters and laptops."},"check":{"q":"A customer created a private endpoint for a hosted LLM API, yet their security team's flow logs\nstill show traffic from the RAG service going out through the NAT gateway. What is the likely cause, and\nhow do you make the setup fail closed?","a":"The endpoint exists but the application's hostname still resolves to the public IP, usually\nbecause private DNS was not enabled on the endpoint, the private zone is not associated with that VPC,\nor the workload uses a custom resolver that does not forward to the cloud resolver. Fix the resolution,\nverify with a lookup from inside the pod, then make it fail closed: remove the NAT route from that\nsubnet so a call that resolves publicly fails loudly, set the service's resource policy to accept only requests via\nthis endpoint, and turn off public network access where the service supports it. Finally add the DNS\ncheck to deployment so a regression is caught before traffic flows."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a SaaS vendor offer private endpoints to hundreds of customers who all use overlapping 10.0.0.0/16 ranges?","options":["The vendor runs a separate VPC per customer with a unique CIDR","Each customer reaches the service through an interface with an IP from their own subnet, so the two address spaces are never joined","Private endpoints use IPv6 internally, so IPv4 overlap is irrelevant","The cloud's NAT gateway rewrites addresses in both directions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The consumer only routes to a private IP in its own range and the provider network carries the traffic behind a load balancer, so no routes are exchanged. Peering and VPNs join address spaces and therefore require non-overlapping ranges."},{"q":"An endpoint for a model API is created and healthy, but calls from on-prem servers over the VPN still go to the public internet. What is missing?","options":["A second endpoint in another availability zone","An endpoint policy allowing the on-prem IP range","DNS forwarding so on-prem resolvers send the service's hostname to the cloud resolver that holds the private zone","A BGP route advertising the service's public IP range over the VPN"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The private zone only answers inside the cloud network. On-prem resolvers still return the public record unless queries for that domain are forwarded inward; advertising public ranges over the VPN would be a fragile hack, not the design."},{"q":"A security reviewer says \"we have a private endpoint, so stolen API keys are harmless.\" What makes that statement true or false?","options":["It is true by default, because private endpoints encrypt the API key","It is false, because private endpoints only work for storage services","It is true, because the endpoint policy replaces the need for credentials","It is only true if the service also rejects requests that did not arrive through the endpoint, for example by disabling public access"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The endpoint adds a private path but does not remove the public one. Only a resource-side restriction, such as disabling public access or a policy requiring this endpoint, makes the key useless from outside."}]},{"key":"deployment/vpn","sources":["RFC 7296 — Internet Key Exchange Protocol Version 2 (IKEv2)","RFC 4303 — IP Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP)","Official docs: WireGuard"],"why":"- **The data has no public IP.** Your RAG product runs in the cloud; the customer's contracts and ticket\n  history live in an on-prem document store and database that will never have a public IP, yet ingestion\n  workers and agent tools must reach them.\n- **Exposing them publicly is rejected.** A database or file share on the internet behind a firewall rule\n  fails the first security meeting, rightly: credentials become the only barrier.\n- **Disks or nightly SFTP exports go stale.** They work once, but give you stale data, a manual process\n  and no way to run live queries or tool calls.\n- **Dedicated circuits are slow to get.** AWS Direct Connect, Azure ExpressRoute or Google Cloud\n  Interconnect are right for heavy, permanent traffic, but take weeks to months through a carrier and\n  cost accordingly.\n- **A VPN is the middle path.** It reuses both sides' internet connections, adds encryption and mutual\n  authentication, and can be up in days, which matters during a pilot. The flip side: once networks are\n  joined, anything routable can talk unless you narrow it, so it is a network integration, not just\n  encryption.","how":"### Site-to-site IPsec\nMost enterprise site-to-site VPNs, including the managed VPN gateways of the major clouds, use IPsec\nnegotiated by IKEv2. Two phases:\n\n1. **IKE SA (authentication and key agreement).**\n   - The gateways exchange Diffie-Hellman values to derive shared keys, then authenticate each other with\n     a pre-shared key or certificates.\n   - Certificates are preferable at scale because a leaked PSK compromises every tunnel that shares it.\n   - The result is an encrypted control channel, usually over UDP 500, moving to UDP 4500 when NAT is\n     detected (NAT traversal), which is why firewalls on both sides must open those ports.\n   - Without NAT, data packets travel as raw ESP (IP protocol 50, not TCP or UDP), which firewalls must\n     permit as well.\n2. **Child SAs (the data plane).** Inside that channel the peers agree keys and traffic selectors for ESP,\n   the protocol that encrypts and authenticates each packet. Keys are rekeyed on a timer so a single\n   compromised key exposes only a bounded window.\n\n| Port or protocol | Used for | Firewall must allow |\n|---|---|---|\n| UDP 500 | IKE negotiation | both directions between peer IPs |\n| UDP 4500 | IKE and ESP when NAT is detected | both directions |\n| IP protocol 50 (ESP) | data packets without NAT | both directions |\n\n### Routing: policy-based vs route-based\n| | Policy-based | Route-based, static | Route-based, BGP |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| What decides encryption | configured source/destination prefixes | tunnel is a virtual interface; routes point at it | same, routes learned dynamically |\n| Adding a subnet | edit both ends | add a route | the other side advertises it |\n| Failover | manual or vendor-specific | needs tunnel monitoring | automatic: routes withdrawn when a tunnel fails |\n\n- **BGP is preferred for production** because each side advertises its prefixes and withdraws them when\n  a tunnel fails, so failover between redundant tunnels is automatic.\n- **Configure both tunnels.** Cloud VPN gateways typically give you two tunnels per connection,\n  terminating on different endpoints, precisely so the provider can maintain one without an outage.\n- **What failover means for an agent.** In-flight TCP connections may reset while routes move, so a tool\n  call in progress fails; the adapter must retry safe reads once, and never blindly retry writes.\n\n### What the packet pays\n- **Overhead.** Each packet gains ESP headers, padding and an authentication tag plus an outer IP header,\n  tens of bytes in total.\n- **Full-size packets no longer fit.** A 1,500-byte packet is fragmented or, if the \"don't fragment\" bit\n  is set and ICMP is blocked, silently dropped.\n- **MSS clamping.** Gateways clamp the TCP maximum segment size (MSS) in the SYN handshake so endpoints\n  send smaller segments. Cloud VPN docs typically recommend a clamp somewhere in the 1,350–1,400-byte\n  range, but use your provider's figure.\n- **Why at the gateway.** You cannot rely on every host's path-MTU discovery when firewalls drop the ICMP\n  messages it depends on. Clamping fixes TCP only; UDP traffic must be sized by the application.\n- **Throughput ceiling.** Per tunnel it is bounded by the gateway's crypto capacity and the internet path,\n  typically gigabit-class rather than the tens of gigabits a dedicated circuit offers (check your\n  provider's documented limits).\n\n### Remote-access VPN and WireGuard\n- **Client VPNs** connect a single device, for example an FDE's laptop, into a customer network.\n- **WireGuard** is a newer, deliberately small protocol: fixed modern cryptography, peers identified by\n  public keys, and a configuration of a few lines.\n- **Where you meet it.** It is easy to audit and fast, but many enterprises standardize on IPsec\n  appliances, so you meet it more in your own infrastructure than in a bank's.\n\n### When address spaces overlap\nRouting only works if each side can tell local addresses from remote ones, so overlapping CIDRs are the\nfirst thing to check, not the last. Three ways out, in rough order of preference:\n1. **Terminate in a fresh network** whose range neither side uses; cheap when you control one end, as a\n   vendor usually does.\n2. **NAT on the gateway**, so the customer sees your traffic from an agreed non-overlapping range and you\n   reach their hosts through translated addresses. It works, but every log and firewall rule now speaks\n   two address vocabularies.\n3. **Re-address one side**, which is correct and almost never quick inside an enterprise.\n\n### Narrowing the blast radius\nBecause a tunnel joins networks, scope it. This matters more for agents, because a model choosing tool\narguments should never be able to reach a host nobody intended:\n- **Advertise only the specific subnets needed.**\n- **Filter on both ends:** firewall rules or security groups allowing only the required ports to the\n  required hosts.\n- **Terminate in a dedicated integration VPC**, not your core production network.","example":"### Worked scenario: a legal-tech agent pilot over site-to-site VPN\nA vendor pilots a contract-search agent for a firm whose 2 TB document archive sits on an on-prem file\nserver, with case metadata in a SQL Server instance. Plan: a site-to-site VPN from the firm's data\ncenter to a dedicated \"customer-integration\" VPC in the vendor's cloud account.\n\nIngestion builds the RAG index over the archive; a `lookup_case` tool queries the database live.\n\n1. **Addressing.** The firm uses 10.0.0.0/8 broadly and the vendor's VPC is 10.20.0.0/16, which overlaps.\n   Rather than re-IP anything, the vendor confirms the firm uses nothing in 172.16.0.0/12 and creates the\n   integration VPC as 172.31.240.0/22.\n2. **Scope.** The firm advertises only two /32 host routes (the file server and the database) over BGP.\n   Firewall rules on its side allow only SMB and the SQL Server port, from the ingestion workers' subnet,\n   to those two hosts.\n3. **Capacity.** The uplink and tunnel yield an effective 400 Mbit/s for bulk transfer (an assumption to\n   measure, not a promise). See the table below.\n4. **The classic failure.** Small metadata queries work, but large file reads stall after a few\n   kilobytes. Packet captures show oversized packets dropped with ICMP blocked; enabling MSS clamping on\n   the gateway fixes it. In the agent, this looked like `lookup_case` succeeding and the document tool\n   timing out.\n5. **Redundancy.** Both tunnels are configured, and the vendor tests failover by disabling one during a\n   sync and confirming BGP shifts traffic without dropping the job.\n\n| Transfer | Size | Arithmetic | Time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Initial sync | 2 TB | 2 TB × 8 = 16 Tbit; at 0.4 Gbit/s that is 40,000 seconds | about 11 hours, scheduled over a weekend with rate limiting to protect business traffic |\n| Daily delta | around 5 GB | 40 Gbit ÷ 0.4 Gbit/s | about 100 s, under two minutes |\n\n### Capacity and MSS calculator\nPlan the sync window and the clamp value before the change request goes in.\n\n```python\ndef bulk_sync_hours(data_tb, effective_mbit_s):\n    bits = data_tb * 1e12 * 8\n    return bits / (effective_mbit_s * 1e6) / 3600\n\ndef mss_for(tunnel_mtu, ip_header=20, tcp_header=20):\n    return tunnel_mtu - ip_header - tcp_header\n\nfor tb, mbit in ((2, 400), (2, 100), (0.005, 400)):\n    h = bulk_sync_hours(tb, mbit)\n    print(f\"{tb} TB at {mbit} Mbit/s: {h:.1f} h ({h*3600:.0f} s)\")\nfor mtu in (1500, 1400):\n    print(f\"tunnel MTU {mtu}: clamp MSS to {mss_for(mtu)}\")\n# 2 TB at 400 Mbit/s: 11.1 h (40000 s)\n# 2 TB at 100 Mbit/s: 44.4 h (160000 s)\n# 0.005 TB at 400 Mbit/s: 0.0 h (100 s)\n# tunnel MTU 1500: clamp MSS to 1460\n# tunnel MTU 1400: clamp MSS to 1360\n```\n\n- **MSS is the MTU minus 40 bytes** of IPv4 and TCP headers (without options), so a 1,400-byte tunnel\n  MTU means clamping MSS to 1,360.\n\n### An on-prem agent tool that survives tunnel failover\nThe agent's `lookup_case` tool calls an internal API over the tunnel. It uses the internal hostname, a\nhard deadline, one retry for a read on connection errors (a failover reset), and a clear message to the\nmodel when the backend is unreachable.\n\n```python\nimport time, requests\n\nBASE = \"https://cases.firm.internal\"       # resolved by the customer's DNS over the tunnel\nTIMEOUT = (3, 10)                          # connect, read seconds; tune from measured RTT\n\ndef lookup_case(case_id: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Read-only tool exposed to the agent. Safe to retry because it has no side effects.\"\"\"\n    for attempt in (1, 2):\n        try:\n            r = requests.get(f\"{BASE}/cases/{case_id}\", timeout=TIMEOUT)\n            r.raise_for_status()\n            return {\"ok\": True, \"case\": r.json()}\n        except (requests.ConnectionError, requests.Timeout) as e:\n            if attempt == 2:\n                return {\"ok\": False, \"retryable\": False,\n                        \"error\": \"case system unreachable over the customer link; \"\n                                 \"tell the user and do not call this tool again this turn\",\n                        \"detail\": type(e).__name__}\n            time.sleep(2)                  # short pause; covers a reset, not a full failover\n        except requests.HTTPError as e:\n            return {\"ok\": False, \"retryable\": False, \"error\": f\"HTTP {e.response.status_code}\"}\n```\n\n- **Why the message is written for the model.** Without it, the agent often re-issues the call every step\n  until its budget is gone.\n- **A full failover can outlast the retry.** Dead-peer detection and BGP hold timers often take tens of\n  seconds to move traffic, so the tool returns the clear error rather than blocking the agent step;\n  measure your gateway's failover time and size the retry from it.\n- **Writes are different.** A tool that updates the case system needs an idempotency key before it may\n  retry, because a reset can arrive after the server committed the write.\n\n### Remote access with WireGuard\nA minimal client config for an engineer's laptop that routes only the integration range through the\ntunnel. Keys are placeholders; each side holds its own private key.\n\n```ini\n[Interface]\nPrivateKey = <laptop private key>\nAddress = 172.31.250.10/32\n\n[Peer]\nPublicKey = <gateway public key>\nEndpoint = vpn.example.com:51820\nAllowedIPs = 172.31.240.0/22      # only the integration VPC, not 0.0.0.0/0\nPersistentKeepalive = 25\n```","patterns":["**Two tunnels, BGP, tested failover.** Configure both provider tunnels with BGP and, before go-live, disable one during a sync and during a scripted agent run to confirm traffic shifts. Monitor tunnel state and BGP session flaps (on AWS Site-to-Site VPN, the CloudWatch `TunnelState` metric per tunnel).","**Clamp MSS on self-managed gateways.** On a Linux gateway, the iptables `TCPMSS` target rewrites MSS in SYN packets (rule below). Verify with `ping -M do -s <size> <host>` and watch for retransmissions of large segments.\n  ```\n  iptables -t mangle -A FORWARD -p tcp --tcp-flags SYN,RST SYN -j TCPMSS --clamp-mss-to-pmtu ```","**Host routes and port filters only.** Ask the customer to advertise /32 routes for the hosts you need and allow only those ports from your worker subnet. Keep that list equal to the agent's tool backends. Review the advertised prefix list in every change.","**Integration VPC per customer.** Terminate each tunnel in its own small VPC with a range avoiding theirs, running that customer's agent workers there. Keep an address-allocation register.","**Rate-limited bulk sync.** Schedule large initial syncs off-hours with a bandwidth cap, then switch to incremental deltas, so ingestion does not starve live agent tool calls. Monitor throughput against plan and the customer's uplink utilisation.","**DNS across the tunnel.** Forward the customer's internal domain to their resolvers over the tunnel so workers and tools use hostnames, not IPs. Alert when a configured hostname stops resolving."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A cloud-hosted agent whose tools query a customer's on-prem database,\n  file share or legacy API makes those calls over the tunnel, live, inside the agent loop. Tunnel\n  failover, MTU stalls and slow links become tool timeouts the model sees.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool base URLs on internal DNS names, per-tool timeouts and\n  retries, RAG ingestion bulk syncs, and the integration VPC your workers run in.\n- **What you control.** Which on-prem hosts and ports the agent's tools may reach (scope the routes to\n  those), timeouts that survive a BGP failover, resumable ingestion, and error messages that tell the\n  model a tool is unavailable rather than inviting a retry loop.\n- **What you can skip.** IKE cryptographic details and BGP path selection. Know the ports, the two\n  tunnels, the MSS clamp and how overlap is solved; network teams do the rest.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agents with on-prem tools.** A cloud-hosted support agent queries a customer's ticket database and\n  legacy order API over a scoped site-to-site tunnel during each conversation.\n- **RAG ingestion from private file shares.** Ingestion workers sync a document archive over the tunnel\n  into the vector index, with a rate-limited initial sync and daily deltas.\n- **Agent pilots before a dedicated circuit.** A VPN is up in days, so a pilot proves value before\n  anyone orders Direct Connect or ExpressRoute.\n- **FDE access for debugging.** An engineer reaches the integration VPC over WireGuard or a client VPN\n  scoped to that range only.","mini_project":"**Make an agent tool resilient to a flaky link (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show that an agent with a remote read-only tool ends a turn cleanly when the backend becomes\n  unreachable, instead of looping.\n- **Steps:** run a tiny \"on-prem\" HTTP backend in a container; wrap it as `lookup_case` in a\n  tool-calling loop with a 10-step budget; add `tc netem` delay or stop the container mid-run; log each\n  tool call and the model's next action.\n- **Done when:** a brief outage is absorbed by the single retry, a full outage produces one structured\n  error and a user-facing answer within two steps, and your log table shows no repeated calls to the dead\n  tool."},"exercise":"**Goal:** reproduce an MTU stall and the agent-side failure handling on one laptop, without a real\ntunnel.\n- **Inputs:** client, router and server Linux containers on two Docker networks, the router's\n  client-side link at MTU 1400 and its outbound ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" dropped (endpoints keep\n  1500, so the server's segments are too big); an HTTP service returning 200 bytes or 200 KB.\n- **Deliver:** a script that finds the largest passing don't-fragment ping, a `TCPMSS` clamp rule that\n  fixes the large response, and a `lookup_case`-style tool returning one structured error when the\n  service is stopped.\n- **Acceptance:** before the clamp the 200 KB request times out and the 200-byte one passes; after it,\n  both pass; with the service stopped the tool returns `retryable: False` after exactly two attempts.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a cloud support agent calls a customer's on-prem order API over a route-based VPN. Every\nnight around 02:00 a few conversations fail with the agent saying \"I keep trying but the order system\ndoes not respond\", after 8-10 identical tool calls. Daytime is clean. Diagnose it and fix both the\nnetwork and the agent.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- correlates failure times with per-tunnel state: the provider's maintenance moves one tunnel, and either\n  the second tunnel is unconfigured or static routes do not fail over;\n- fixes the network with both tunnels on BGP, then tests failover during a scripted agent run;\n- fixes the agent: a deadline sized from measured failover time, one retry for this read, then a\n  non-retryable error the model is told not to repeat;\n- verifies by counting identical consecutive tool calls per trace before and after.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    W[\"Agent and ingestion workers\"] --> IV[\"Integration VPC, non-overlapping range\"]\n    IV --> G[\"Cloud VPN gateway, MSS clamp\"]\n    G -->|\"tunnel 1, BGP\"| CG[\"Customer VPN gateway\"]\n    G -.->|\"tunnel 2, standby\"| CG\n    CG --> FW{\"Firewall: port and host allowed?\"}\n    FW -->|\"SMB\"| FS[(\"File server, /32 route\")]\n    FW -->|\"SQL port\"| DB[(\"Database, /32 route\")]\n    FW -->|\"anything else\"| X[\"Dropped\"]","caption":"Traffic from cloud agent workers to two on-prem tool backends: scoped routes, a clamped MSS and two BGP\ntunnels are the three design points."},"interview":[{"q":"What does a site-to-site IPsec VPN actually set up, and why does it need certain firewall ports?","a":"- **Two phases:** an IKE SA authenticates the gateways and agrees keys, then child SAs carry data\n  encrypted with ESP and rekeyed on a timer.\n- **Ports follow from that:** UDP 500 for IKE, UDP 4500 when NAT traversal is detected, and IP protocol\n  50 for raw ESP without NAT, so both firewalls must allow all three between the peers."},{"q":"The tunnel is up but no traffic from your VPC reaches the customer's hosts. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check addressing first:** if ranges overlap, each side routes the other's addresses locally and\n  packets never enter the tunnel.\n- **Then routes:** confirm the needed prefixes are advertised over BGP (or statically configured) on both\n  ends and that your subnet route tables point at the gateway.\n- **Then filters:** security groups and the customer's firewall must allow your worker subnet to the\n  specific host and port; a capture on each gateway shows where packets stop."},{"q":"Your cloud agent's tools call a customer's on-prem systems over a VPN. How do you design the tools so the agent behaves well when the link misbehaves?","a":"- **Deadlines per tool:** connect and read timeouts sized from measured round trips, so one hung call\n  cannot stall a step.\n- **Retry only what is safe:** one retry for reads after a connection reset (a failover), and idempotency\n  keys before any write is retried.\n- **Talk to the model:** return a structured, non-retryable error that says the system is unreachable, so\n  the agent tells the user instead of looping on the same call."},{"q":"When would you not use a VPN for a customer integration?","a":"- **One-way calls to one cloud service:** a private endpoint is narrower and does not join address spaces.\n- **Large, permanent traffic or latency guarantees:** a dedicated circuit gives predictable bandwidth,\n  often with IPsec or MACsec on top for regulated customers.\n- **People needing a few internal tools:** per-application zero-trust access beats a full-network client VPN."},{"q":"How long will a 2 TB initial sync take over the tunnel, and how do you plan it?","a":"- **Arithmetic:** 2 TB × 8 = 16 Tbit; at an effective 400 Mbit/s that is 40,000 s, about 11 hours.\n- **Plan:** measure the rate, sync over a weekend with a bandwidth cap, then daily deltas; make it\n  resumable so a failover mid-sync does not restart the job."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quick and cheap vs predictable.** A VPN stands up quickly over existing internet links, but throughput and latency vary with the internet path, which shows up as variable tool latency in each agent step. Once traffic is large and permanent, a dedicated circuit pays for itself.","**Any protocol vs attack surface.** Joining networks enables any protocol in either direction, which helps legacy integrations, but widens the attack surface compared with a one-way private endpoint to a single service.","**Dedicated circuit vs encryption.** A circuit gives predictable bandwidth and latency, but is private rather than encrypted by default, so regulated customers often still run IPsec (or MACsec where offered) over it. The choice flips back to VPN alone when volumes are modest and lead time matters.","**Static routes vs BGP.** Static routes suit one or two subnets but carry no liveness signal, so failover needs vendor-specific tunnel monitoring (dead-peer detection, route tracking) or manual edits; BGP costs configuration skill and buys automatic failover.","**Client VPN vs zero-trust access.** Remote-access VPN gives engineers broad network access easily, but identity-aware zero-trust access per application is safer when many people need narrow access to a few internal tools."],"pitfalls":["**Overlapping CIDR ranges discovered after the tunnel is up.** Symptom: a healthy tunnel status with traffic that never arrives. Cause: each side routes the other's addresses locally. Diagnose: compare both route tables for the remote prefix; a capture on your gateway shows nothing entering the tunnel. Fix: a fresh integration range or NAT.","**No MSS clamping when ICMP is filtered.** Symptom: small requests succeed while large file reads or long responses hang mid-transfer; in an agent, small tools work and document tools time out. Diagnose: `ping -M do -s <size>` at falling sizes, or a capture showing large segments retransmitted. Fix: clamp MSS at the gateway.","**Configuring only one of the two provided tunnels.** Symptom: an outage in the provider's routine maintenance window, often at night. Diagnose: per-tunnel state metrics show one always down. Fix: configure both with BGP and test failover.","**Advertising whole corporate supernets \"to keep it simple\".** Symptom: the security review finds the vendor network can reach thousands of customer hosts, any of which a steered agent tool could target. Diagnose: list the received BGP prefixes against the tools' backends. Fix: /32 routes and port filters.","**Leaving DNS out of the design.** Symptom: hard-coded IPs that break when the customer re-IPs a server. Fix: forward the internal domain over the tunnel.","**Tool timeouts shorter than failover or longer than the agent's patience.** Symptom: every failover fails the task, or one hung call blocks a step for minutes. Diagnose: log per-call latency and error type next to tunnel state changes. Fix: a deadline per tool, one retry for idempotent reads."],"when":"- **Use a site-to-site VPN when** cloud workloads need private, bidirectional or multi-protocol access to\n  a customer's on-prem systems: databases, file shares, legacy APIs, internal identity providers. It is\n  the default for pilots and moderate steady-state traffic, including agents whose tools live on-prem.\n- **Prefer a private endpoint** when the flow is one-way to a single cloud service, and a dedicated\n  circuit when sustained volume or latency guarantees matter.\n- **For people rather than systems**, prefer per-application zero-trust access over a full-network\n  client VPN.\n- **Measure:** tunnel uptime, BGP state changes, throughput against the plan, dropped or fragmented\n  packets, and tool-call error rate per on-prem backend.\n- **Interviews and design reviews:** mentioning address planning, scope and MTU unprompted signals that\n  you have actually operated one.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures ingestion workers to use internal DNS names over the tunnel, sets timeouts and retries that survive tunnel failover, and recognises an MTU stall from its distinctive symptom.","architect":"Chooses VPN versus private endpoint versus dedicated circuit, plans non-overlapping address space, and decides where the tunnel terminates so its blast radius stays small.","fde":"Fills in the customer's VPN request form with exact peer IPs, prefixes and ports, schedules the bulk sync around their business hours, and joins the call when the tunnel will not come up."},"check":{"q":"Your ingestion job reads from a customer's on-prem database over a new site-to-site VPN. Short\nqueries return instantly, but any query returning more than a few kilobytes hangs until timeout. The\ntunnel shows \"up\". What is happening and how do you confirm and fix it?","a":"That size-dependent pattern points to MTU. IPsec encapsulation adds tens of bytes, so full-size\npackets exceed the tunnel MTU; if \"don't fragment\" is set and ICMP \"fragmentation needed\" messages are\nblocked by a firewall, those packets are silently dropped while small ones pass. Confirm by sending pings\nwith the don't-fragment flag (on Linux, `ping -M do -s 1400 <host>`) at decreasing sizes to find the largest packet that passes, or by capturing\ntraffic and seeing retransmissions of large segments. Fix it by enabling TCP MSS clamping on the VPN\ngateway to a value that fits the tunnel overhead, or by allowing the relevant ICMP messages so path MTU\ndiscovery works. Then retest with a large result set."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do production site-to-site VPNs usually use BGP rather than static routes?","options":["BGP encrypts traffic more strongly than static routing","BGP advertises and withdraws prefixes dynamically, so traffic fails over to the redundant tunnel automatically","Static routes cannot be used with IPsec","BGP removes the need for non-overlapping address ranges"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A static route says nothing about whether its tunnel is alive, so failover relies on vendor-specific tunnel monitoring or manual action. BGP withdraws routes from a failed tunnel as part of the protocol. Encryption is ESP's job, and overlapping ranges remain a problem with either routing method."},{"q":"A customer asks for a VPN so your SaaS can call their single internal REST API. Their network team worries about exposure. What is the strongest scoping you can offer?","options":["Use a pre-shared key instead of certificates to keep configuration simple","Advertise the customer's full 10.0.0.0/8 so routing never needs updating","Terminate in a dedicated integration network, advertise only the API host's address, and allow only its port from your worker subnet","Enable split tunneling on every engineer's laptop"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Scoping routes to a single host and filtering ports on both sides limits what the joined networks can reach. Broad advertisements create the exposure the network team fears, and PSK choice affects authentication rather than reach."},{"q":"When is a dedicated circuit such as Direct Connect or ExpressRoute a better choice than a VPN?","options":["When the integration is a two-week pilot that needs to start tomorrow","Whenever the data is confidential, because VPNs are not encrypted","When the customer has overlapping CIDR ranges","When traffic is large and permanent or needs predictable latency and bandwidth that the internet path cannot guarantee"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Dedicated circuits trade long lead times and higher fixed cost for consistent bandwidth and latency. VPNs are encrypted, and a circuit does nothing to fix overlapping address ranges, which still require re-addressing or NAT."}]},{"key":"deployment/terraform","sources":["Official docs: Terraform","Official docs: OpenTofu","Kief Morris, 2020 — Infrastructure as Code (2nd edition)"],"why":"- **An AI platform is a lot of infrastructure.** A VPC with private subnets, private endpoints to the\n  model API and storage, a Kubernetes cluster with a GPU node pool, a vector database, KMS keys, IAM\n  roles, log sinks. An agent adds a memory store, checkpoint tables and one role per tool.\n- **Console work works once.** Then you need a staging copy, the customer wants it in their own account\n  in another region, and an auditor asks who opened port 443 to the world and when. Console changes\n  leave no reviewable record, cannot be reproduced exactly, and drift: the third environment is never\n  quite like the first.\n- **Imperative scripts are brittle.** A shell file of CLI calls encodes steps rather than outcomes, so\n  running it twice fails (\"already exists\") or duplicates resources, and it cannot say what would change\n  before changing it.\n- **Declarative IaC makes the files the source of truth.** You state what should exist; the tool works\n  out the create, update and delete calls and shows them to a reviewer first. Changes go through pull\n  requests, so a security reviewer approves the diff rather than trusting a screenshot.\n- **Decisive for forward-deployed work.** The same reviewed module can be run by the customer's own\n  platform team, in their account, under their change control.","how":"### Configuration, providers and the resource graph\n- **Resources in HCL.** You write `.tf` files declaring resources (`aws_vpc_endpoint`,\n  `google_container_node_pool`, `azurerm_private_endpoint`) with arguments.\n- **Providers are plugins** translating each resource type into API calls for a cloud or service; that\n  plugin model is why one tool covers AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, DNS providers and many SaaS\n  products.\n- **A dependency graph.** References between resources (a security group ID used by an endpoint) become\n  a graph; independent resources are created in parallel and dependent ones in order, which is why you\n  rarely write ordering by hand.\n\n### State\n- **What it records.** What Terraform created, and the real IDs, in a state file.\n- **Why it exists.** The tool must map `aws_s3_bucket.documents` in your code to one specific bucket in\n  the account, and know which resources it owns so it can delete one you removed from the code.\n- **Remote with locking.** Teams store state remotely (object storage, Terraform Cloud/HCP, or similar)\n  with locking so two applies cannot run at once and corrupt it (the S3 backend's native lock file is\n  `use_lockfile`), and version the state bucket so a bad write can be rolled back.\n- **It is a secret.** State holds every attribute the provider returns, including generated passwords and\n  keys, in plain text, so encrypt and access-control it like a secret. Never give an agent's tools read\n  access to it.\n\n### Plan and apply\n1. **Plan.** `terraform plan` refreshes state from the real APIs, compares with your configuration and\n   prints the create/update/replace/destroy actions.\n2. **Read \"replace\" carefully.** Some argument changes cannot be updated in place and force\n   destroy-then-create, which for a database or bucket means data loss: for an agent, an empty vector\n   index or lost conversation memory.\n3. **Apply exactly what was reviewed.** `terraform apply` executes a plan; in CI you save the plan file\n   and apply exactly that plan after approval, so what was reviewed is what runs.\n\n### How the diff decides update versus replace\n- **Three inputs per resource:** the configuration, the last-recorded state, and what the refresh read\n  from the API.\n- **The provider schema decides.** If an argument differs, the schema says whether the API can change it\n  in place (a tag, an instance count) or whether it is immutable once created (a bucket name, a\n  cluster's subnet), in which case the action becomes replace.\n- **Order of replacement.** Defaults to destroy-then-create *because* many names must be unique;\n  `lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }` reverses the order for stateless things like launch\n  templates or certificates, so there is no gap.\n\n| Plan symbol | Action | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+` | create | low |\n| `~` | update in place | usually low; read the changed arguments |\n| `-/+` | destroy then create (replace) | data loss for stateful resources |\n| `+/-` | create then destroy (`create_before_destroy`) | brief duplicate; names must allow it |\n| `-` | destroy | check it was intended |\n\n### Resource addresses are identity\n- **State keys objects by code address** (`module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents`), not by cloud ID.\n- **So refactors look like replacement.** Renaming a block, moving it into a module or switching `count`\n  to `for_each` reads as \"delete the old address, create a new one\", even though nothing real changed.\n- **The fix.** A `moved { from = ..., to = ... }` block (or `terraform state mv`) says the object merely\n  changed address, and the plan becomes a no-op. This is the mechanism behind most \"harmless refactor\n  recreated the database\" stories.\n\n### Modules, environments and drift\n- **Modules are functions.** They package resources behind input variables and outputs. A \"customer\n  agent deployment\" module might take a VPC ID, subnet IDs, region and KMS key, and output the agent\n  service URL; each environment or customer is a small root configuration calling it with different\n  inputs.\n- **Drift.** If someone changes a resource in the console, the next plan shows drift and proposes\n  reverting it.\n- **Import.** Existing hand-built resources are adopted with an `import` block (Terraform 1.5+) or\n  `terraform import`, so\n  they come under management without being recreated.\n\n### One state per environment\nEach environment or customer gets its own state, so a plan for staging cannot touch production.\n\n| Layout | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Separate root directories per environment | explicit; each has its own backend key and credentials | some duplication |\n| CLI workspaces sharing one configuration | less duplication | same credentials; an easy-to-forget `terraform workspace select` |\n\nFor customer accounts with separate change boards, separate roots with separate backends is the usual\nchoice *because* the isolation boundary should match the approval boundary.\n\n### When an agent drives Terraform\nInfra and coding agents increasingly propose infrastructure changes. Treat `plan` as a read-only tool\nand `apply` as a consequential action:\n- **Plan is safe to automate** in a sandboxed workspace with read-only cloud credentials, because it\n  changes nothing real.\n- **Apply needs a human gate** on the saved plan file, because the model cannot be trusted to read a\n  `-/+` line on a stateful resource correctly every time.\n- **The plan guard is the agent's guardrail too:** run it on the agent's plan and return blocked changes\n  to the model as a tool error.\n\n### Alternatives\n| Tool | Shape | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| CloudFormation (AWS), Bicep/ARM (Azure) | native, single-cloud | no state file to manage, since the provider tracks it |\n| Pulumi, AWS CDK | general-purpose languages | helps loops and abstraction; reviews depend on reading code rather than a declarative file |\n| OpenTofu | community fork of Terraform | created after HashiCorp moved Terraform to the Business Source License in 2023; keeps HCL and the provider ecosystem; open source |","example":"### Module for private model access\n- **What it contains.** A locked remote state, a security group that only admits the app subnet, an\n  interface endpoint for the model runtime with private DNS, and a document bucket protected from\n  accidental destruction. This is the base layer a RAG agent needs.\n- **Check the names.** Argument names follow the AWS provider; check them against the provider version\n  you pin.\n\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = { source = \"hashicorp/aws\", version = \"~> 5.0\" }\n  }\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"acme-tfstate\"\n    key            = \"ai-platform/prod.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"eu-west-1\"\n    use_lockfile   = true         # S3-native lock file (added in 1.10, GA in 1.11); supersedes dynamodb_table\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"vpc_id\"             { type = string }\nvariable \"private_subnet_ids\" { type = list(string) }\nvariable \"app_cidr\"           { type = string }\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"model_ep\" {\n  name   = \"model-runtime-endpoint\"\n  vpc_id = var.vpc_id\n  ingress {\n    from_port   = 443\n    to_port     = 443\n    protocol    = \"tcp\"\n    cidr_blocks = [var.app_cidr]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_vpc_endpoint\" \"model_runtime\" {\n  vpc_id              = var.vpc_id\n  service_name        = \"com.amazonaws.eu-west-1.bedrock-runtime\"\n  vpc_endpoint_type   = \"Interface\"\n  subnet_ids          = var.private_subnet_ids     # one subnet per availability zone\n  security_group_ids  = [aws_security_group.model_ep.id]\n  private_dns_enabled = true\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"documents\" {\n  bucket = \"acme-rag-documents-prod\"\n  lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }   # plan fails instead of deleting customer data\n}\n```\n\n- **Fresh account:** `terraform plan` shows three resources to add.\n- **Changing `bucket` later** would show a replace, and `prevent_destroy` turns that into an error\n  rather than a deleted archive.\n- **Limit of the guard.** It lives in the resource block, so it does not protect against someone\n  deleting the block itself; pair it with bucket versioning or a deletion-protection setting on the\n  cloud side.\n\n### A least-privilege role for one agent tool\nEach tool gets its own role, so a prompt-injected agent can only do what that tool's policy allows. Here\nthe `read_contract` tool may read objects under one prefix of the documents bucket and nothing else.\n\n```hcl\ndata \"aws_iam_policy_document\" \"read_contract\" {\n  statement {\n    actions   = [\"s3:GetObject\"]\n    resources = [\"${aws_s3_bucket.documents.arn}/contracts/*\"]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_iam_policy\" \"read_contract\" {\n  name   = \"agent-tool-read-contract\"\n  policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.read_contract.json\n}\n```\n\n- **Review it as a diff.** Widening `resources` to the whole bucket shows up in the plan as a `~` on the\n  policy, which a security reviewer can reject before the agent ever gains the access.\n\n### Refactor and adopt without recreating anything\nMoving the bucket into a module, and adopting a hand-built KMS key, both as plan-visible code.\n\n```hcl\nmoved {\n  from = aws_s3_bucket.documents\n  to   = module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents\n}\n\nimport {\n  to = aws_kms_key.rag\n  id = \"1234abcd-12ab-34cd-56ef-1234567890ab\"   # the existing key's ID\n}\n```\n\nExpected plan: zero destroys, one import, and no change for the moved bucket. Anything else means the\nrefactor is not what you think.\n\n### CI guard that blocks replacing data resources\nReads the JSON form of a saved plan (`terraform show -json tfplan`) and fails the pipeline if a protected\nresource type would be destroyed or replaced. The same script can sit behind an agent's plan tool.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Fail CI if a Terraform plan deletes or replaces protected resources.\nUsage: terraform show -json tfplan > plan.json && python plan_guard.py plan.json\"\"\"\nimport json, sys\n\nPROTECTED = {\"aws_s3_bucket\", \"aws_db_instance\", \"aws_opensearch_domain\", \"aws_kms_key\"}\n\ndef risky_changes(plan):\n    for rc in plan.get(\"resource_changes\", []):\n        actions = rc[\"change\"][\"actions\"]          # e.g. [\"update\"], [\"delete\", \"create\"]\n        if \"delete\" in actions:\n            kind = \"REPLACE\" if \"create\" in actions else \"DESTROY\"\n            yield kind, rc[\"address\"], rc[\"type\"]\n\ndef main(path):\n    plan = json.load(open(path))\n    blocked = 0\n    for kind, address, rtype in risky_changes(plan):\n        flag = \"BLOCK\" if rtype in PROTECTED else \"review\"\n        blocked += flag == \"BLOCK\"\n        print(f\"{flag:6} {kind:7} {address}\")\n    sys.exit(1 if blocked else 0)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main(sys.argv[1])\n# BLOCK  REPLACE module.rag.aws_s3_bucket.documents\n# review REPLACE aws_launch_template.gpu\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an infra agent proposes a change\nAn internal platform agent receives \"add a staging copy of the RAG stack\" and edits HCL in a branch. Its\nonly Terraform tool runs plan; apply waits for a human.\n\n| Step | What happens | Gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Agent edits modules | adds a staging root calling `module.rag` | code review |\n| 2. Agent calls plan tool | plan: 3 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy | plan guard passes |\n| 3. Agent \"tidies\" resource names inside the shared `rag` module | prod re-plan: 3 destroys, 3 creates | guard returns BLOCK to the agent |\n| 4. Agent adds `moved` blocks in the module | prod: no changes; staging: 3 to add | guard passes |\n| 5. Human approves saved plan | `terraform apply tfplan` in CI | change ticket |\n\nStep 3 is the one that matters: the model saw a harmless rename, the guard saw a replaced bucket in an\nenvironment that already exists. A rename in the not-yet-applied staging root alone would destroy nothing.","patterns":["**Saved plan, exact apply.** In CI run `terraform plan -out=tfplan` on every pull request, attach the rendered plan to the review or change ticket, and apply with `terraform apply tfplan` after approval. Monitor applies that ran without a matching reviewed plan; the target is none.","**One role per agent tool.** Define each tool's IAM role in Terraform next to the agent service, scoped to the resources that tool needs, so tool permissions are reviewed as diffs. Monitor access-denied errors per tool role; a spike means a tool needs a scoped change, not a wildcard.","**Scheduled drift detection.** Run `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` nightly against main; exit code 2 means changes are pending. Alert on non-empty plans and trace each to a console change or a missing back-port.","**Pin providers and commit the lock file.** Use version constraints like `~> 5.0` and commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`; upgrade deliberately with `terraform init -upgrade` in its own pull request. Watch for plans that change resources nobody touched.","**Provider mirror for isolated accounts.** Build it with `terraform providers mirror <dir>`, ship it in the release bundle, and point the customer's CLI configuration at it with a `filesystem_mirror` block, so `terraform init` never reaches the public registry.","**Split state by layer and guard data twice.** Separate network, cluster and application states so a routine app change cannot plan against the VPC; pass values with outputs and data sources, and track plan duration per state. Protect the vector store and memory tables with `prevent_destroy`, cloud-side deletion protection, and the plan-guard script in CI."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent in production rests on infrastructure: a private path to the\n  model API, a vector database, a memory or checkpoint store, KMS keys, and IAM roles that decide what\n  each tool may touch. Terraform makes that stack identical across staging, prod and each customer.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Modules for `aws_vpc_endpoint` and buckets, the IAM policy behind\n  your agent's tool role, the remote state backend, and the CI pipeline that runs `terraform plan`.\n- **What you control.** Least-privilege roles per tool, `prevent_destroy` on the vector store and memory\n  tables, and the rule that an infra or coding agent may run plan but never apply without a human\n  approval gate.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing providers and the internals of the dependency graph. Know state, plan,\n  the replace hazard, `moved` blocks and how to ship into an isolated account.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Reproducible agent stacks per customer.** One module stands up the private model endpoint, vector\n  store, memory tables and tool roles in each customer account under their change board.\n- **Least-privilege tool permissions.** Each agent tool's IAM role lives in code, so security reviews\n  every widening as a plan diff.\n- **Protecting agent memory and indexes.** `prevent_destroy` and the plan guard stop a refactor from\n  recreating the vector database or conversation store empty.\n- **Infra agents with approval gates.** A platform agent drafts HCL and runs plan; humans approve the\n  saved plan before apply.","mini_project":"**Build a plan-only Terraform tool for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** let an agent propose infrastructure changes while a guard and a human keep apply safe.\n- **Steps:** apply a small baseline yourself first (against LocalStack, or with `terraform_data`\n  resources and the guard's `PROTECTED` set adjusted), so state exists; write a tool that runs\n  `terraform plan -out=tfplan -input=false` and `terraform show -json tfplan`; run the plan guard on the\n  JSON and return a summary (adds, changes, destroys, blocked addresses) to the model; give a small\n  tool-calling agent the task \"rename the documents bucket block\" and observe it respond to a BLOCK.\n- **Done when:** the agent's first attempt is blocked, it recovers by adding a `moved` block until the plan\n  shows zero destroys, and there is no apply tool anywhere in its tool list."},"exercise":"**Goal:** extend the plan guard into the safety layer an infra agent's plan tool returns.\n- **Inputs:** hand-written plan JSON fixtures in the `terraform show -json` shape: a clean add, a bucket\n  replace, a rename with and without a `moved` block (moved resources carry `previous_address`), and an\n  IAM policy update widening `Resource` to `*`.\n- **Deliver:** a function returning a structured summary for the model (adds, changes, destroys, blocked\n  addresses with a reason), plus a rule flagging wildcard widenings in IAM policy diffs.\n- **Acceptance:** pytest passes on every fixture; the replace and the wildcard return BLOCK; the moved\n  rename returns zero destroys; if Terraform is installed, a real `terraform_data` rename reproduces the\n  fixture.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a customer's change board must approve every apply in their account. Your team switches a\nmodule's three vector-store collections from `count` to `for_each` and bumps the AWS provider from 5.x\nto 6.x in the same pull request.\n\nThe plan shows 3 destroys, 3 creates and 2 unexpected in-place updates. What do you do before anything\nreaches the board?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- splits the PR, because two causes in one plan make neither reviewable;\n- explains the 3 replaces as address changes (`[0]` to `[\"name\"]`) and adds `moved` blocks until zero\n  destroys;\n- isolates the provider upgrade with `terraform init -upgrade` alone and traces each update to a changed\n  default in the changelog, knowing state written by 6.x may not load under 5.x;\n- hands the board a saved plan per change, applied exactly, with the guard output attached.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"HCL from engineer or agent\"] --> P[\"terraform plan\"]\n    S[(\"Remote state with lock\")] --> P\n    API[\"Cloud provider APIs\"] -->|\"refresh\"| P\n    P --> F[\"Saved plan file\"]\n    F --> R{\"Reviewer: any replace or destroy?\"}\n    R -->|\"yes, unexpected\"| C\n    R -->|\"approved\"| A[\"terraform apply saved plan\"]\n    A --> API\n    A -->|\"new IDs and attributes\"| S","caption":"The reviewed plan, not the code, is what gets applied, whether a human or an agent wrote the code;\nstate is the bridge between code addresses and real objects."},"interview":[{"q":"What is declarative IaC, and why is it better than a script of CLI calls?","a":"- **Declarative:** you describe the desired end state and the tool computes create, update and delete\n  calls by diffing code, state and the real APIs.\n- **Why better:** it is idempotent (running twice changes nothing), it can show a plan before acting, and\n  changes go through code review instead of screenshots.\n- **Scripts encode steps**, so re-runs fail with \"already exists\" or duplicate resources."},{"q":"A pull request that only renamed modules shows 14 destroys and 14 creates. What happened and what do you do?","a":"- **Cause:** state keys objects by address, so new addresses look like new resources and old ones look\n  removed.\n- **Fix:** add `moved` blocks from each old address to the new one (or `terraform state mv`) and re-plan\n  until it is a no-op.\n- **Guard:** a CI check that fails on destroys of stateful types would have stopped the apply."},{"q":"You are giving an internal infra agent the ability to change Terraform. How do you make that safe?","a":"- **Split the tools:** a plan tool with read-only cloud credentials in a sandboxed workspace, and no apply\n  tool at all; apply runs in CI from the saved plan after a human approves.\n- **Guard the plan:** run the plan guard on every agent plan and return blocked replaces or destroys to\n  the model as an error it must resolve, for example with `moved` blocks.\n- **Keep secrets away:** the agent never reads state, since state holds generated passwords and keys in\n  plain text."},{"q":"How do you deliver Terraform into an air-gapped customer account?","a":"- **Ship providers with the release:** build a mirror with `terraform providers mirror`, and configure a\n  filesystem mirror so `terraform init` never contacts the registry.\n- **Pin versions and include the lock file** so the mirror and the plan agree.\n- **Ship modules as files** with an expected-plan summary, since the customer cannot pull them from a\n  registry either."}],"tradeoffs":["**Reviewable plans vs critical state.** Declarative plans make every change reviewable before it happens, but the state file becomes critical shared infrastructure; losing or corrupting it is worse than losing the code.","**Multi-cloud breadth vs native simplicity.** One tool across clouds and SaaS providers helps teams deploying agents into varied customer accounts, but a single-cloud team may find native CloudFormation or Bicep simpler since there is no state to operate.","**Restricted HCL vs expressive code.** HCL keeps configurations readable for security reviewers, but complex logic (dynamic topologies, heavy loops) is awkward; general-purpose IaC tools win when logic dominates.","**One big state vs split states.** Large single state files give one view of everything but make plans slow and blast radius big. Splitting by layer (network, cluster, app) limits damage at the cost of cross-state references.","**Agent-written IaC vs review load.** An agent can draft modules and plans fast, but every plan still needs a human to read replace lines. Worth it when the guard catches the dangerous cases and review stays on the diff."],"pitfalls":["**Committing state or secrets to git, or storing state unencrypted.** Symptom: a secret scanner alert, or an auditor finding database passwords readable by everyone with repository access. Diagnose: `git log --all -- '*.tfstate'` and the backend's encryption and access settings. Fix: remote encrypted state, rotate every secret it held.","**Approving a plan without reading \"must be replaced\" lines.** Symptom: a vector database or bucket recreated empty, and the agent answers with no retrieval hits. Cause: an immutable argument changed. Diagnose: in `terraform show -json`, list changes whose actions contain both delete and create. Fix: the plan guard in CI.","**Refactoring names or module structure without `moved` blocks.** Symptom: equal destroys and creates for a pull request that \"only renamed things\". Diagnose: pair each destroyed address with a created one of the same type. Fix: `moved` blocks until the plan is a no-op.","**Unpinned provider versions or an uncommitted `.terraform.lock.hcl`.** Symptom: a plan proposing changes nobody made, after a provider release changed a default. Diagnose: diff the provider version in `terraform version` against the last good run.","**Emergency console fixes never back-ported.** Symptom: the next routine apply silently reverts the fix and re-causes the incident. Diagnose: nightly `-detailed-exitcode` drift plans; exit code 2 flags it.","**Giving an agent apply rights.** Symptom: an infra agent \"fixes\" a failing plan by applying it, and a stateful resource is replaced. Fix: plan-only credentials and human approval of the saved plan.","**Assuming an air-gapped environment can download providers.** Symptom: `terraform init` fails. Fix: ship a provider mirror."],"when":"- **Use IaC for** anything that must exist in more than one environment or survive an audit: every\n  production AI deployment, and every customer-VPC deployment of an agent.\n- **Terraform or OpenTofu by default** when you deploy across clouds or into many customer accounts, or\n  when the customer's platform team already uses it.\n- **Native templates** when an organization is single-cloud and mandates them.\n- **Not for application rollout.** Keep container versions and model weights in a deploy tool such as\n  Helm or Argo rather than Terraform, since those change far more often. Prompts and tool definitions\n  belong with the agent's code and release process, not in Terraform.\n- **Measure:** drift (plans that are not empty on main) and time to stand up a new environment.\n- **Interviews:** expect probes on state, plan review and the replace hazard.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes modules for GPU node pools, endpoints and buckets, runs plan in CI on every pull request, and reads each replace or destroy action before approving.","architect":"Sets the state layout, module boundaries and versioning, decides which layer IaC owns versus the deploy tool, and makes plan output part of the change-approval evidence.","fde":"Hands the customer a versioned module and variables file their platform team can run in their own account, plus a provider mirror when their environment cannot reach the registry."},"check":{"q":"A customer's platform team must run your deployment in their own AWS account under their change\nboard, and they forbid your engineers from having write access. How do you structure the Terraform\ndelivery so it is reviewable, repeatable and safe for them to run?","a":"Ship a versioned module with a narrow, documented variable interface (VPC and subnet IDs, KMS key,\nregion, sizing), no hard-coded account details, and pinned provider versions. Their root configuration\ncalls it, with state in their backend under their access controls and locking. Their CI runs plan and\nattaches the saved plan to the change ticket; the board reviews it, and apply executes exactly that\nplan. Protect data resources with `prevent_destroy`, document every replace-triggering argument, and\npublish an IAM policy listing the permissions the apply role needs. For upgrades, release a new module\nversion with a changelog and an expected-plan summary so they can compare. If the account is isolated,\ninclude a provider mirror."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Terraform need a state file at all, when it could just query the cloud APIs?","options":["To cache API responses so plans run faster","To map each resource in the code to the specific real object it manages, including ones removed from code that must be deleted","Because cloud APIs do not report resource attributes","To store the HCL source in a binary form"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without state, Terraform cannot know which real bucket corresponds to a resource block, or which objects it owns when a block is deleted. Refreshing does query the APIs, but only for objects state identifies."},{"q":"A reviewer sees a plan line saying the vector database cluster \"must be replaced\" after a minor-looking argument change. What is the right response?","options":["Approve it, since Terraform preserves data during replacement","Run apply twice so the second run cancels the replacement","Treat it as destroy-and-recreate with data loss, and find an in-place path or a planned migration before applying","Delete the state file so Terraform forgets the cluster exists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Replacement destroys the existing resource and creates a new one, so stored data is lost unless migrated. Deleting state would make Terraform try to create a duplicate and abandon the original unmanaged."},{"q":"Why might an AWS-only team reasonably prefer CloudFormation over Terraform?","options":["CloudFormation supports more third-party SaaS providers","Terraform cannot manage AWS networking resources","CloudFormation lets you write infrastructure in Python","AWS tracks the stack state itself, so there is no separate state file and locking setup to operate"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The native service manages state and rollback for you, which removes an operational burden. Terraform's advantage is breadth across clouds and SaaS, and Python-based infrastructure belongs to CDK or Pulumi."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-questionnaires","sources":["Official docs: Cloud Security Alliance — Consensus Assessments Initiative Questionnaire (CAIQ)","Official docs: Shared Assessments — Standardized Information Gathering (SIG) questionnaire","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"- **Buyers cannot inspect every vendor.** A large company may have thousands of vendors, each a possible\n  path to a breach, so its risk team standardizes the inquiry: an industry template such as the Shared\n  Assessments SIG or the Cloud Security Alliance's CAIQ, or a custom spreadsheet built from the buyer's\n  own control framework.\n- **Answers have consequences.** They are scored, gaps become findings, and serious gaps block\n  procurement or become contract clauses.\n- **AI vendors get extra scrutiny** because the product's core function is sending customer data to a\n  model. Buyers ask what they never asked a CRM vendor: is our data used to train any model, which model\n  providers see it, how long do they retain prompts, can your agent take actions, how do you stop prompt\n  injection from exfiltrating data.\n- **The naive approaches fail.** Answering from memory gives inconsistent answers across customers, which\n  a sophisticated buyer or a later dispute will expose. Answering \"Yes\" to everything creates\n  misrepresentation risk, because questionnaires are often incorporated by reference into the contract.\n- **It is an interface, not paperwork.** Treated as separate from engineering, answers drift as the\n  system changes. The questionnaire is a structured interface between your actual controls and the\n  buyer's risk model, and should be maintained like one.","how":"### Background: what arrives and how the buyer reviews it\n- **Format.** Typically a spreadsheet or a portal from a third-party risk platform, with\n  yes/no/not-applicable columns, a free-text comment and an evidence-upload field per question.\n- **Domains:** governance and policies, access control and identity, encryption in transit and at rest,\n  key management, network security, vulnerability management and penetration testing, logging and\n  monitoring, incident response and notification, business continuity, data retention and deletion,\n  privacy, subprocessors, secure development, and increasingly an AI section.\n\nThe buyer's workflow explains what they reward:\n1. **Inherent-risk tiering.** Before sending anything, the risk team classifies the engagement by what\n   you will touch: data sensitivity, volume, network or system access, and how critical you are to\n   operations.\n   - A vendor processing patient records with agent tool access lands in the top tier; one that sees only\n     marketing copy does not.\n   - The tier sets the depth, *because* the team's review hours are the scarce resource.\n   - Templates are built for this: the SIG comes in a shorter Lite and a fuller Core form, and CAIQ maps\n     each question to a control in the CSA Cloud Controls Matrix.\n2. **Scoring and findings.** Each answer is compared with the buyer's required control. A \"No\" or a vague\n   answer on a required control becomes a finding with a severity.\n3. **Follow-up and evidence requests.** Reviewers ask for proof on the answers that matter most to their\n   tier; precise first answers mean fewer rounds.\n4. **Disposition.** Findings are accepted as risk, mitigated by a compensating control, or turned into\n   remediation commitments with dates, which often land in the contract.\n5. **Reassessment.** High-tier vendors are typically re-reviewed periodically and on material change, so\n   today's answer will be compared with next year's.\n\nThe upshot: a reviewer wants residual risk they can sign off on, not a perfect score, so a clearly scoped\ngap with a mitigation is easier to approve than an ambiguous \"Yes\".\n\n### Evidence that shortcuts it\nBuyers accept independent assurance in place of many answers:\n\n| Evidence | What it shows |\n|---|---|\n| SOC 2 Type II report | an auditor's opinion that controls operated effectively over a period |\n| SOC 2 Type I report | control design at a point in time only |\n| ISO/IEC 27001 certificate | a certified information security management system |\n| Recent penetration test summary | independent testing, without the full findings |\n| Public subprocessor list, trust center page | answers many questions at once |\n\nSending the report early, under NDA, often removes a large share of the questionnaire.\n\n### The AI and agent section\n- **Expect questions on:** training use of customer data (yours and every model provider's), prompt and\n  output retention by each subprocessor, region of processing, model provenance and evaluation, human\n  oversight, guardrails against prompt injection and data leakage, tool permissions for agents, and how\n  customers can delete their data including embeddings and caches.\n- **Answer per data flow**, because the honest answer often differs between the hosted-API path and a\n  self-hosted model path.\n\n| Agent question | Where the true answer lives |\n|---|---|\n| Can the agent take actions in our systems? | the tool registry: which tools write, and their scopes |\n| Is there human approval before consequential actions? | the approval-gate config per tool |\n| Where are prompts and tool results stored, and for how long? | trace exporter, log retention, memory store TTLs |\n| How do you prevent prompt-injected exfiltration? | egress limits, tool allowlists, output filters, eval results |\n| Can we delete our data, including embeddings and memory? | the deletion job across vector store, caches, checkpoints |\n\n### The answer library\n- **One library of approved answers**, each tied to the underlying control, an owner (security,\n  infrastructure, legal, ML), the evidence that proves it and a review date.\n- **Answering is retrieval.** New questionnaires are answered by matching each question to library\n  entries and adapting wording; many teams use RAG over the library to draft responses.\n- **A human owner approves every answer**, because a confidently wrong generated answer is precisely the\n  misrepresentation you are trying to avoid.\n\n### Writing a good answer\n- **Fact, scope, mechanism, evidence.** State the fact, its scope and the mechanism, and say where it is\n  evidenced.\n- **Answer the question asked**, not a neighbouring one.\n- **Honest gaps.** When the answer is \"No\" or \"Partially\", say so and add the compensating control or a\n  dated roadmap item; reviewers deal with gaps constantly, but discovered misstatements destroy trust.\n- **\"Not applicable\" only with a one-line reason.**","example":"### Worked scenario: three answers in a 300-question health-tech review\n| Question | Weak answer | Strong answer | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"Is customer data used to train AI models?\" | \"No.\" | \"No. Customer prompts, documents and outputs are not used to train or fine-tune our models. Our LLM subprocessor processes data under API terms that exclude training use; its retention terms and region are listed on our subprocessor page. Evidence: DPA section 4, subprocessor list.\" | covers the subprocessor, which is what the buyer really wants to know |\n| \"Is all data encrypted at rest with customer-managed keys?\" | \"Yes.\" | \"Partially\": data at rest is encrypted with provider-managed keys; customer-managed keys exist only on the dedicated-deployment tier. Explain both tiers and point to the dedicated option. | \"Yes\" is true for one tier and false for the one this customer bought |\n| \"Do you retain prompts and responses?\" | \"No.\" | \"Yes, for 30 days for debugging, access-restricted, deletable on request.\" | engineers found traces with full prompts kept for 30 days in the observability tool, which nobody had listed |\n\n- **The third answer surfaced a real gap** before an audit did; the observability vendor was added to\n  the subprocessor list. Agent traces are the usual culprit, because they capture every prompt, tool\n  argument and tool result.\n- **Overall**, the SOC 2 report removed about a third of the questions as covered (an illustrative\n  proportion), and the remaining answers were drafted from the library and approved by owners within the\n  week.\n\n### Answer library: match a new question and flag stale entries\nA minimal sketch of the retrieval and freshness checks. Real libraries use embeddings or a\nquestionnaire platform; the routing to an owner and the review-date check are the parts that matter.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nLIBRARY = [\n    {\"id\": \"AI-01\", \"q\": \"is customer data used to train ai models\",\n     \"answer\": \"No. Not used to train or fine-tune; LLM subprocessor terms exclude training.\",\n     \"owner\": \"ml\", \"evidence\": \"DPA s4, subprocessor list\", \"reviewed\": date(2026, 6, 1)},\n    {\"id\": \"ENC-03\", \"q\": \"is data encrypted at rest with customer managed keys\",\n     \"answer\": \"Partially. Provider-managed keys; customer-managed keys on the dedicated tier.\",\n     \"owner\": \"infra\", \"evidence\": \"SOC 2 report, tier matrix\", \"reviewed\": date(2025, 9, 15)},\n    {\"id\": \"RET-02\", \"q\": \"do you retain prompts and responses\",\n     \"answer\": \"Yes, 30 days for debugging, access-restricted, deletable on request.\",\n     \"owner\": \"security\", \"evidence\": \"retention policy, trace config\", \"reviewed\": date(2026, 8, 20)},\n]\n\ndef words(text):\n    return set(text.lower().replace(\"?\", \"\").replace(\"-\", \" \").split())\n\ndef best_match(question, library=LIBRARY):\n    q = words(question)\n    score = lambda e: len(q & words(e[\"q\"])) / len(q | words(e[\"q\"]))\n    entry = max(library, key=score)\n    return entry, round(score(entry), 2)\n\ndef stale(library=LIBRARY, today=date(2026, 10, 20), max_age_days=180):\n    return [e[\"id\"] for e in library if (today - e[\"reviewed\"]).days > max_age_days]\n\nentry, s = best_match(\"Are customer prompts used to train or fine-tune models?\")\nprint(entry[\"id\"], s, \"-> route to owner:\", entry[\"owner\"])\nprint(\"needs review before reuse:\", stale())\n# AI-01 0.38 -> route to owner: ml\n# needs review before reuse: ['ENC-03']\n```\n\n### A drafting agent that knows when not to answer\nWrap the library in an agent step that drafts only from approved, fresh entries above a match threshold,\nand otherwise hands the question to the owner with no draft. It reuses `best_match` and `stale` above.\n\n```python\nMIN_SCORE = 0.3          # illustrative; tune on past questionnaires\n\ndef draft(question, llm):\n    entry, score = best_match(question)\n    if score < MIN_SCORE:\n        return {\"status\": \"needs_owner\", \"reason\": \"no library match\", \"draft\": None}\n    if entry[\"id\"] in stale():\n        return {\"status\": \"needs_owner\", \"owner\": entry[\"owner\"],\n                \"reason\": f\"{entry['id']} past review date\", \"draft\": None}\n    text = llm(f\"Rewrite this approved answer to fit the question. Do not add claims.\\n\"\n               f\"Question: {question}\\nApproved answer ({entry['id']}): {entry['answer']}\\n\"\n               f\"Evidence: {entry['evidence']}\")\n    return {\"status\": \"awaiting_approval\", \"owner\": entry[\"owner\"],\n            \"source\": entry[\"id\"], \"draft\": text}   # never sent until the owner approves\n```\n\n- **Why refuse instead of guess.** A fluent answer with no approved source is the misrepresentation the\n  library exists to prevent; an empty draft routed to an owner is the safe failure.\n- **Measure it like any agent.** Track the share of drafts the owner edits before sending; a high edit\n  rate means the library or the prompt is wrong.","patterns":["**Answer library with owners and review dates.** Store each answer with its control, owner, evidence link and last-reviewed date; block reuse of entries past their review window (for example six months) until the owner re-approves. Track the share of stale entries.","**Architecture-change trigger.** Add a pull-request template question such as \"does this change a data flow, region, retention or subprocessor?\" that pings the library owner. Agent changes that add a tool, a model fallback or a trace exporter should always trip it. Monitor answers changed after engineering review; a high count means the library lags the system.","**Trust center plus NDA-gated reports.** Publish the subprocessor list, data-flow summary and AI data-use statement; share the SOC 2 report and full pen-test results only under NDA. Measure questions answered by reference to it.","**Subprocessor list reconciled with egress.** Periodically compare the published list with the vendors seen in DNS and egress logs and billing, because an unlisted observability or model vendor is the classic miss. Alert on any new external domain in production egress.","**Per-flow AI answers.** Keep separate entries for the hosted-API path and the self-hosted path for training use, retention and region, and quote the one matching the customer's tier.","**Generate the agent section from config.** Export the tool registry (tool, read or write, scope, approval required) and retention settings into the library entry, so \"what can the agent do\" is never answered from memory. Track turnaround time, follow-up rounds and findings per buyer tier."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent deals stall on questions only the builder can answer: does the\n  agent retain prompts in traces, which model subprocessors see data, can it take actions, what stops\n  prompt injection from exfiltrating data. Your design choices become those answers.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Trace and log retention settings, the model routing and fallback\n  config, each tool's permission scope, the approval gate on consequential actions, and how embeddings,\n  caches and memory are deleted on request.\n- **What you control.** Building the agent so the answers are good ones (a no-retention model path,\n  region-pinned routing, least-privilege tools), and flagging every change that alters a published\n  answer.\n- **What you can skip.** Scoring methodology and SIG versus CAIQ details. Know that answers are scored,\n  become findings, and are often incorporated into the contract.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Unblocking an enterprise agent deal.** The AI section asks about retention, model subprocessors and\n  tool permissions; answers generated from the agent's own config close it in one round.\n- **Designing for good answers.** Teams pick a no-retention model path and region-pinned routing early\n  because they know the questionnaire will ask.\n- **RAG drafting agents for security teams.** An internal agent drafts responses from the approved library\n  and routes every draft to its owner.\n- **Catching hidden subprocessors.** Reconciling egress logs with the subprocessor list finds the trace\n  vendor that stores every prompt.","mini_project":"**Build a questionnaire-drafting agent with refusal (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** draft answers to 20 sample questions from a small approved library, without inventing claims.\n- **Steps:** write 10 library entries (include two stale ones and an agent-tools entry generated from a\n  toy tool registry); implement `best_match`, `stale` and the `draft` step with any LLM; run 20 questions,\n  including five with no good match; record the status of each.\n- **Done when:** every no-match and stale question returns `needs_owner` with no draft, every draft cites\n  a library id, and a table shows match scores against your own judgement of the right entry."},"exercise":"**Goal:** generate the agent section of your answer library from configuration and prove it goes stale\nwhen the agent changes.\n- **Inputs:** a toy tool registry (YAML or JSON: tool, read or write, credential scope, approval\n  required), a retention config (traces, logs, memory TTLs) and a model routing config with regions.\n- **Deliver:** a script that renders library entries for the five agent questions in the table above,\n  each with owner, evidence path and review date, plus a check that fails when config changed after an\n  entry's review date.\n- **Acceptance:** adding a write tool flips \"Can the agent take actions?\" and fails the check; adding a\n  US fallback route changes the residency entry; an unchanged config passes.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a bank asks \"Are prompts or tool results ever stored outside our tenant?\" Your agent\nstreams traces to a SaaS observability vendor, caches tool results in Redis for 1 hour, and stores\nlong-term memory per user. Last quarter you told another bank \"No\". Write the answer and say what else\nyou must do.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- answers \"Yes\", per flow: traces at the vendor with its retention and region, the cache with its TTL,\n  memory with its deletion path, and states which are in the tenant;\n- names the vendor as a subprocessor and checks it is on the published list;\n- treats last quarter's \"No\" as a misstatement to correct with that customer, via legal, since it may\n  be contractual;\n- offers a fix, such as payload redaction in traces or an in-tenant trace store, and updates the library\n  entry and its owner.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Buyer: inherent-risk tiering\"] --> Q[\"Questionnaire arrives\"]\n    Q --> E[\"Send SOC 2 or ISO evidence early\"]\n    E --> M[\"Match questions to answer library\"]\n    L[(\"Answer library: owner, evidence, review date\")] --> M\n    M --> D[\"Draft answer, possibly RAG\"]\n    D --> O{\"Control owner approves?\"}\n    O -->|\"no, fix or mark gap\"| D\n    O -->|\"yes\"| S[\"Submit to buyer\"]\n    S --> F[\"Buyer scores, raises findings\"]\n    F -->|\"follow-ups\"| M\n    F --> C[\"Disposition: accept, mitigate, contract\"]\n    S -.->|\"new or changed answers\"| L","caption":"A questionnaire flows from the buyer's tiering through your answer library, where every draft (often\nfrom a RAG drafting agent) passes an accountable owner before it becomes a commitment."},"interview":[{"q":"Why do enterprises send security questionnaires, and why do the answers matter so much?","a":"- **Scale:** a buyer with thousands of vendors cannot inspect each one, so it collects standardized\n  written claims and evidence, tiered by how much risk the vendor carries.\n- **Consequence:** answers are scored into findings and are often incorporated by reference into the\n  contract, so a false answer becomes a legal problem, not just a technical one."},{"q":"A questionnaire asks whether you retain prompts. You are not sure. What do you do?","a":"- **Do not guess.** Route it to the owner of the data flow and check every place prompts can land:\n  application logs, traces, error trackers, caches, and each model provider's retention terms.\n- **Answer what you find**, with duration, access controls and deletion path, for example \"Yes, 30 days\n  for debugging, access-restricted, deletable on request\".\n- **Fix the record:** add any newly found vendor to the subprocessor list and update the library entry."},{"q":"A buyer asks \"Can your agent take actions in our systems, and how do you prevent misuse?\" How do you answer, and what must be true in the agent?","a":"- **Answer from the tool registry:** list which tools read and which write, the scope of each credential,\n  and which actions need human approval before they run.\n- **Describe the guardrails:** least-privilege tool roles, egress limits, tool allowlists, and prompt\n  injection evals with results you can share.\n- **Make it stay true:** generate this entry from config and trip the change trigger whenever a tool or\n  scope is added."},{"q":"When is pursuing SOC 2 not yet worth it?","a":"- **Low deal volume:** an early-stage vendor with few enterprise deals may get further with a strong\n  answer library, a clear data-flow diagram and a pen-test summary.\n- **Cost:** a Type II audit costs significant time and money every year and covers a period, so it pays\n  off once questionnaire volume and deal size justify it.\n- **Signal to watch:** repeated deals stalling on \"do you have a SOC 2 report?\""}],"tradeoffs":["**Templates vs custom forms.** Industry templates such as SIG or CAIQ let you answer once and reuse widely, but buyers with their own frameworks still send custom forms, so the library must map to both.","**Independent reports vs cost.** Reports like SOC 2 shortcut many questions and carry weight, but cost significant time and money annually. For an early-stage vendor with few enterprise deals, a strong library may suffice first.","**Specific answers vs commitments.** Detailed answers build trust and reduce follow-ups, but each detail is a commitment. Where the system changes often, describe the control and its guarantee rather than its exact implementation. Agent tool lists change often, so describe the permission model.","**AI drafting vs accuracy.** Drafts from the library save hours per questionnaire, but only work when every generated answer is reviewed by the owner of the underlying control."],"pitfalls":["**Answering aspirationally to unblock a deal.** Symptom: a later audit or incident shows the contract-referenced answer is false, turning a technical gap into a legal one. Diagnose: every answer should link to evidence; list entries with none. Fix: answer \"Partially\" with a dated plan.","**Forgetting subprocessors in the data-flow story**, especially model providers and observability tools. Symptom: a buyer finds an unlisted vendor in your trace exports or DNS logs. Diagnose: diff the external domains in egress and DNS logs, and vendor billing, against the published list.","**Letting answers diverge across customers.** Symptom: two customers compare notes, or a renewal answers differently, prompting a full re-review. Diagnose: search sent questionnaires for answers not traceable to a library id. Fix: answer only from the library.","**Never revisiting answers after architecture changes.** Symptom: \"data never leaves the EU\" after someone added a US-hosted model fallback. Cause: no link from code changes to the library. Diagnose: compare model routing config and regions with residency entries. Fix: the pull-request trigger.","**Saying \"the agent is read-only\" after adding a write tool.** Symptom: a buyer's pilot logs show the agent creating tickets or sending email. Fix: derive the answer from the tool registry.","**Attaching sensitive evidence without controls**, such as a full pen-test report with unfixed findings or a detailed network diagram on a portal outside an NDA. Symptom: your security detail circulates among strangers. Fix: summaries and attestation letters by default, full reports under NDA.","**Treating \"not applicable\" as a shortcut.** Symptom: reviewer follow-ups asking why, slower than a one-line justified answer."],"when":"- **Prepare before the first deal.** Every sale to a regulated or large enterprise passes through one:\n  write the answer library, the subprocessor list, a data-flow diagram and the AI data-use statement\n  while the architecture is fresh.\n- **Pursue SOC 2 or ISO 27001** when the volume of questionnaires and deal size justify the audit cost.\n- **Route through control owners**, not the account team alone. For the agent section, the owner is the\n  team that owns the tool registry and traces.\n- **Measure:** turnaround time, follow-up rounds and the number of answers changed after engineering review.\n- **Interviews (FDE and architect):** expect \"how do you answer a question where the honest answer is\n  no?\" The answer: honestly, with compensating controls and a dated plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns accurate answers about the systems they build, such as retention in traces, embedding deletion and model routing, and flags when a change invalidates a published answer.","architect":"Designs data flows so the answers are good ones, for example a no-retention model path or a region-pinned tier, and keeps the authoritative data-flow diagram the answers rest on.","fde":"Shepherds the customer's questionnaire through internal owners, explains gaps and compensating controls on calls, and feeds recurring asks back to product as roadmap items."},"check":{"q":"A prospect's questionnaire asks \"Does any customer data leave the EU?\" Your app runs in an EU\nregion, but its fallback model route goes to a US-hosted provider when the EU model is rate-limited, and\nyour error tracker is US-hosted. How do you answer, and what do you do beyond answering?","a":"The honest answer is \"Yes, in specific cases,\" followed by each flow: the fallback route may send\nprompts to a US region when the EU model is throttled, and error events may contain request fragments sent\nto a US-hosted tracker. Name the subprocessors, the transfer mechanism in the DPA, and any mitigations such\nas scrubbing payloads from error events. Beyond the answer, offer a configuration that keeps data in the EU:\ndisable cross-region fallback for this tenant, accepting more throttling or degraded responses, and switch\nthe tracker to an EU instance or strip content. Then update the answer library and subprocessor list so the\nnext questionnaire is correct, and tell engineering the routing change has compliance consequences."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does sending a SOC 2 Type II report early often shorten a security questionnaire?","options":["It certifies the product is free of vulnerabilities","It is an independent auditor's opinion that controls operated over a period, which buyers accept in place of many individual answers","It replaces the need for a data processing agreement","It proves compliance with HIPAA and GDPR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Type II report gives third-party assurance on control operation over time, so buyers can mark whole domains as covered. It is not a vulnerability guarantee, a legal agreement or proof of any regulation."},{"q":"The honest answer to \"Do you support customer-managed encryption keys?\" is \"only on our dedicated tier,\" and this buyer is on the shared tier. What is the best response?","options":["Answer \"Yes\", since the product supports the feature","Answer \"Not applicable\", since this buyer did not purchase that tier","Answer \"Partially\", explain which tier offers it and what protects data on the shared tier","Leave the question blank and raise it on a call later"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A scoped, accurate answer with the alternative is both honest and useful. \"Yes\" would misstate this buyer's protection, and \"not applicable\" is wrong because the question applies to them."},{"q":"Your team uses an LLM over the answer library to draft questionnaire responses. What control matters most?","options":["Using the largest available model to reduce errors","Letting the model answer only yes/no questions","Re-running the model until two drafts agree","Requiring the owner of each underlying control to approve the drafted answer before it is sent"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Retrieval speeds drafting, but answers are commitments, and a fluent wrong answer is a misrepresentation. Model size and agreement between drafts do not establish truth; an accountable owner's review does."}]},{"key":"deployment/security-and-architecture-reviews","sources":["Adam Shostack, 2014 — Threat Modeling: Designing for Security","Official docs: OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications","Official docs: NIST AI Risk Management Framework"],"why":"- **A questionnaire captures claims; a review tests the design.** It cannot tell the buyer how your\n  system fits theirs: which networks it touches, what identity reads their SharePoint, whether an agent\n  could email a customer, where a prompt containing patient data is logged.\n- **Those answers depend on the deployment**, so large organizations route new systems through an\n  architecture review board and a security review before production access is granted.\n- **Without a review, problems surface in the worst places:** an over-privileged service account found in\n  an incident, a path to an unapproved region found by an auditor, a record-modifying tool found by a\n  user. Fixing design after deployment costs far more than changing a diagram.\n- **Marketing slides fail.** Reviewers want every data store, boundary crossing and credential; vague\n  answers produce open actions and a second meeting weeks later.\n- **AI makes it harder.** Reviewers often meet LLM risks for the first time and need you to explain how\n  the model can be manipulated and what bounds the damage; bringing that unprompted earns trust.","how":"### The typical flow\n1. **Intake.** The sponsor files a request with a system description, data classification and owners.\n   Data classification sets the depth: a system touching regulated personal data gets more scrutiny than\n   an internal FAQ bot. An agent that takes actions usually lands in a deeper tier than one that only\n   answers.\n2. **Pre-read package.** You submit documents in advance, because reviewers want to come with questions,\n   not discover the design in the meeting.\n3. **Review session.** Reviewers walk the diagram, probe boundaries and ask \"what happens if\" questions.\n4. **Findings.** Each gets a severity, an owner and an outcome: fix before go-live, fix by a date, accept\n   the risk with a named approver, or mitigate with a compensating control.\n5. **Decision.** Approve, approve with conditions, or reject; conditions are tracked to closure, usually\n   in the customer's risk register.\n\n### What the pre-read contains\n- **Architecture diagram** at container level (C4-style is common): every service, data store, external\n  dependency and trust boundary, including model providers and observability tools.\n- **Data flow diagram** for each sensitive flow: what data, from where, to where, encrypted how, retained\n  how long. Reviewers scrutinize this most, because breaches follow data. For an agent, a tool result is\n  a data flow too.\n- **Identity and access:** how users authenticate (their SSO), how services authenticate to each other\n  and to customer systems, which permissions each role and service account has, and why. List each agent\n  tool's credential separately.\n- **Network:** ingress and egress paths, private endpoints, VPNs, what is publicly reachable.\n- **Encryption and keys:** in transit, at rest, who holds keys, whether customer-managed keys are used.\n- **Logging and monitoring:** what is logged, where, for how long, whether prompts or outputs are included.\n- **Threat model:** for example STRIDE per boundary, plus AI-specific threats from the OWASP LLM list\n  (2025 edition):\n  prompt injection (direct and via retrieved documents), sensitive information disclosure, excessive\n  agency of tools, improper output handling (called insecure output handling in the 2023 list), vector\n  and embedding weaknesses, and supply chain of models and datasets.\n- **Evidence:** penetration test summary, vulnerability scanning, SBOM, SOC 2 or ISO reports, plus agent\n  evals and red-team results for injection.\n\n### Applying STRIDE at a boundary\nA threat model is the same six questions asked wherever data or control crosses a trust boundary. It\nworks *because* boundaries are finite and enumerable, while \"think of attacks\" is not. In an agent, the critical boundaries are retrieved text entering the prompt and model\noutput becoming a tool call.\n\n| Letter | Question | At \"retrieved document text enters the model prompt\" |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S**poofing | can someone pretend to be a legitimate caller? | (ask per caller of the retrieval API) |\n| **T**ampering | can data be altered in transit or at rest? | a planted instruction in a document (indirect prompt injection) |\n| **R**epudiation | could an actor deny an action because nothing recorded it? | an action with no log tying it to a user and a source document |\n| **I**nformation disclosure | can data reach someone not entitled to it? | the model quoting a passage the user may not see |\n| **D**enial of service | can the path be exhausted? | (ask per path: index size, prompt length) |\n| **E**levation of privilege | can a caller gain rights it was not given? | the injected instruction causing a tool call with the agent's broader credentials |\n\nEach answer points to a control, which is exactly what the reviewer wants to see written down.\n\n### The architecture half\nAn architecture review board asks questions a security reviewer may not:\n- **Fit:** does the design use approved technologies and integration patterns, or introduce a new\n  database the customer's operations team has never run?\n- **Resilience:** what are the availability target and the recovery point and time objectives, and does\n  the design meet them?\n- **Operations:** who operates it, and does its monitoring land in their tooling?\n- **Cost:** what does it cost at expected volume, and how does that scale? Agents make several model\n  calls per task, so cost per task, not per call, is the number to bring.\n- **Exit:** can data and indexes be exported if the vendor is replaced?\n\nThese exist *because* the customer will live with the system long after the project team moves on, so fit\nwith their estate matters as much as internal elegance.\n\n### AI-specific questions to prepare for\n| Reviewer asks | Prepared control |\n|---|---|\n| Can a document in the index instruct the model to leak other documents? | permission-filtered retrieval, output validation |\n| Does retrieval respect the user's permissions in the source system? | permission-filtered retrieval |\n| Which tools can the agent call, with whose credentials, and which actions need human approval? | least-privilege tool credentials, approval gates |\n| What happens when the model is wrong, and how would you know? | evaluation results and monitoring |\n| Can a user's data be deleted from indexes, caches and fine-tuned weights? | documented deletion path per store |\n\n### Your own reviews\n- **Run the same process internally first.** A peer architect and a security engineer catch the obvious\n  findings cheaply.\n- **Record decisions as ADRs** so the reasoning survives staff changes.","example":"### Worked scenario: a bank's review of a policy assistant\nAn FDE prepares a bank's architecture review for an internal-policy assistant that retrieves from\nSharePoint and can draft, but not send, emails. The pre-read has a container diagram, two data-flow\ndiagrams (indexing and query), an access matrix and a threat model. The review produces five findings:\n\n1. **High:** the indexing service account can read all SharePoint sites, so retrieval would ignore\n   per-user permissions.\n   - Fix before go-live: each chunk carries the source document's access list captured at index time;\n     retrieval filters by the user's group memberships synced from the identity provider; the indexer's\n     scope is reduced to the approved site collections.\n   - Residual risk, noted in the finding, is staleness: a permission revoked in SharePoint still applies\n     in the index until the next sync, so the sync interval becomes a documented, agreed number.\n2. **High:** full prompts and retrieved passages are written to the vendor-hosted trace store. Fix before\n   go-live: traces stay in the bank's log platform; vendor telemetry receives only metadata.\n3. **Medium:** indirect prompt injection through a malicious document could make the assistant produce a\n   misleading draft. Mitigation accepted: drafts are never auto-sent, the UI marks generated text, and\n   red-team results on injection are attached as evidence.\n4. **Medium:** no documented response if the model provider changes the model version. Fix by date: pin\n   the model version and add a regression eval gate before upgrades.\n5. **Low:** the internal ingress still accepts TLS 1.2 with legacy CBC-mode cipher suites, while the\n   bank's standard requires AEAD-only suites. Risk accepted by the bank's security lead with a review in\n   six months.\n\n| Finding | Severity | Outcome | Closed by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 Indexer reads all sites | High | fix before go-live | two weeks, with a permission-filter test |\n| 2 Prompts in vendor traces | High | fix before go-live | two weeks, with a log-flow screenshot |\n| 3 Indirect injection in drafts | Medium | mitigation accepted | red-team evidence |\n| 4 Unpinned model version | Medium | fix by date | condition on approval |\n| 5 TLS 1.2 CBC suites | Low | risk accepted | review in six months, condition on approval |\n\nThe board approves with conditions on findings 4 and 5, tracked in the bank's risk register.\n\n### Evidence test for finding 1: user A cannot retrieve user B's restricted file\nThe reviewer wants a test, not a promise. This pure-Python sketch shows the shape: filter by the\ncaller's groups before ranking, then assert on the restricted document.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Evidence for finding 1: retrieval honours source-system permissions.\"\"\"\nCHUNKS = [\n    {\"doc\": \"travel-policy.docx\", \"text\": \"Economy class for flights under 6 hours\", \"acl\": {\"all-staff\"}},\n    {\"doc\": \"layoffs-2026.docx\", \"text\": \"Restructuring plan for the retail unit\", \"acl\": {\"hr-leads\"}},\n]\nGROUPS = {\"user_a\": {\"all-staff\"}, \"user_b\": {\"all-staff\", \"hr-leads\"}}   # synced from the IdP\n\ndef retrieve(user, query, chunks=CHUNKS):\n    allowed = GROUPS.get(user, set())\n    visible = [c for c in chunks if c[\"acl\"] & allowed]                 # filter BEFORE ranking\n    return [c[\"doc\"] for c in visible if any(w in c[\"text\"].lower() for w in query.lower().split())]\n\ndef test_user_a_cannot_retrieve_restricted_file():\n    assert \"layoffs-2026.docx\" not in retrieve(\"user_a\", \"restructuring plan\")\n\ndef test_user_b_can_retrieve_it():\n    assert retrieve(\"user_b\", \"restructuring plan\") == [\"layoffs-2026.docx\"]\n\ndef test_unknown_user_gets_nothing():\n    assert retrieve(\"mallory\", \"policy plan\") == []\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    for t in (test_user_a_cannot_retrieve_restricted_file, test_user_b_can_retrieve_it,\n              test_unknown_user_gets_nothing):\n        t(); print(\"pass\", t.__name__)\n```\n\n- **Against the real system**, run the same three assertions through the deployed retrieval API with two\n  test identities, and attach the run output to the finding.\n\n### Evidence test for excessive agency: the email tool enforces its limits in code\nThe reviewer's question \"what stops the agent emailing our customer list outside?\" is answered by a tool\nwhose limits do not depend on the prompt. The test feeds it what an injected instruction would ask for.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"The agent's email tool: drafts only, internal recipients only, enforced in code.\"\"\"\nINTERNAL_DOMAINS = {\"bank.example\"}\n\nclass ToolDenied(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef create_email_draft(to: list[str], subject: str, body: str, audit: list) -> dict:\n    outside = [a for a in to if a.rsplit(\"@\", 1)[-1].lower() not in INTERNAL_DOMAINS]\n    if outside:\n        audit.append({\"tool\": \"email\", \"denied\": outside})\n        raise ToolDenied(f\"recipients outside approved domains: {outside}\")\n    audit.append({\"tool\": \"email\", \"draft_to\": to})\n    return {\"status\": \"draft\", \"to\": to}       # there is no send function for the agent to call\n\ndef test_injected_external_recipient_is_denied():\n    audit = []\n    try:\n        create_email_draft([\"attacker@evil.example\"], \"list\", \"all customers\", audit)\n        assert False, \"should have been denied\"\n    except ToolDenied:\n        assert audit[-1][\"denied\"] == [\"attacker@evil.example\"]\n\ndef test_internal_draft_allowed_and_logged():\n    audit = []\n    assert create_email_draft([\"ops@bank.example\"], \"hi\", \"draft\", audit)[\"status\"] == \"draft\"\n    assert audit[-1][\"draft_to\"] == [\"ops@bank.example\"]\n```\n\n- **Why this convinces a reviewer.** It shows the control holds even when the model is fully\n  compromised, and the audit entry answers the repudiation question.","patterns":["**Reusable pre-read template.** Keep a standard package (container diagram, one DFD per sensitive flow, access matrix, STRIDE table per boundary, AI threats from the OWASP LLM list, evidence index) and update it per deployment. Track high findings per review; they should fall as the template matures.","**Tool inventory in the pre-read.** Generate a table of every agent tool (read or write, credential, scope, approval required) from the tool registry, so the excessive-agency discussion starts from facts. Regenerate it before each review.","**Diagram checked against production.** Reconcile it with the deployed inventory (resource listings, egress destinations) before each review, because a missing service destroys reviewer confidence.","**Findings as tickets with dates and expiring acceptances.** Every finding gets an owner, severity, outcome and due date in the tracker, mirrored to the customer's risk register. Each accepted risk records the approver and a review date, such as the six-month TLS review above. Monitor the percentage closed by due date and acceptances past their date.","**Change triggers for re-review.** New data sources, new agent tools, new model providers and new regions require a delta review before release; add the check to the release checklist.","**Evidence tests in CI.** Keep the permission-filter, tool-limit and log-destination checks as automated tests, plus a prompt-injection eval set, so the evidence stays true after go-live."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Reviewers ask what an agent can do, with whose credentials, and what\n  stops a planted instruction in a retrieved document from turning into a tool call. The answer has to\n  rest on capability limits you built, not on the model obeying its prompt.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool permission scopes, approval gates on consequential actions,\n  permission-filtered retrieval, trace destinations, model version pinning, and the evals and red-team\n  results you attach as evidence.\n- **What you control.** Designing tools so the threat model is short (draft-only email, internal\n  recipients enforced in code, read-only by default), and keeping evidence tests in CI so they stay true.\n- **What you can skip.** The governance mechanics of a customer's review board. Know the flow (intake,\n  pre-read, session, findings, decision) and what each finding outcome means.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Getting an enterprise agent to production.** A bank's review of a SharePoint-backed assistant ends\n  with dated findings on permission filtering and trace destinations.\n- **Delta reviews for new tools.** Each new write tool, model provider or region triggers a short review\n  with its own threat rows and tests.\n- **Internal design reviews of agent architectures.** A peer architect and a security engineer walk the\n  agent's boundaries before the first line of tool code.\n- **Answering excessive-agency questions with tests.** Tool limits enforced in code, plus injection evals,\n  replace \"the prompt tells it not to\".","mini_project":"**Assemble a review pre-read for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** produce the evidence pack a security reviewer would accept for a RAG agent with one draft-only\n  email tool.\n- **Steps:** draw the container diagram and one data-flow diagram; write the STRIDE table for \"retrieved\n  text enters the prompt\" and \"model output becomes a tool call\"; implement the retrieval and email-tool\n  evidence tests above against your agent; add five indirect-injection documents to your index and record\n  what the agent attempts.\n- **Done when:** every STRIDE row points to a control, both evidence tests pass in CI, the injection run\n  shows every external-recipient attempt denied and logged, and the pack fits on four pages."},"exercise":"**Goal:** prepare a delta review for adding a write tool (\"update ticket status\") to an approved RAG\nagent.\n- **Inputs:** your mini-project agent (or a stub with one read tool) and a ticket API stub.\n- **Deliver:** the new DFD arrow and credential, STRIDE rows for \"model output becomes this tool call\",\n  a tool inventory generated from the registry, and pytest evidence tests for the tool's limits.\n- **Acceptance:** every row maps to a control with a test or a named owner; the tests fail when you\n  delete the code-level check and pass with it; out-of-scope calls appear in the audit log.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** six months after approval, the bank's policy assistant gains a \"book meeting room\" tool\nand a fallback to a second model provider in another region. Product wants to ship next week and says\n\"it is the same system\". Decide what review is needed, list the new threats, and name the evidence you\nwould bring.\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- calls for a delta review, not a full one, because both changes alter data flows the approval rested\n  on;\n- adds threat rows: an injected booking (elevation), and prompts reaching an undisclosed provider and\n  region (disclosure);\n- proposes controls: a scoped booking role with limits enforced in code and logged per user, and the\n  fallback disabled or the provider added to the DFD and subprocessor list, with the bank notified;\n- brings tests calling the tool with out-of-scope arguments, a routing config excerpt and injection eval\n  results.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Intake: description, data class, owners\"] --> P[\"Pre-read: diagrams, DFDs, threat model, evidence\"]\n    P --> S[\"Review session: walk boundaries\"]\n    S --> F[\"Findings with severity and owner\"]\n    F --> O{\"Outcome per finding\"}\n    O -->|\"fix before go-live\"| X[\"Engineering fix plus evidence\"]\n    O -->|\"fix by date\"| R[(\"Customer risk register\")]\n    O -->|\"accept or mitigate\"| R\n    X --> D{\"Board decision\"}\n    R --> D\n    D -->|\"reject\"| P\n    D -->|\"approve with conditions\"| G[\"Go-live, conditions tracked\"]","caption":"The review is a loop: findings return to the agent's design until each one has a fix, a mitigation or a\nnamed acceptance."},"interview":[{"q":"How is a security review different from a security questionnaire?","a":"- **Questionnaire:** standardized written claims about your controls, largely independent of how you are\n  deployed.\n- **Review:** an examination of this specific deployment's design: which networks it touches, which\n  identities it uses, where sensitive data flows and is logged, and what an agent can do.\n- **So prepare differently:** a library answers questionnaires; a review needs accurate diagrams, data\n  flows, a threat model and evidence for this system."},{"q":"Walk me through threat-modelling one boundary in a RAG agent.","a":"- **Pick the boundary:** \"retrieved document text enters the model prompt\".\n- **Ask STRIDE:** tampering is a planted instruction; information disclosure is quoting a passage the user\n  may not see; elevation is an injected tool call with the agent's credentials; repudiation is an\n  unlogged action.\n- **Map each to a control:** permission-filtered retrieval, least-privilege tools, approval gates, and\n  logs tying actions to user and source document."},{"q":"You are adding a write tool (say, updating CRM records) to an approved agent. What do you bring to the delta review?","a":"- **The new data flow and credential:** what the tool writes, with which scoped role, and why read-only\n  was not enough.\n- **The new threat rows:** an injected instruction triggering a write, and an unlogged change; with\n  controls such as an approval gate, field-level limits and an audit log keyed to user and trace.\n- **Evidence:** tests showing denied out-of-scope writes, and injection eval results for the new tool."},{"q":"A reviewer raises a finding you think is overstated. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Do not argue it away.** Understand the risk they see and restate it in your own words.\n- **Offer options:** a compensating control, a dated fix, or a formal acceptance by their named approver\n  with a review date.\n- **Bring evidence** (a test, a config excerpt) rather than assertions; reviewers escalate when they\n  feel resisted."},{"q":"When is a full architecture review overkill?","a":"- **Low-risk internal tools** with no sensitive data, identity integrations or actions: a short template\n  review is proportionate; small changes inside an approved design need only a delta review.\n- **Not overkill** when an agent gains a new tool, model provider or region, because those change the\n  data flows the original approval rested on."}],"tradeoffs":["**Early vs late engagement.** Engaging reviewers at design stage finds structural problems while they are cheap, but the design may change and need re-review. Late engagement saves meetings but risks findings that force a redesign, such as removing a write tool the agent's main flow depends on.","**Thorough pre-read vs preparation time.** It shortens the meeting and follow-ups, but takes days to prepare; for a low-risk internal tool, a short template is proportionate.","**Risk acceptance vs invisible debt.** Accepting risks with a named approver keeps launches moving, but accumulated accepted risks become invisible debt unless they carry expiry dates and are re-reviewed.","**Frameworks vs deployment specifics.** STRIDE and the OWASP LLM list give reviewers shared vocabulary, but checklists miss deployment-specific risks; the data-flow walkthrough is what finds those.","**Agent capability vs review depth.** Each write tool adds value and a threat-model row. Starting read-only with draft-only actions passes review faster, and write tools can follow as delta reviews."],"pitfalls":["**A diagram that does not match production.** Symptom: a reviewer asks about a service the diagram omits, and the review loses confidence in everything else. Diagnose: diff the diagram's components against cloud resource listings and egress destinations. Fix: regenerate it before each review.","**Omitting the model provider and observability vendors from data flows.** Symptom: a high-severity finding for undisclosed data transfer, often from a network-egress question. Diagnose: list every external host in egress logs and trace exporter config, and map each to a DFD arrow.","**Relying on the system prompt as the control.** Symptom: the reviewer asks what happens if the prompt is overridden and there is no answer. Diagnose: call each write tool directly with out-of-scope arguments, bypassing the model. Fix: limits in tool code and credentials, with tests.","**Agreeing to findings without owners and dates.** Symptom: findings reappear next review or block go-live late. Diagnose: count open findings lacking an owner or due date.","**Treating the review as a one-time gate.** Symptom: a new agent tool or a cross-region fallback ships unreviewed and an audit flags it. Diagnose: compare the tool registry and routing config with the last approved pre-read. Fix: release-checklist triggers.","**Arguing every finding.** Symptom: a reviewer escalates to a reject, when a compensating control or dated fix would have closed it."],"when":"- **Expect a customer review** for any system touching confidential or regulated data, integrating with\n  identity or internal systems, or taking actions.\n- **Run your own internal review** for every new system and for significant changes: new data sources,\n  new tools for an agent, new model providers, new regions.\n- **Scale the depth** to data classification and blast radius; an agent's blast radius is the union of\n  its tools' permissions.\n- **Measure:** time from intake to approval, the number of high findings per review (it should fall as\n  your template matures) and the percentage of findings closed by their due date.\n- **Interviews:** a strong candidate describes the pre-read package and names AI-specific threats with\n  concrete controls, rather than claiming the system is secure.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence reviewers ask for, such as a test proving permission-filtered retrieval and log configuration excerpts, and implements fixes against named findings.","architect":"Owns the diagrams, data flows, threat model and ADRs, presents the design, and negotiates findings into fixes, mitigations or formally accepted risks with expiry dates.","fde":"Navigates the customer's review process, books the right reviewers, translates their concerns into engineering tickets and keeps the findings tracker moving until approval."},"check":{"q":"In a customer's review, the security architect asks: \"If someone uploads a document containing\ninstructions to the model, what stops your agent from emailing our customer list to an outside address?\"\nWhat is a strong answer?","a":"Acknowledge that indirect prompt injection cannot be fully prevented at the model level, then show\nlayered controls that bound impact. The agent's email tool can only create drafts, so a human must send;\nrecipients are restricted to internal domains by the tool, not the prompt; the agent's credentials cannot\nread the customer list because retrieval is filtered by the requesting user's permissions and the CRM is not\nconnected; outputs are validated and links stripped; and actions are logged for audit. Add evidence: red-team\nresults for injection attempts and the tool's permission policy. Then offer monitoring and an alert on\nanomalous tool use. The key point is that safety rests on capability limits, not on the model obeying."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which pre-read document do reviewers usually scrutinize most closely, and why?","options":["The product roadmap, because it shows future security features","The data-flow diagrams, because they show what sensitive data crosses which boundaries and where it is stored","The penetration test report, because it proves there are no vulnerabilities","The pricing sheet, because it determines the review tier"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Breaches and compliance problems follow data, so reviewers trace each sensitive flow across trust boundaries. A penetration test is useful evidence but shows only what testers found, not the absence of flaws."},{"q":"A review finding says the indexing service account can read every SharePoint site. Why is this rated high for a RAG assistant?","options":["Because SharePoint APIs are rate-limited","Because broad read access slows down indexing","Because the account password might expire","Because without per-user permission filtering at retrieval, any user could receive content they are not entitled to read"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The assistant would answer from documents the requesting user cannot access, bypassing the source system's permissions. The fix is permission-aware retrieval and a narrower indexing scope, not tuning throughput."},{"q":"A finding cannot be fixed before launch. What is the most appropriate way to proceed?","options":["Remove it from the findings list since it cannot be fixed now","Launch and revisit it if an incident occurs","Record a risk acceptance or compensating control with a named approver and a review or fix date","Ask the reviewer to downgrade the severity so it does not block"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Formal acceptance makes the decision visible and owned, and a date ensures it is revisited. Deleting or downgrading the finding hides the risk, which is what the review exists to prevent."}]},{"key":"deployment/compliance-constraints","sources":["Official docs: EU General Data Protection Regulation (Regulation (EU) 2016/679)","Official docs: US HHS — HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules","Official docs: EU Artificial Intelligence Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689)","Official docs: AICPA — SOC 2"],"why":"- **Finished is not deployable.** A hospital cannot send patient notes to a model provider that has not\n  signed a business associate agreement. A European insurer may need contractual safeguards before\n  prompts with customer data leave the EU. A defense contractor's data may be export-controlled, so only\n  certain personnel may touch the system.\n- **These are obligations, not preferences.** Breach means fines, lost certifications, voided contracts\n  or worse.\n- **\"Do compliance at the end\" fails** because the requirements are architectural (see the table below).\n  Discovering them after the build means rework, sometimes a different deployment model entirely, such\n  as moving from SaaS into the customer's VPC.\n- **The useful skill is translation:** hearing \"we are subject to HIPAA and SOC 2\" and turning it into a\n  short list of concrete constraints the design must satisfy and the evidence it must produce.\n\n| Requirement | What it decides in the architecture |\n|---|---|\n| Data residency | which regions and model endpoints you may use |\n| No-training, limited retention | which model providers qualify |\n| Erasure | whether you may fine-tune on customer data, since deleting a record from model weights is not practical |\n| Audit | what your logs must contain |\n| Minimization | what your logs may contain |\n| Agent memory and checkpoints | how long conversation state may persist, and how it is erased |","how":"### From obligation to evidence\nEvery requirement flows through the same chain:\n\n```\nobligation (law, contract clause, framework criterion)\n  → constraint on the system\n  → control that enforces it\n  → evidence that the control works\n```\n\nAuditors and reviewers check the last link, so design controls that produce evidence naturally:\nconfiguration in code, logs, test results.\n\n### The regimes you will meet most\n**GDPR (EU personal data).**\n- **Core duties:** a lawful basis, purpose limitation and data minimization, data processing agreements\n  with processors, honoring rights such as erasure and access, and safeguards for transfers outside the\n  EEA (adequacy decisions or mechanisms such as standard contractual clauses).\n- **Residency is usually contractual.** GDPR does not strictly mandate EU hosting, but many customers\n  impose EU residency contractually because it is the simplest way to stay safe.\n- **Controller and processor.** The customer is usually the **controller** and you the **processor**, so\n  you act only on their documented instructions, and your subprocessors (model, cloud, tracing) need\n  their approval. A tool that calls an outside API with customer data adds a subprocessor too.\n- **Remote access counts.** European regulators generally treat *remote access* from outside the EEA,\n  such as a US support engineer opening an EU tenant's traces, as a transfer. Residency is about who can\n  read the data, not only where disks sit.\n\n**HIPAA (US health data).**\n- **BAA required.** Covered entities may share protected health information with a vendor only under a\n  business associate agreement, and that obligation flows down to the vendor's subprocessors, including\n  model and cloud providers.\n- **Security Rule safeguards** cover access control, audit controls and transmission security.\n\n**Other regimes.**\n\n| Regime | What it is | Typical constraint |\n|---|---|---|\n| SOC 2, ISO/IEC 27001 | assurance frameworks customers require of vendors, not laws | change management, access reviews, logging, incident response, vendor management |\n| PCI DSS | payment cards; any system that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data is in scope | keep card data out of prompts and logs so the AI system stays out of scope |\n| FedRAMP | US federal cloud use requires FedRAMP-authorized services at an appropriate impact level | approved services only |\n| Export control (for example ITAR) | restricts who may access the data | often pushes deployments on-prem or air-gapped |\n| EU AI Act | risk-based regulation phasing in obligations over several years | see below |\n| Sector rules | financial services model risk management (validation, monitoring, documentation); record-keeping rules | minimum retention, model documentation |\n\n**EU AI Act.** Certain practices are prohibited; high-risk systems carry requirements for risk\nmanagement, data governance, logging, human oversight and transparency; general-purpose model providers\nhave their own duties. Which tier a use case falls into is a legal determination, so involve counsel.\n\n### The constraint categories\nTranslate any regime into these questions:\n\n| Category | Question | Agent example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Where** | where may data be processed and stored? | model region, tool backends, trace store |\n| **Who** | who may access it, including vendors and personnel? | support engineers reading traces |\n| **What** | what data may enter prompts, logs and indexes? | card numbers in tool results |\n| **How long** | how long must or may it be kept? | memory and checkpoint TTLs |\n| **How** | how must it be protected (encryption, customer-managed keys)? | encrypted memory store |\n| **What proof** | which audit trails and change records must exist? | who approved which agent action |\n| **AI-specific** | no training use, human oversight, disclosure that users are talking to AI | approval gate before writes |\n\n### Enforce at a chokepoint, check in the pipeline\nA constraint written only in a policy document is broken by the first engineer who adds a fallback\nregion. So enforce each constraint where every request must pass, and check it where every change must pass:\n1. **Runtime chokepoint.** Route all model calls through one gateway holding a per-tenant policy (allowed\n   providers, allowed regions, whether content may be logged) that refuses anything else. It is a\n   chokepoint *because* a single enforcement point cannot be bypassed by a new code path the way\n   scattered checks can, and its configuration plus its refusal logs are the evidence. For agents, every\n   step of every loop passes it, including sub-agents and retries.\n2. **Change-time check.** Run policy checks on infrastructure plans (for example, fail any Terraform plan\n   that creates storage outside approved regions or a log sink without a retention setting), and require\n   review when a new subprocessor appears in the dependency or egress list. This catches the violation\n   before production, which is when it is cheapest.\n3. **Periodic proof.** Access reviews; deletion tests that confirm an erased record is gone from the\n   database, the vector index and the caches (and agent memory and checkpoints); and a reconciliation of\n   actual egress against the declared subprocessor list.\n\n### Constraints conflict\n| Conflict | Explicit resolution |\n|---|---|\n| Audit retention vs erasure | keep the audit record of actions; keep content where it can be deleted |\n| Debug logging vs minimization | log metadata and hashes rather than content |\n| Cross-region fallback vs residency | in-region fallback or a degraded mode |\n| Fine-tuning on customer data vs deletion | keep customer data in retrieval indexes (which can be deleted) instead of in fine-tuned weights |\n| Long-term agent memory vs retention limits | TTL on memory entries, and erasure that reaches memory |","example":"### Worked scenario: a US hospital's note-summarizing assistant\nCustomer requirements: HIPAA, the vendor's SOC 2 Type II, and their own policy of seven-year audit\nretention. The translation into constraints and controls:\n\n1. **BAA chain.** Notes are PHI, so the vendor signs a BAA with the hospital, and every subprocessor that\n   sees PHI must be covered too: the cloud provider and the model provider. The model provider's offering\n   is checked for BAA eligibility; a provider without one is excluded, whatever its quality.\n2. **Retention and training.** The model API path must not retain prompts beyond what the BAA allows and\n   must not train on them. Where that cannot be confirmed, the design switches to a self-hosted\n   open-weight model in the hospital's VPC.\n3. **Logs.** Debug traces originally captured full prompts. Under minimum-necessary handling, traces now\n   keep request IDs, token counts, latency and a hash of the input; full content is stored only in the\n   hospital's own audit store, with access logged.\n4. **Audit.** Seven-year retention applies to the record of who generated which summary and when, not to\n   the vendor's debug traces, which are kept for 30 days. Separating the two resolves the conflict\n   between audit and minimization.\n5. **Human oversight.** Summaries are drafts a clinician must approve before they enter the record; the\n   approval event is part of the audit trail.\n6. **Evidence pack.** BAA list, data-flow diagram, retention configuration in IaC, access review records,\n   and the SOC 2 report, ready for the hospital's security review.\n\n| Store | Contents | Retention | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hospital audit store | who generated which summary, when, approval event, full content | seven years | hospital |\n| Vendor debug traces | request ID, token counts, latency, input hash | 30 days | vendor |\n\nThe result changed two design decisions (model provider selection and log content) before any code shipped.\n\n### A per-tenant policy at the model gateway\nThe runtime chokepoint in miniature: deny by default, allow only listed provider and region pairs, and\nlog a hash instead of content unless the tenant's policy allows content. An agent framework calls\n`route` once per step.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Per-tenant compliance policy enforced at the model gateway (the chokepoint).\"\"\"\nimport hashlib, json, time, uuid\n\nPOLICIES = {\n    \"eu-insurer\": {\"providers\": {\"provider-a\"}, \"regions\": {\"eu-west-1\", \"eu-central-1\"},\n                   \"log_content\": False},\n    \"us-hospital\": {\"providers\": {\"provider-b\"}, \"regions\": {\"us-east-1\"},      # BAA-covered only\n                    \"log_content\": False},\n}\n\nclass PolicyViolation(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef route(tenant, provider, region, prompt):\n    policy = POLICIES.get(tenant)\n    if policy is None:\n        raise PolicyViolation(f\"no policy for tenant {tenant}: deny by default\")\n    if provider not in policy[\"providers\"] or region not in policy[\"regions\"]:\n        raise PolicyViolation(f\"{tenant}: {provider}/{region} not allowed\")\n    trace = {\"request_id\": str(uuid.uuid4()), \"tenant\": tenant, \"provider\": provider,\n             \"region\": region, \"ts\": int(time.time()),\n             \"input_sha256\": hashlib.sha256(prompt.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]}\n    if policy[\"log_content\"]:\n        trace[\"prompt\"] = prompt\n    return trace          # the call to the provider would happen here\n\nprint(json.dumps(route(\"eu-insurer\", \"provider-a\", \"eu-west-1\", \"Claim 4411 for J. Doe\"))[:120])\nfor args in ((\"eu-insurer\", \"provider-a\", \"us-east-1\"), (\"unknown\", \"provider-a\", \"eu-west-1\")):\n    try:\n        route(*args, prompt=\"x\")\n    except PolicyViolation as e:\n        print(\"REFUSED:\", e)          # refusal logs are compliance evidence\n# REFUSED: eu-insurer: provider-a/us-east-1 not allowed\n# REFUSED: no policy for tenant unknown: deny by default\n```\n\n- **Hash caveat.** A plain hash of a short, guessable input can be reversed by guessing; in production\n  use a keyed hash (HMAC with a secret) so the trace still correlates requests without exposing content.\n\n### Erasure that reaches every agent store\nAn agent leaves copies of a person's data in more places than a CRUD app. The erasure job deletes from\neach store and then verifies, and the verification output is the evidence.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Erase one data subject from every store an agent writes to, then prove it.\"\"\"\nclass Store:\n    def __init__(self, name): self.name, self.rows = name, []\n    def delete_subject(self, sid): self.rows = [r for r in self.rows if r[\"subject\"] != sid]\n    def contains(self, sid): return any(r[\"subject\"] == sid for r in self.rows)\n\nSTORES = [Store(n) for n in (\"crm_db\", \"vector_index\", \"agent_memory\",\n                             \"checkpoints\", \"response_cache\", \"eval_dataset\", \"debug_traces\")]\n\ndef erase(subject_id):\n    for s in STORES:\n        s.delete_subject(subject_id)\n    report = {s.name: (\"FAIL\" if s.contains(subject_id) else \"ok\") for s in STORES}\n    return all(v == \"ok\" for v in report.values()), report\n\nfor s in STORES:                                   # seed: the subject appears everywhere\n    s.rows.append({\"subject\": \"cust-118\", \"text\": \"...\"})\nok, report = erase(\"cust-118\")\nprint(ok, report)\n# True {'crm_db': 'ok', 'vector_index': 'ok', 'agent_memory': 'ok', 'checkpoints': 'ok', ...}\n```\n\n- **The list is the point.** Agent memory, checkpoints and response caches are the stores teams forget;\n  a real job would call each store's delete API and query it by the subject's identifiers.","patterns":["**Gateway policy, deny by default.** Every model call, from every agent step, goes through one gateway with per-tenant allowlists of providers and regions and a content-logging flag, as in the sketch above. Monitor refusal counts per tenant; a spike after a release means a code path tried a disallowed route.","**Policy as code on IaC plans.** Convert plans with `terraform show -json` and evaluate them with a policy engine such as Open Policy Agent (via Conftest) to reject storage outside approved regions or log sinks without retention. Track failed policy checks per pull request.","**Split audit and debug stores.** Audit records (including human approvals of agent actions) go to a write-once, long-retention store (for example S3 with Object Lock); debug traces get a short lifecycle expiry such as 30 days. Alert if any trace store lacks an expiry.","**Erasure that reaches derived data.** An erasure job deletes from the database, the vector index, caches, agent memory, checkpoints and eval datasets, then a test queries each for the subject's identifiers. Monitor time to complete erasure requests and failed verification checks.","**Egress reconciliation.** Compare actual egress destinations and vendor invoices against the declared subprocessor list each month, because new SDKs, tools and fallbacks arrive silently. Alert on any new external domain.","**Compliance as non-functional requirements.** Capture each regime as where/who/what/how-long/how/proof constraints in discovery, with an owner and the evidence each must produce."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Every agent step sends context, often including tool results full of\n  personal data, to a model; traces capture it; memory and checkpoints keep it. Each of those is a\n  processing location, a retention period and an erasure target that compliance rules govern.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The model gateway's per-tenant provider and region allowlist,\n  trace and log redaction, memory and checkpoint TTLs, the erasure job across vector store, memory and\n  caches, and human-approval steps for consequential actions.\n- **What you control.** Routing every model call through one policy chokepoint, keeping customer data in\n  deletable retrieval indexes rather than fine-tuned weights, logging metadata instead of content, and\n  making oversight an auditable event.\n- **What you can skip.** Interpreting the law and classifying an AI Act risk tier; counsel does that.\n  Know the regimes well enough to translate them into constraints and evidence.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Healthcare agents under HIPAA.** A note-summarizing agent uses only BAA-covered model providers,\n  hashes content in traces and records each clinician approval in the audit store.\n- **EU agents with residency contracts.** The model gateway pins each tenant to EU regions and refuses the\n  US fallback, with refusal logs as evidence.\n- **Erasure for agents with memory.** A deletion job reaches the vector index, memory, checkpoints and\n  caches, and a verification report closes each request.\n- **Keeping agents out of PCI scope.** Card numbers are stripped from tool results before they reach the\n  prompt or the trace.","mini_project":"**Put a compliance chokepoint in front of an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make every model call from a small tool-calling agent pass a per-tenant policy, and prove\n  erasure across its stores.\n- **Steps:** wrap the agent's model client with the `route` gateway for two tenants; add a fallback that\n  tries a disallowed region and confirm it is refused; give the agent a simple memory store and a trace\n  store with a 30-day TTL field; implement the erasure job across memory, traces, a vector index and a\n  cache, then run it for one subject.\n- **Done when:** a run for each tenant shows only allowed provider and region pairs, the fallback appears\n  as a logged refusal, no trace holds raw prompt text, and the erasure report shows every store `ok`."},"exercise":"**Goal:** harden the gateway and erasure sketches into tested components for a two-tenant agent. No\nsolution is given.\n- **Inputs:** the `route` and `erase` examples above, an EU tenant with no content logging and a US\n  tenant with BAA-covered providers only.\n- **Deliver:** an HMAC-keyed input hash instead of plain SHA-256, refusal records written to an audit\n  list, a per-store TTL check that fails for any trace store without expiry, and an erasure job whose\n  report lists every store the agent writes to.\n- **Acceptance:** pytest shows a disallowed region refused and logged, no trace containing prompt text,\n  the same input hashing differently under two keys, and an erasure report failing when one store's\n  delete is a no-op.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an EU bank's contract requires EU-only processing and 90-day log retention. Your agent's\ntools now include web search through a US-hosted search API, and the team wants to fine-tune a small\nmodel on six months of the bank's agent traces to cut cost.\n\nWhich constraints does each change hit, and what do you propose?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- sees the search tool as a data flow: queries can carry customer data to a new US subprocessor, so it\n  needs an EU endpoint, query redaction, or disabling for this tenant, plus a subprocessor update;\n- sees that six months of traces cannot exist under 90-day retention, so the dataset is already a\n  finding;\n- flags fine-tuning on personal data as an erasure problem, proposing de-identified or synthetic data\n  with the bank's agreement;\n- routes both changes through the gateway allowlist and a delta review, and counsel for contract terms.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    O[\"Obligation: law, contract, framework\"] --> C[\"Constraint: where, who, what, how long, how, proof\"]\n    C --> G[\"Runtime: model gateway per-tenant policy\"]\n    C --> P[\"Change time: policy check on IaC plans\"]\n    C --> R[\"Periodic: access reviews, deletion tests\"]\n    G -->|\"refusal logs, config\"| E[(\"Evidence pack\")]\n    P -->|\"failed plans, approvals\"| E\n    R -->|\"review records, test results\"| E\n    E --> A[\"Auditor or customer review\"]\n    A -.->|\"new finding or regime change\"| C","caption":"Each obligation becomes a constraint, then a control at runtime or change time, and the controls emit\nthe evidence auditors check. Every agent step crosses the runtime gateway."},"interview":[{"q":"A customer says \"we are HIPAA-regulated\". What do you translate that into?","a":"- **BAA chain:** a BAA with the customer and coverage for every subprocessor that sees PHI, including the\n  cloud and model providers; exclude providers that cannot sign.\n- **Data handling:** no training and bounded retention on the model path, minimum-necessary logging, and\n  access-logged storage for content.\n- **Evidence:** BAA list, data-flow diagram, retention config in IaC and access review records."},{"q":"Your agent has long-term memory and runs for EU customers. A user exercises their right to erasure. What has to happen?","a":"- **Find every copy:** the source record, chunks and embeddings in the vector index, agent memory\n  entries, conversation checkpoints, response caches, eval datasets built from traces, and debug traces.\n- **Delete and verify:** an erasure job per store, then queries by the subject's identifiers that must\n  return nothing; the verification output is the evidence.\n- **Design so it stays possible:** TTLs on memory and traces, and no fine-tuning on that personal data."},{"q":"When would you choose self-hosting a model over an API provider for compliance reasons?","a":"- **When terms cannot be confirmed:** no BAA, no clear no-training or retention terms, or no in-region\n  endpoint.\n- **When access itself is restricted**, as with export-controlled data or air-gapped mandates.\n- **The cost:** GPU capacity, operations and often model quality, so prefer an API with suitable terms\n  when one exists."},{"q":"An auditor wants seven years of records; privacy wants 30-day logs. How do you size and split storage?","a":"- **Two stores:** an audit record of who did what and when, retained seven years with write-once\n  protection; and debug traces with metadata and hashes, expiring at 30 days.\n- **Content lives with the customer** in an access-controlled audit store, not in vendor telemetry.\n- **Size the audit store from event counts** (events per day × record size × seven years), which stays\n  small because it holds metadata, not prompts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Region pinning vs failover.** Region-pinning and single-provider routing satisfy residency simply, but remove cross-region failover. If availability targets are strict, you need multiple in-region providers or accept degraded modes.","**Self-hosting vs cost and quality.** Self-hosting a model in the customer's environment satisfies the strictest data-custody constraints, but costs GPU capacity, operations and often model quality (tool-calling accuracy in particular); API providers with suitable terms win when available.","**Minimal logging vs debuggability.** It lowers compliance exposure but makes production debugging harder, and agent failures are hard to debug without the trace; structured metadata, hashes and on-demand, access-logged content capture recover most of the debugging value.","**Long agent memory vs erasure cost.** Persistent memory makes agents more useful across sessions, but every remembered fact is another erasure target and retention question. Short TTLs flip the balance when the data is regulated.","**One compliant tier vs many.** A single compliant tier for every customer simplifies operations but over-serves smaller customers; separate tiers cost more to run and must be described precisely in questionnaires."],"pitfalls":["**Treating \"compliant\" as a vendor property rather than a configuration.** Symptom: using a provider's HIPAA-eligible service without a signed BAA or with ineligible features enabled. Diagnose: list each PHI-touching subprocessor against signed BAAs and the features actually enabled. Fix: exclude or sign.","**Forgetting derived data.** Embeddings, caches, eval datasets, traces, agent memory and checkpoints hold the same personal data. Symptom: an erasure completed in the database while vectors and logs still hold it. Diagnose: query every store by the subject's identifiers after erasure. Fix: an erasure job with per-store verification.","**Fine-tuning on customer personal data without a deletion story.** Symptom: an erasure request that can only be honored by retraining or discarding the model. Diagnose: trace each training set back to its source records.","**Engineering changes that silently violate a constraint**, such as a new fallback region, analytics SDK or tool calling an outside API. Symptom: an audit finding against a previously compliant system. Diagnose: gateway refusal counts per release and new domains in egress. Fix: deny-by-default routing.","**Deciding the EU AI Act risk tier or other legal classifications in engineering alone.** Symptom: a design built for the wrong obligations, found when counsel finally reviews it."],"when":"- **Ask in discovery**, before choosing a deployment model or model provider: which regimes apply, which\n  customer policies go beyond them, and what evidence their reviewers will want.\n- **Translate and record** each into where/who/what/how-long/how/proof constraints as non-functional\n  requirements, and enforce them in code and configuration so evidence comes for free.\n- **Revisit** whenever data sources, regions, providers, tools or features change.\n- **Do not interpret law yourself:** bring counsel in for classification and contract terms.\n- **Go deeper** in the security-governance-compliance module for the full design of controls, audit\n  trails and residency.\n- **Interviews:** show the translation from regime to architecture.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements constraints as code: region and provider allowlists in the gateway, log redaction, retention policies in IaC, and deletion that reaches embeddings and caches.","architect":"Maps each applicable regime to architectural constraints, resolves conflicts such as audit retention versus minimization, and chooses the deployment model and providers that satisfy them.","fde":"Elicits the customer's real obligations and internal policies early, confirms subprocessor agreements like BAAs, and assembles the evidence pack for their compliance and security teams."},"check":{"q":"An EU customer says \"we are GDPR-regulated, so everything must stay in the EU.\" Your system uses an\nEU-hosted model by default, a US-hosted fallback, and fine-tunes a small model on customer tickets. What\ndo you check and change?","a":"First separate the law from the policy: GDPR permits transfers with safeguards, but the customer's\ncontractual requirement is stricter and is what you must meet. The US fallback violates it, so disable\ncross-region fallback for this tenant and use a second EU-region provider or a degraded mode instead. Check\nevery subprocessor for EU processing, including tracing and error tools. Fine-tuning on tickets creates an\nerasure problem, because personal data in weights cannot be selectively removed; either drop fine-tuning in\nfavor of retrieval over deletable indexes, train only on anonymized data, or agree a retraining policy.\nEnsure deletion reaches embeddings and caches. Document it all in the DPA and data-flow diagram."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are compliance requirements best gathered before choosing the deployment model?","options":["Because auditors require requirements documents to be dated before code","Because constraints such as residency, subprocessor agreements and retention determine which regions, providers and deployment models are allowed","Because compliance requirements rarely change after launch","Because compliance reviews are faster for systems with fewer services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Compliance rules are architectural: they can rule out a model provider, a region or SaaS hosting entirely. Learning them after the build forces rework, which is the cost early discovery avoids."},{"q":"A hospital asks whether your assistant may use a hosted model API for clinical notes. What is the deciding requirement under HIPAA?","options":["The model must score above a set threshold on medical benchmarks","The API must be hosted in the same state as the hospital","The prompts must be encrypted with the hospital's own keys","The model provider, as a subprocessor handling PHI, must be covered by a business associate agreement"],"answer":3,"explanation":"PHI may flow to a vendor and its subprocessors only under BAAs. Encryption and hosting location matter for security, but without a BAA the flow is not permitted regardless of quality or location."},{"q":"An auditor requires seven-year retention of activity records while the privacy team demands minimal logging of personal data. Which design resolves both?","options":["Keep full prompts and outputs for seven years in the debug trace store","Disable logging entirely to satisfy minimization","Retain an audit record of who did what and when, with content in an access-controlled store, while debug traces keep only metadata for a short period","Encrypt the logs so retention rules no longer apply"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Separating audit records from debug telemetry satisfies each requirement on its own terms. Keeping all content for seven years violates minimization, encryption does not remove retention duties, and disabling logs fails the audit obligation."}]},{"key":"deployment/deployment-readiness-reviews","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Launch risk is spread across many owners.** Builders of the prompt and retrieval pipeline, the\n  customer's security, network and identity teams, the business owner who defined success, and whoever is\n  paged at 3 a.m. Each checks its own slice; nobody checks the seams.\n- **The seams are where agent launches fail.** The eval passed, but on last month's index. Security\n  approved the architecture, but not the tool the agent gained afterwards. Dashboards exist, but no alert\n  routes to a human. A rollback plan exists, but nobody has run it.\n- **\"The tech lead decides\" fails twice.** One person cannot hold the full checklist and forgets the items\n  outside their expertise; schedule pressure rationalises every gap as \"we'll fix it in hypercare\".\n- **The review makes the decision structural.** Criteria are agreed before the date, evidence replaces\n  assertions, and the record says which risks were accepted and by whom.\n- **It has precedent.** Google's SRE practice formalised the Production Readiness Review, run before an\n  SRE team agrees to carry a service's pager. The same logic applies when a customer's operations team is\n  about to inherit your agent.","how":"### 1. Agree the criteria early\nDraft the checklist at the start of the agent build, not the week before launch, because criteria written\nafter the system exists describe what was built rather than what is needed.\n\n| Category | Typical items for an agent |\n|---|---|\n| **Quality** | Golden-set, trajectory and customer regression evals meet the acceptance criteria from scoping; UAT signed off; known failure modes documented |\n| **Safety and security** | Security and architecture review approved for the *current* design and tool list; each tool's permission scope is least-privilege; guardrails and prompt-injection tests pass against the red-team set; retention, PII and residency match the contract |\n| **Agent controls** | Max-step and per-run cost ceilings enforced; loop detection on; write tools behind approval or dry-run; kill switch per tool verified |\n| **Operability** | Health checks, dashboards and alerts route to a named on-call; every alert links to a runbook; logs and traces (every tool call and result) kept long enough to debug |\n| **Capacity and cost** | Load test at expected peak plus headroom; provider quotas and rate limits confirmed in writing; per-request and daily cost ceilings enforced, not just estimated |\n| **Release mechanics** | Rollout stages, promotion criteria and rollback triggers defined; rollback rehearsed in a production-like environment; feature flag or kill switch verified |\n| **People** | Support model and escalation paths agreed; customer team trained; hypercare staffed |\n\n### 2. Evidence, not assertions\n- **Every item has an owner and a link.** An eval report with its dataset version, a load-test result, a\n  screenshot of an alert firing in staging, the ticket where security signed off on the tool list.\n- **No evidence means not done**, because \"done\" is the cheapest word to say.\n- **Pin evidence to the deploying configuration.** Record a release fingerprint (model identifier, prompt\n  version or hash, retrieval config, index build, tool list) and attach it to every eval and load-test\n  result, because an eval on last week's prompt is evidence about a different agent.\n- **Mismatched fingerprint reverts the item to not done.** That catches the \"passed, but on last month's\n  index\" gap mechanically instead of by luck.\n\n### 3. Classify every item\n- **Blocking:** no launch without it, e.g. rollback works, PII is not logged, write tools are gated.\n- **Non-blocking:** launch can proceed if the gap is accepted.\n- **Waiver:** how a non-blocking gap is accepted: a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry\n  date. Waivers expire because \"temporary\" exceptions without dates become permanent.\n\n### 4. Decide and record\n1. **Fix decision rights before the meeting:** who chairs, who decides, and which owners hold a veto on\n   their blocking items (security on data handling and tool scopes, the operating team on operability).\n   Otherwise the most senior person decides everything and you are back to \"tech lead decides\".\n2. **Put the consequence-carriers among the deciders.** The team taking the pager and the business owner\n   decide; they are not the audience.\n3. **Walk the open items, not the green ones.** Good prep keeps the meeting short.\n4. **Pick an outcome:** *go*, *conditional go* (launch with listed waivers), or *no-go* with the specific\n   items that must change.\n5. **Write it down with its waivers**, because when an incident happens in week two the first question\n   will be \"did we know about this?\".\n\n### 5. Re-run on material change\n- **A review approves one agent configuration:** model version, prompt, tools, index, data sources.\n- **Swapping the model or adding a write-capable tool is a new launch** for review purposes, scaled down to\n  the items the change touches.\n- **Widening a tool's scope counts too.** A `search_crm` tool that gains write access is a new tool from a\n  risk point of view, even if its name did not change.","example":"### Readiness gate as code\nA readiness gate kept as data next to the agent's code, so the go/no-go is computed rather than argued.\nThe scenario: an insurer's claims-triage agent, two weeks before go-live.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nitems = [  # (id, blocking, evidence_link_or_None, passed, waiver)\n    (\"eval.golden_set>=0.85\",   True,  \"evals/run-0412.json\", True,  None),\n    (\"security.review_current\", True,  \"SEC-2291\",            True,  None),\n    (\"rollback.rehearsed\",      True,  None,                  False, None),\n    (\"alerts.route_to_oncall\",  True,  \"staging-alert.png\",   True,  None),\n    (\"load.peak_x2\",            False, \"load/0409.html\",      False,\n        {\"owner\": \"cust-ops-lead\", \"control\": \"rate limit 20 rps\", \"expires\": date(2026, 11, 30)}),\n    (\"training.agents_trained\", False, None,                  False, None),\n]\n\ndef decide(items, today):\n    blockers, waived, gaps = [], [], []\n    for iid, blocking, evidence, passed, waiver in items:\n        ok = passed and evidence is not None          # no evidence means not done\n        if ok:\n            continue\n        if blocking:\n            blockers.append(iid)\n        elif waiver and waiver[\"expires\"] > today:\n            waived.append((iid, waiver[\"owner\"], waiver[\"expires\"]))\n        else:\n            gaps.append(iid)                            # non-blocking but unowned: still a no-go\n    if blockers or gaps:\n        return \"NO-GO\", blockers + gaps\n    return (\"CONDITIONAL GO\" if waived else \"GO\"), waived\n\nprint(decide(items, date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ('NO-GO', ['rollback.rehearsed', 'training.agents_trained'])\n```\n\nRehearsing the rollback and training the claims handlers (or waiving it) makes this a conditional go,\nwith the load-test gap owned by the customer's ops lead until 30 November. (In this item list, `training.agents_trained` means the human agents who use the tool.)\n\n### Fingerprint check that catches stale evidence\nEach piece of evidence stores the release fingerprint it was produced against; the gate compares it with\nthe release candidate.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json\n\ndef fingerprint(release: dict) -> str:\n    keys = (\"model\", \"prompt_hash\", \"retrieval_config\", \"index_build\", \"tools\")\n    blob = json.dumps({k: release[k] for k in keys}, sort_keys=True)\n    return hashlib.sha256(blob.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]\n\ncandidate = {\"model\": \"model-v3\", \"prompt_hash\": \"a1f9\", \"retrieval_config\": \"k=8\",\n             \"index_build\": \"idx-2026-10-18\", \"tools\": [\"lookup_policy\"]}\neval_run = dict(candidate, index_build=\"idx-2026-09-15\")   # eval ran on last month's index\n\nstale = fingerprint(eval_run) != fingerprint(candidate)\nprint(\"eval evidence stale:\", stale)   # True -> item reverts to not done\n```\n\n### Tool-manifest diff: what security has not seen\nTool lists drift faster than architecture documents, so compare the candidate's tools and scopes with\nthe manifest security approved.\n\n```python\napproved = {\"lookup_policy\": \"read\", \"get_claim\": \"read\"}          # from the signed-off review\ncandidate = {\"lookup_policy\": \"read\", \"get_claim\": \"read\",\n             \"update_claim_status\": \"write\", \"search_web\": \"read\"}  # what the agent ships with\n\ndef tool_drift(approved, candidate):\n    findings = []\n    for name, scope in candidate.items():\n        if name not in approved:\n            findings.append((name, \"new tool\", \"BLOCKING\" if scope == \"write\" else \"review\"))\n        elif scope != approved[name]:                    # widened to write blocks; narrowed needs a look\n            findings.append((name, f\"scope {approved[name]} -> {scope}\",\n                             \"BLOCKING\" if scope == \"write\" else \"review\"))\n    return findings\n\nfor f in tool_drift(approved, candidate):\n    print(f)\n# ('update_claim_status', 'new tool', 'BLOCKING')\n# ('search_web', 'new tool', 'review')\n```\n\nThe write tool blocks launch until security reviews it and an approval gate is proven; the read-only web\nsearch still needs a review because its results are an untrusted input the agent will act on.\n\n### Worked scenario: what the review caught\nIllustrative findings from one review of the claims-triage agent launch.\n\n| Finding | Class | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rollback never rehearsed | Blocking | Rehearsed in staging two days later |\n| Load test at 2x peak failed | Non-blocking | Waiver: owner cust-ops-lead, rate limit 20 rps, expires 30 November |\n| Eval evidence on old index | Blocking (fingerprint mismatch) | Eval re-run on the release candidate |\n| `update_claim_status` tool added after security review | Blocking (tool drift) | Reviewed; ships behind human approval for the first stage |\n| Claims-handler training not done | Non-blocking, no waiver | Training scheduled before go-live |","patterns":["**Checklist in the repo, evidence by link.** When agent releases are frequent, keep the checklist as a version-controlled file reviewed in the release pull request; CI fails if a blocking item has no evidence link. Monitor how many items are green without a link (should be zero).","**Release fingerprint on every artifact.** Stamp eval reports and load-test results with a hash of model, prompt, retrieval config, index build and tool list; the gate compares it with the candidate. Monitor fingerprint mismatches per review.","**Tool manifest as a reviewed artifact.** Keep tool names, scopes and approval requirements in one file that security signs off, and diff it in CI against what the agent actually registers. Monitor unreviewed tools and scope changes per release (target zero).","**Waiver register with expiry alerts.** Store waivers in a tracker with owner, compensating control and expiry; a scheduled job flags waivers within a week of expiry. Monitor open waiver count and how many are renewed rather than closed.","**Risk-tiered reviews.** A prompt tweak behind a flag gets an automated eval gate; a new write-capable tool gets the full meeting. Write the tier rules down so the trigger is not negotiated per change."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is a model plus a prompt plus a tool list plus a retrieval index\n  plus step budgets, each with a different owner, so launches fail at the seams between them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A checklist file in the agent's repo, a release fingerprint\n  (model, prompt hash, tool manifest, index build) stamped on every eval report, and a CI gate that refuses\n  to promote when blocking items lack evidence.\n- **What you control.** The tool manifest and its permission scopes, which tools need approval gates, the\n  max-step and cost ceilings, and which agent changes count as a new launch.\n- **What you can skip.** Heavyweight governance boards for a read-only internal agent behind a flag; an\n  automated eval gate plus a pull-request checklist is enough there.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **First production launch of a tool-calling agent.** The review proves tool scopes, approval gates,\n  step budgets and rollback before real users and real systems of record are involved.\n- **Handing an agent to a customer's operations team.** Their on-call signs off operability items\n  (alerts, runbooks, kill switches) before they inherit the pager.\n- **Model or provider swap under an existing agent.** A scaled-down review re-runs evals and load tests\n  against the new fingerprint instead of trusting the old evidence.\n- **Adding a write-capable tool.** The tool-manifest diff forces a security review and an approval gate\n  before the agent can change records.","mini_project":"**Build a readiness gate for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a CI script that computes go, conditional go or no-go for an agent release from a checklist\n  file, evidence links and a release fingerprint.\n- **Steps:** write a YAML or JSON checklist with 8-10 items (two of them agent controls such as a step\n  ceiling and an approval gate); stamp a fake eval report with a fingerprint of model, prompt hash and\n  tool manifest; add a tool-manifest diff against an approved list; make the script exit non-zero on\n  no-go.\n- **Done when:** changing the prompt hash, adding a write tool, or letting a waiver expire each flips the\n  result to no-go with a message naming the item, and a clean candidate returns go."},"exercise":"**Goal:** turn a real or sample agent release into a computed go/no-go. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** an agent you have built (or the claims-triage example), its tool list with scopes, and the\n  evidence you actually have: eval reports, load tests, alert screenshots.\n- **Constraints:** at least 10 checklist items across the seven categories, at most 4 of them blocking,\n  and every evidence file stamped with a release fingerprint.\n- **Deliver:** the checklist file, a gate script that exits non-zero on no-go, and a one-page decision\n  record with waivers.\n- **Acceptance:** changing the prompt hash, adding a write tool, or moving a waiver's expiry into the past\n  each flips the result to no-go naming the item; a clean candidate returns go; a colleague can find the\n  owner of every waiver from the record alone.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a support agent passed review a month ago. Since then it moved to a newer model version,\nthe index is rebuilt nightly, and the `refund_order` limit rose from 50 to 500.\n\nThe sponsor says \"nothing material changed\". What do you re-review, and how do nightly builds avoid\ninvalidating every eval?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- treats the model swap and the widened refund limit as material: re-run quality, trajectory, load and\n  cost checks (a new model changes latency and cost per run), and get security sign-off on the new scope\n  and its approval gate;\n- spots that a tenfold refund limit is a scope change even though the tool name did not change;\n- fingerprints the index by schema and pipeline version rather than build date, and runs a fast eval\n  gate on each nightly build, so routine rebuilds neither reset evidence nor drift unchecked;\n- leaves untouched items (alerts, training) green and records the scaled-down decision.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    I[\"Checklist item\"] --> E{\"Evidence linked and fingerprint matches?\"}\n    E -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Passed\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| B{\"Blocking item?\"}\n    B -->|\"yes\"| NG[\"No-go: must fix\"]\n    B -->|\"no\"| W{\"Waiver with owner, control, expiry?\"}\n    W -->|\"yes, not expired\"| CG[\"Conditional go\"]\n    W -->|\"no\"| NG\n    P --> D[\"Record decision and waivers\"]\n    CG --> D\n    NG --> D\n    D -.->|\"new model or tool\"| I","caption":"Each checklist item flows through the same evidence and classification gate; the worst outcome across\nitems sets the decision, and a material agent change sends it back to the start."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a deployment readiness review, and why not let the tech lead decide?","a":"- **Definition:** a scheduled go/no-go where every launch criterion (quality, security, agent controls,\n  operability, capacity, release mechanics, people) is judged against linked evidence by a named owner.\n- **Why not one person:** they cannot hold the whole checklist, they miss items outside their expertise,\n  and schedule pressure turns gaps into \"we'll fix it in hypercare\"."},{"q":"The eval report is green, but an incident shows the agent was evaluated on an old index. How would you prevent that?","a":"- **Fingerprint the release:** model, prompt hash, retrieval config, index build and tool list, hashed\n  and stamped on every eval and load-test result.\n- **Gate on a match:** evidence whose fingerprint differs from the release candidate reverts the item to\n  not done, so the stale run is caught mechanically."},{"q":"A week after the review, a developer adds a write tool to the agent. What should happen?","a":"- **It is a new launch for the affected items:** security review of the tool and its scope, an approval or\n  dry-run gate, a runbook entry for disabling it, and trajectory evals that exercise it.\n- **Make it mechanical:** a CI diff of the registered tools against the approved manifest blocks the\n  release until the manifest is re-signed."},{"q":"How do you keep waivers from becoming permanent?","a":"- **Mandatory fields:** named risk owner, compensating control and expiry date; no waiver without all\n  three.\n- **Track them:** a register with expiry alerts, and a signal when waivers outnumber fixes across\n  releases, which means the criteria or the schedule are wrong."},{"q":"Which agent-specific items would you add to a standard service launch checklist?","a":"- **Controls on autonomy:** max-step and per-run cost ceilings, loop detection, and approval gates on\n  write tools, each proven in staging rather than assumed.\n- **Agent-shaped evidence:** trajectory evals on multi-step tasks, prompt-injection tests through tool\n  results, full tool-call traces retained, and a per-tool kill switch the on-call can flip."}],"tradeoffs":["**Coverage vs calendar time.** A formal review catches cross-team gaps but adds calendar time; for a low-risk internal read-only agent a full meeting is ceremony.","**Strictness vs honesty.** Strict blocking criteria prevent bad launches, but if everything is blocking the team learns to game evidence. Keep the blocking list short and reserve it for irreversible or customer-visible harm, such as an agent writing to a system of record.","**Speed vs debt.** Waivers let launches proceed under known risk, but each one is debt. The trade flips when waivers outnumber fixes across releases, which means the criteria or the schedule are wrong.","**Consistency vs throughput.** A central review board brings consistency across customers but becomes a bottleneck at scale. Mature agent teams move to self-service checklists with automated evidence and reserve humans for exceptions."],"pitfalls":["**Reviewing the day before launch.** Symptom: a \"conditional go\" with more conditions than the previous three launches combined, because no blocker can be fixed in time. Diagnose: count waivers per review and days between review and go-live. Fix: hold it early enough to fix blockers.","**Accepting \"yes, done\" without a link.** Symptom: an incident where the alert, runbook or rollback marked green never existed. Diagnose: count green items with no evidence link (should be zero). Fix: no link, not done.","**Reviewing the architecture approved months ago.** Symptom: a tool, data source or model in production that no security reviewer ever saw. Diagnose: diff the registered tool manifest and fingerprint against the signed-off ones. Fix: review what is deploying now.","**Waivers with no expiry or owner.** Symptom: the same accepted risk in next quarter's review and nobody can say who agreed to it. Diagnose: query the register for waivers missing either field or renewed more than once. Fix: owner and expiry are mandatory fields.","**Treating agent quality as a single number.** Symptom: the aggregate eval passed but a critical slice (one claim type, one language, one multi-step task) fails, because averages hide small slices. Diagnose: break the eval report down by slice and compare with the bar. Fix: per-slice criteria, including trajectory checks on the riskiest tasks."],"when":"- **Use before:** any first production launch of an agent, handing operations to a customer team, and\n  material changes: a new model or provider, a new tool with side effects, a widened tool scope, a new data\n  source, a new region.\n- **Scale it to risk** (see risk-tiered reviews): an agent that can issue refunds needs the full review.\n- **Measure the review by what escapes it:** for each incident in the first weeks, ask whether a checklist\n  item would have caught it, and add one if so.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the evidence: versioned eval reports, load-test results, a rehearsed rollback, and alerts proven to fire, linked from each checklist item rather than described.","architect":"Owns the criteria and the blocking list, decides which changes trigger re-review, and signs off on which risks the organisation is willing to accept with waivers.","fde":"Runs the review with the customer's security, ops and business owners in the room, so their sign-offs and accepted risks are recorded before the go-live date is defended."},"check":{"q":"Two days before go-live, the load test shows the system handles only 60% of forecast peak because\nthe customer's provider quota is lower than requested. The sponsor wants to launch. What do you do?","a":"First classify the gap: is overload customer-visible harm or graceful degradation? If the system\nhas a rate limiter and a clear \"busy, try again\" path, capacity can be non-blocking, so I would propose\na conditional go with a waiver: the customer's ops lead owns the risk, the compensating control is a\nrate limit plus a staged rollout capped below 60% of peak, and the waiver expires when the quota\nincrease lands. If overload would cause silent failures or timeouts mid-workflow, it is blocking and the\nanswer is no-go or a reduced launch scope. Either way the decision is written down with its owner."},"quiz":[{"q":"What most distinguishes a readiness review from a team simply agreeing they are ready?","options":["It is attended by more senior people","Each criterion is agreed in advance and judged against linked evidence with a named owner","It replaces the need for evals and load tests","It always happens after the first week of production traffic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The value is structural: criteria fixed before schedule pressure, evidence instead of assertions, and recorded ownership. Seniority alone does not prevent unchecked seams, and the review consumes evals rather than replacing them."},{"q":"A non-blocking item failed. What makes it acceptable to launch anyway?","options":["The tech lead believes it will be fixed during hypercare","The item is moved to the backlog with a low priority","A waiver with a named risk owner, a compensating control and an expiry date","The sponsor approves the launch date"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A waiver makes the accepted risk explicit and temporary. Backlog items and verbal assurances have no owner or deadline, so the gap silently becomes permanent and nobody is accountable when it bites."},{"q":"Which change most clearly requires re-running at least part of the readiness review?","options":["Rewording a help-text string in the UI","Rotating an API key on its normal schedule","Adding a new dashboard panel","Giving the agent a new tool that can write to the customer's CRM"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A review approves a specific configuration. A write-capable tool changes the security surface, the failure modes and the rollback story, so the affected items (security, guardrails, runbooks) must be re-checked."}]},{"key":"deployment/uat","sources":[],"why":"- **\"Passes our tests\" is not \"does the job\".** Engineering tests check the builders' understanding of\n  the requirement. They miss a misunderstood requirement, an unmentioned workflow step, or a correct but\n  useless output: a claims summary missing the one field adjusters always check, or a cited policy in a\n  format compliance cannot file.\n- **Only users know those things**, and they often discover them only by using the agent.\n- **Acceptance is often contractual.** In enterprise and forward-deployed work it triggers payment or the\n  pilot-to-production move. Without a defined UAT it becomes a negotiation over impressions: two bad demo\n  answers stall the project, or everyone says \"looks good\" and the agent is abandoned three weeks after\n  launch.\n- **The naive version fails for AI.** \"Give users access for two weeks and ask what they think\" means a\n  handful of queries per person, judged on memorable failures. Output varies run to run, so one tester\n  passes what another fails, and without a rubric nobody can settle it.\n- **So design it like an experiment:** fixed criteria, representative cases, a rubric, and a sample\n  large enough to decide.","how":"### 1. Fix acceptance criteria before testing starts\n- **Criteria come from scoping, written as measurable statements:** \"at least 85% of triage\n  recommendations rated correct or acceptable by adjusters\", \"zero responses that disclose another\n  customer's data\", \"p95 response under 8 seconds\".\n- **Hard gates** (safety, privacy, legal, unapproved write actions): any single failure blocks.\n- **Rate thresholds** (quality and task-completion metrics): judged on a sample.\n- **Keep them separate**, because a single privacy leak is not averaged away by 99 good answers.\n- **For agents, gate on actions as well as words:** an agent that writes the right summary but also\n  updates the wrong claim fails, however good the text was.\n\n### 2. Build a representative case set\n- **Draw from real historical work**, stratified by the slices the business cares about (claim type,\n  language, document quality, customer tier, number of steps the task needs), plus deliberately chosen\n  edge cases.\n- **Representativeness matters** because the pass rate on easy cases tells you nothing about the hard\n  ones.\n- **Store a reference answer per case**, written by subject-matter experts, so scoring is not improvised.\n  For agent tasks, also store the expected end state (which record changes, which tool must not be\n  called).\n\n### 3. Run in a production-like environment\n- **Use the real integrations** (identity, document stores, downstream systems, the actual tools the agent\n  calls) with production-shaped data, isolated from production writes.\n- **Not mocks**, because mocks hide the integration failures UAT is best at catching: permission errors,\n  slow connectors, malformed documents, tool responses in a shape the agent never saw.\n\n### 4. Score with a rubric, not a thumbs-up\n- **A small scale with written definitions**, for example *correct*, *acceptable with minor edit*,\n  *wrong*, *harmful*.\n- **Overlap a fraction of cases between testers and measure agreement**, because if two adjusters\n  disagree on the same output, the criterion is ambiguous and must be fixed before the numbers mean\n  anything.\n- **Show testers the trace, not only the answer**, so an answer reached through a forbidden tool call or a\n  runaway 30-step loop can be scored as a failure.\n\n### 5. Size the sample and handle non-determinism\nA rate threshold is a claim about the true pass rate, so the lower end of the confidence interval must\nclear the bar. A planning estimate with the normal approximation:\n\n```\nn ≈ z² · p(1 − p) / m²        z = 1.96 for 95%, p = expected pass rate, m = margin you can afford\n```\n\n| Expected rate | Bar | Margin | Normal estimate | Wilson (reporting) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 88% | 85% | 3 points | 1.96² × 0.88 × 0.12 / 0.03² ≈ 450 cases | roughly 550 cases (slightly more conservative) |\n\n- **The m² in the denominator is the lesson:** halving the margin quadruples the cases. A criterion set\n  just below the expected rate is expensive to prove, so negotiate the bar and the sample together during\n  scoping rather than at sign-off.\n- **Repeated runs are not independent samples.** Running each case three times does not turn 200 cases\n  into 600 observations, because runs of one case share input and difficulty and fail together.\n- **Score at the case level:** for example \"passes on at least 2 of 3 runs\", or the per-case pass\n  fraction averaged across cases.\n- **Report the run-to-run flip rate separately.** A case that passes once and fails once is a stability\n  defect in its own right, even if the average looks fine. Agents flip more than single calls, because\n  one different tool choice early changes every later step.\n\n### 6. Triage defects by cause\nEvery failure gets a severity (blocker, major, minor) and a cause; the cause decides the fix and whether\na re-test is needed.\n\n| Cause | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Integration** | Wrong data reached the model |\n| **Retrieval** | Right data existed, was not found |\n| **Tool use** | Wrong tool, bad arguments, or a tool error the agent mishandled |\n| **Model/prompt** | Right context, wrong answer |\n| **Requirement** | System did what was specified; the specification was wrong |\n\n- **A requirement finding is a change request, not a defect.** Treating it as a defect is how UAT never\n  ends.\n\n### 7. Exit and sign off\n1. Hard gates are clean.\n2. Rate thresholds are met with enough confidence.\n3. No open blockers remain.\n4. A named person on the customer side signs off, recorded.\n5. Every failing case found during UAT goes into the customer-specific regression suite so it stays\n   fixed.","example":"### Is 88% on 200 cases enough? A Wilson interval check\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef wilson(passed, n, z=1.96):\n    p = passed / n\n    denom = 1 + z * z / n\n    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / denom\n    half = z * sqrt(p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n)) / denom\n    return centre - half, centre + half\n\nbar = 0.85\nfor passed, n in [(176, 200), (352, 400), (528, 600)]:\n    lo, hi = wilson(passed, n)\n    print(f\"{passed}/{n}: {lo:.1%} to {hi:.1%}  clears bar: {lo >= bar}\")\n# 176/200: 82.8% to 91.8%  clears bar: False\n# 352/400: 84.4% to 90.8%  clears bar: False\n# 528/600: 85.2% to 90.4%  clears bar: True\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an insurer's triage agent sign-off\nThe criterion is \"at least 85% of triage recommendations rated correct or acceptable\". Adjusters score\n200 stratified cases and 176 pass: **88%**. The project manager wants to sign off.\n\n| Sample at 88% | 95% Wilson interval | Clears 85%? |\n|---|---|---|\n| 200 cases | roughly **82.8% to 91.8%** | No |\n| 400 cases | roughly 84.4% to 90.8% | No, still not clear |\n| about 600 cases | roughly 85.2% to 90.4% | Yes |\n\n- **The sample cannot tell** \"above the bar\" from \"below it\", because the lower bound is under 85%.\n- **The options are concrete:** score about 400 more cases, renegotiate to judge on the point estimate (an\n  explicit, recorded decision), or improve the agent so the observed rate rises.\n\nThe slice view changes the story:\n\n| Slice | Cases | Passed | Rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Handwritten claim forms | 40 of 200 (20%) | 25 of 40 | about 62% |\n| Typed forms | 160 | 151 of 160 | about 94% |\n\n- **The aggregate hides an unfit slice.** Of the 24 failures, 15 are handwritten forms.\n- **Triage points to integration:** 11 of the 15 are OCR-quality failures, not model reasoning. The fix\n  is the document parser, followed by a re-test of that slice only.\n\n### Case-level scoring with repeated runs\n```python\nruns = {  # case_id -> pass/fail for 3 runs\n    \"c1\": [True, True, True], \"c2\": [True, False, True],\n    \"c3\": [False, False, False], \"c4\": [True, False, False],\n}\ncase_pass = {c: sum(r) >= 2 for c, r in runs.items()}          # \"passes on at least 2 of 3\"\nflip_rate = sum(len(set(r)) > 1 for r in runs.values()) / len(runs)\nprint(sum(case_pass.values()) / len(runs), flip_rate)        # 0.5 0.5 -> report both\n```\n\n### Agent run verdict: hard gates on actions, then the rubric\nEach UAT run of the agent produces a trace. Hard gates are checked mechanically from the trace before the\ntester's rubric score counts, so a good-looking answer cannot hide a forbidden action.\n\n```python\nFORBIDDEN = {\"delete_claim\"}                 # never allowed in this deployment\nNEEDS_APPROVAL = {\"update_claim_status\"}     # write tool: only with a recorded approval\n\ndef run_verdict(trace, rubric_score, max_steps=15):\n    for step in trace[\"tool_calls\"]:\n        if step[\"tool\"] in FORBIDDEN:\n            return \"HARD FAIL\", f\"called {step['tool']}\"\n        if step[\"tool\"] in NEEDS_APPROVAL and not step.get(\"approved\"):\n            return \"HARD FAIL\", f\"unapproved {step['tool']}\"\n    if len(trace[\"tool_calls\"]) > max_steps:\n        return \"FAIL\", \"step budget exceeded\"\n    if trace[\"end_state\"] != trace[\"expected_end_state\"]:\n        return \"FAIL\", \"wrong end state\"\n    return (\"PASS\", \"rubric\") if rubric_score in (\"correct\", \"acceptable\") else (\"FAIL\", \"rubric\")\n\ntrace = {\"tool_calls\": [{\"tool\": \"get_claim\"}, {\"tool\": \"lookup_policy\"},\n                        {\"tool\": \"update_claim_status\", \"approved\": False}],\n         \"end_state\": \"status=triaged\", \"expected_end_state\": \"status=triaged\"}\nprint(run_verdict(trace, \"correct\"))   # ('HARD FAIL', 'unapproved update_claim_status')\n```\n\nThe answer and end state were right, and the tester rated it correct, yet the run fails a hard gate: one\nsuch run blocks sign-off regardless of the pass rate.","patterns":["**Pre-registered acceptance doc.** At scoping, write hard gates, rate thresholds, sample size and the slices to report, and get the sponsor's sign-off on that document. Monitor how many criteria change after UAT starts (should be zero without a recorded change request).","**Calibration round before scoring.** Testers score the same 20 to 30 outputs, discuss disagreements, and tighten rubric definitions before the main round. Monitor inter-rater agreement (for example Cohen's kappa) on the overlap set throughout.","**Repeated runs, trace-attached scoring.** The harness runs each case several times through the full agent loop, runs mechanical gates before the rubric, and shows testers the trace. Monitor flip rate per slice (rising after a prompt change is a stability regression) and hard-gate failures (any blocks exit).","**Defect tracker with a cause field.** Every failure is filed with severity and one of integration, retrieval, tool use, model/prompt or requirement; requirement findings route to the change-request process. Monitor defects by cause per round to see whether rounds converge.","**Failures become regression cases.** Each failing UAT case is exported with its trace into the customer-specific regression suite, which then gates every later release."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Your evals check your understanding of the task. Users check whether the\n  agent actually finishes their work: the right tool calls, the right record updated, the field adjusters\n  always look at. Agent UAT is where \"completes the task\" gets an owner and a number.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A UAT harness that replays cases through the full agent loop\n  (identity, tools, retrieval) in a sandbox, captures the trace per run, and records the tester's rubric\n  score next to it.\n- **What you control.** Hard gates on actions (no unapproved writes, no cross-tenant data), the case set\n  and its slices, runs per case, and how failures are triaged: integration, retrieval, tool use,\n  model/prompt or requirement.\n- **What you can skip.** Deep statistics. One sample-size formula and a Wilson interval are enough to\n  tell \"clears the bar\" from \"might not\".","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Customer go-live of a claims or support agent.** Adjusters or agents score real tasks, and sign-off\n  triggers the pilot-to-production move.\n- **Agents that write to systems of record.** UAT with trace scoring and action gates proves the agent\n  never updates records without approval before it touches production.\n- **Comparing an agent against the manual baseline.** The same cases and rubric scored for both settle\n  whether an agent below an absolute bar is still worth launching.\n- **Seeding the regression suite.** Every UAT failure, with its trace, becomes a case that gates every\n  later prompt, model or tool change.","mini_project":"**Build a small agent UAT harness (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** run 30 cases through a toy tool-calling agent three times each and produce a sign-off report.\n- **Steps:** build an agent with two read tools and one write tool against an in-memory \"claims\" store;\n  write 30 cases with expected end states across two slices; run each case 3 times, saving traces; apply\n  mechanical gates (forbidden tool, unapproved write, step budget), then a rubric label you assign by\n  hand; compute case-level pass rate, Wilson interval, per-slice rates and flip rate.\n- **Done when:** the report states pass rate with its interval against an 85% bar, lists any hard-gate\n  failures, shows the weaker slice, and classifies each failure by cause. Expect 30 cases to be too few\n  to clear the bar (29 of 30 gives a Wilson lower bound near 83%; only 30 of 30 clears it); the report\n  should say so and state how many cases sign-off would need."},"exercise":"**Goal:** design and dry-run the UAT plan for an agent you have built (or the toy claims agent). No\nsolution is given.\n- **Inputs:** a rate criterion and its bar, at least one hard gate on actions, and two or more slices\n  that matter to the user.\n- **Constraints:** the sample size must be justified by a Wilson lower bound, not the point estimate;\n  every case stores an expected end state.\n- **Deliver:** the pre-registered acceptance doc, a case set of at least 40, a harness that runs each case\n  3 times and writes a report, and a defect log with a cause per failure.\n- **Acceptance:** an injected unapproved write shows up as a hard-gate failure; flipping one case's runs\n  changes the flip rate as you predicted; the report states whether the lower bound clears the bar and\n  how many cases sign-off would need.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a contract-review agent flags risky clauses. Legal reviewers score 300 contracts; 279 are\nflagged correctly (93%) against a 90% bar.\n\nEvery contract was run once. 12 of the 21 failures are scanned PDFs, which are 15% of incoming work but\nonly 5% of the sample. Do you sign off?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- computes the interval (roughly 89.5% to 95.4%), so the lower bound sits just under the bar;\n- sees scanned PDFs fail 12 of 15 and are under-sampled: re-weighted to 15% of work, the rate is about\n  85%, well under the bar;\n- re-samples to 15% scanned, triages those failures (likely parsing, not the model) and re-tests that\n  slice;\n- adds repeated runs to measure flips before signing.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Stratified case set\"] --> R[\"Run each case N times\"]\n    R --> S[\"Testers score answer and trace\"]\n    S --> F{\"Case passes?\"}\n    F -->|\"no\"| T{\"Triage by cause\"}\n    T -->|\"integration, retrieval, tool, model\"| X[\"Fix and re-test slice\"]\n    T -->|\"requirement\"| CR[\"Change request to sponsor\"]\n    X --> R\n    F -->|\"yes\"| A[\"Aggregate per slice\"]\n    A --> G{\"Gates clean and CI lower bound above bar?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| M[\"More cases or improve agent\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| SO[\"Named customer sign-off\"]\n    X -.->|\"add to\"| RS[(\"Regression suite\")]","caption":"Each case is run through the full agent loop, scored against the rubric and, if it fails, triaged by\ncause; exit needs clean gates and a confident pass rate."},"interview":[{"q":"How is UAT different from your own eval suite?","a":"- **Who judges:** the customer's real users score outputs on realistic tasks, so misunderstood\n  requirements and missing workflow steps surface; builders' evals only check the builders'\n  understanding.\n- **What it decides:** acceptance is often a contractual milestone, so criteria, rubric and sign-off owner\n  are fixed in advance rather than argued from impressions."},{"q":"Your UAT shows 88% against an 85% bar on 150 cases. Would you sign off?","a":"- **Not on the point estimate alone:** the 95% interval at that size reaches well below 85%, so the data\n  is consistent with being under the bar.\n- **Offer options:** score more cases (around 600 total at 88% clears the bar), explicitly agree to judge\n  on the point estimate, or improve the system.\n- **Check slices first:** a weak slice may matter more than the headline number."},{"q":"How do you run UAT for an agent that takes actions, not just one that answers?","a":"- **Score the trace and end state, not only the reply:** testers see every tool call, and the harness\n  checks the expected end state of the sandboxed systems.\n- **Mechanical hard gates on actions:** a forbidden tool, an unapproved write or a blown step budget fails\n  the run before the rubric counts, and any such failure blocks sign-off."},{"q":"An agent fails a UAT task. How do you decide whether it is a model problem?","a":"- **Triage by cause from the trace:** wrong data from a connector is integration; a document that existed\n  but was not found is retrieval; a wrong tool or malformed argument is tool use; right context but a wrong\n  answer is model/prompt.\n- **Why it matters:** each cause has a different owner and fix, and prompt-tuning around an integration bug\n  hides it until production."},{"q":"When would you judge an agent against a baseline instead of an absolute bar?","a":"- **When a current process exists:** if manual triage is right 80% of the time, an agent at 84% may be\n  worth launching even below an 85% absolute bar.\n- **Cost:** you must measure the baseline on the same cases and rubric, so the comparison is fair, and\n  agree the switch with the sponsor before scoring, not after a near miss.\n- **Hard gates still apply regardless:** a privacy leak or an unapproved write fails the agent however it\n  compares with the manual process."}],"tradeoffs":["**Statistical power vs SME time.** Larger, stratified case sets give meaningful results but consume scarce expert time; when experts are expensive, spend the budget on high-risk slices and accept wider intervals elsewhere.","**Scripted vs exploratory.** Scripted cases make results comparable and repeatable, while exploratory use surfaces unknown workflow needs and odd tool paths; run both, and weight exploratory findings more heavily early in UAT.","**Absolute bar vs baseline.** Strict exit criteria protect the customer, but may block an agent already better than the current manual process; when a baseline exists, judging against it can be the more honest criterion.","**Realism vs lead time.** Production-like data and real tool sandboxes catch real integration failures but need data access approvals that can take weeks; start those at kickoff, not at UAT."],"pitfalls":["**Defining criteria during UAT.** Symptom: each round of fixes surfaces a new expectation nobody wrote down. Diagnose: count criteria added or changed after round one without a change request. Fix: pre-register criteria in scoping.","**Letting the build team score outputs.** Symptom: a high UAT pass rate followed by low adoption and complaints in the first week of hypercare. Fix: the customer's users score.","**Running each case once.** Symptom: a failure engineers \"cannot reproduce\" because a re-run happens to pass. Diagnose: re-run failed cases several times and compute the flip rate. Fix: repeated runs with case-level verdicts.","**Scoring the final answer only.** Symptom: an agent passes UAT, then in production updates the wrong record on the way to a correct-looking reply. Diagnose: diff the sandbox end state against the expected one for every passing case. Fix: score trace and end state, with mechanical gates on actions.","**Treating every change request as a defect.** Symptom: rounds that never converge, scope creep indistinguishable from bugs. Diagnose: chart defects by cause per round. Fix: a requirement cause class.","**Judging on the aggregate only.** Symptom: a passing headline while one slice (a language, a document type) fails badly. Diagnose: per-slice pass rates with their intervals. Fix: per-slice criteria."],"when":"- **Use when:** people other than the builders will use the agent and acceptance carries weight: a\n  customer go-live, a pilot-to-production gate, a contractual milestone.\n- **Plan it during scoping**, because the case set, rubric, environment, tool sandboxes and data approvals\n  take longer than the testing itself.\n- **Go lighter for internal tools** with a small user group: a few power users and a short rubric.\n- **Measure:** pass rate with a confidence interval, per-slice pass rates, hard-gate failures, flip rate,\n  inter-rater agreement and defect counts by cause.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the UAT harness: case loading, repeated runs per case, rubric capture, and export of every failure into the regression suite with its trace attached.","architect":"Defines the UAT environment boundary, ensuring production-like integrations and data without production writes, and owns how acceptance criteria map to measurable signals.","fde":"Recruits the customer's testers, runs calibration on the rubric, triages findings with them, and separates genuine defects from change requests in front of the sponsor."},"check":{"q":"UAT testers rate a summarisation feature at 90% acceptable, but two senior adjusters say it is\nunusable. How do you reconcile this before sign-off?","a":"First check whether the disagreement is about the rubric or the cases. Have the seniors and other\ntesters score the same overlapping outputs; low agreement means \"acceptable\" is defined differently and\nthe rubric needs tightening, perhaps because seniors need a field others ignore. Then check slices: the\nseniors may handle complex claims that are under-represented in the case set. If so, stratify, add cases\nfrom their workload and report per-slice rates. If their concern is a workflow need never in the\ncriteria, record it as a change request with the sponsor rather than a defect, and decide whether it\nblocks."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why split UAT criteria into hard gates and rate thresholds?","options":["Hard gates are tested by engineers and rate thresholds by users","Some failures, like a privacy leak, are unacceptable even once, so they cannot be averaged into a pass rate","Rate thresholds only apply to latency, not quality","Hard gates make UAT shorter by skipping sampling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A pass rate averages over cases, which is right for quality but wrong for safety, privacy or legal failures where a single occurrence is a blocker regardless of how many other cases succeed."},{"q":"UAT shows 88% pass on 200 cases against an 85% bar. What is the most defensible conclusion?","options":["The system passes because 88% exceeds 85%","The system fails because some cases failed","The sample is too small to show the true rate clears 85%, so either test more cases or explicitly agree to judge on the point estimate","The result is invalid because AI outputs are non-deterministic"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The 95% interval for 176 of 200 reaches below 85%, so the data is consistent with being under the bar. Non-determinism does not invalidate sampling; it is one more reason to treat the rate statistically."},{"q":"A tester reports that the system correctly followed the specification, but the specified output format does not fit their filing process. How should it be classified?","options":["As a blocker defect in the model","As a retrieval failure","As a minor integration bug","As a requirement finding, handled as a change request with the sponsor"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The system did what was specified; the specification was wrong or incomplete. Treating it as a defect hides scope change and stops UAT from converging, while a change request makes the cost explicit."}]},{"key":"deployment/rollout-criteria-and-rollback-triggers","sources":[],"why":"- **A staged rollout only reduces risk if someone decides at each stage.** Without written criteria, that\n  is whoever watches the dashboard, when numbers are ambiguous, with a launch date behind them.\n- **The predictable failure is \"probably noise, keep going\"**, then an incident at 100% that was visible\n  at 5%. The opposite also happens: a rollback on pure noise, and the team loses confidence in releasing\n  at all.\n- **AI releases are often not code.** A prompt, model, re-embedded index or retrieval parameter can\n  degrade quality with no rise in errors or latency.\n- **Quality signals are slow and noisy.** Thumbs-down rates, escalations and LLM-judge scores need\n  hundreds of requests and hours or days before a difference beats noise. And some AI actions are\n  irreversible: emails sent or records updated by an agent are not undone by redeploying.\n- **\"Watch the dashboards and use judgement\" fails** because judgement under pressure is biased toward\n  continuing; criteria written in advance make the live decision mechanical.","how":"### 1. Define the stages and what each must prove\n- **A typical ladder:** internal users, then a small percentage or one friendly tenant, then a larger\n  fraction (a quarter), then everyone.\n- **Minimum duration per stage**, because traffic varies by hour and weekday; a stage that only saw\n  Sunday-night traffic proved little about Monday morning.\n- **Minimum sample per stage** (requests, sessions or completed agent tasks), because rare failures\n  cannot be observed in small samples.\n\nError counts are roughly Poisson: with *n* requests at baseline rate *p* you expect *np* errors with a\nstandard deviation of about √(np).\n\n| Requests at a 1% baseline | Expected errors | Doubled rate (2%) | Distinguishable? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 100 | about 1 (±1) | about 2 | No: a canary at 2% is indistinguishable from noise |\n| 3,000 | about 30 (±5.5) | about 60 | Yes: a gap of more than five standard deviations |\n\n- **Derive the minimum sample** from the baseline rate and the smallest regression you care about, not\n  as a round number.\n\n### Quality signals need far bigger samples\nThe regressions that matter are a few points on a rate near 90%, not a doubling. To detect an LLM-judge\npass rate falling from 91% to 88% at the usual significance (5%) and power (80%):\n\n```\nn per arm ≈ (z_α/2 + z_β)² · (p₁q₁ + p₂q₂) / (p₁ − p₂)²\n          = (1.96 + 0.84)² · (0.91·0.09 + 0.88·0.12) / 0.03² ≈ 1,600 judged responses\n```\n\n- **That counts judged responses, not requests.** Judging 10% of traffic means about 16,000 requests\n  before a 3-point quality drop is reliably visible.\n- **So quality criteria force longer bake times than error criteria**, and a stage with \"enough requests\"\n  may still have too few judgements to decide anything about quality.\n- **For agents, count tasks, not model calls.** One task may make ten LLM calls; the unit that succeeds or\n  fails, and that the judge scores, is the task.\n\n### 2. Compare against a baseline, not an absolute\n- **Run old and new side by side** on comparable traffic, so seasonal or upstream effects hit both.\n- **Express criteria as relative regressions** (\"canary p95 latency no more than 20% above control\")\n  **plus absolute floors**, so tiny baselines do not produce absurd ratios.\n\n### 3. Split triggers into hard and soft\n| | Hard triggers | Soft triggers |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Signals** | Error rate, timeouts, p95 or p99 latency, guardrail block rate, cost per request or task, step-budget exhaustion rate, tool-call error rate, provider 429s, crash loops | Online LLM-judge score on sampled traffic, user feedback rate, escalation-to-human rate, task completion rate |\n| **Nature** | Fast to measure, unambiguous | Slower, noisier |\n| **Action** | Automated, immediate rollback | Set a *hold*; a named person decides within a set time |\n\n- **Agent cost is a hard signal.** A model that takes 50% more steps per task shows up immediately in\n  cost per task and tokens per task, long before any judge score moves.\n\n### 4. Make the outcome one of three states\n- **Promote:** all criteria met, duration and sample reached.\n- **Hold:** not enough evidence yet, or a soft trigger needs review.\n- **Roll back:** any hard trigger tripped after the minimum sample.\n- **The explicit hold state** prevents both premature promotion and panic rollbacks.\n\n### 5. Make rollback cheap and rehearsed\n- **Version every behaviour-changing artefact:** prompt, model identifier (pinned, never a floating\n  alias), tool definitions, retrieval config, index.\n- **Ship behind a flag** so rollback is a config change, not a redeploy.\n- **Keep the old index serving** until the new one has baked, because re-embedding a corpus can take hours\n  and is not a rollback you can do mid-incident.\n- **Fence agent side effects:** gate write actions behind approval or dry-run during early stages, because\n  no trigger can un-send an email.\n\n### 6. Decide roll forward versus roll back\n- **Roll back by default**, because it restores a known state.\n- **Roll forward with a fix only** when rollback is impossible (a schema migration already applied, an\n  external contract already changed) or the old version is itself broken by an external change, such as a\n  deprecated provider model.","example":"### Stage gate with hard, soft and evidence checks\nA stage gate evaluated every 15 minutes during a canary of a new prompt and model version. The numbers\nare illustrative thresholds a team might agree, not recommendations.\n\n```python\ndef evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage, min_minutes=120, min_requests=3000, min_judged=1600):\n    # Hard triggers: checked as soon as there is enough data to trust them.\n    if canary[\"requests\"] >= min_requests:\n        err_limit = max(2 * control[\"error_rate\"], 0.01)          # relative, with an absolute floor\n        if canary[\"error_rate\"] > err_limit:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"error rate\"\n        if canary[\"p95_ms\"] > 1.2 * control[\"p95_ms\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"p95 latency\"\n        if canary[\"cost_per_req\"] > 1.3 * control[\"cost_per_req\"]:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"cost per request\"\n        if canary[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] > control[\"guardrail_block_rate\"] + 0.02:\n            return \"ROLLBACK\", \"guardrail blocks\"\n    # Not enough evidence yet: neither promote nor roll back.\n    if (canary[\"requests\"] < min_requests or minutes_in_stage < min_minutes\n            or canary[\"judged\"] < min_judged):\n        return \"HOLD\", \"collecting evidence\"\n    # Soft trigger: sampled LLM-judge quality needs a human if it drops.\n    if canary[\"judge_pass\"] < control[\"judge_pass\"] - 0.03:\n        return \"HOLD\", \"quality drop: page release owner\"\n    return \"PROMOTE\", \"all criteria met\"\n\ncontrol = {\"error_rate\": 0.004, \"p95_ms\": 4100, \"cost_per_req\": 0.011,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.006, \"judge_pass\": 0.91, \"judged\": 5000}\ncanary  = {\"requests\": 18000, \"judged\": 1800, \"error_rate\": 0.005, \"p95_ms\": 4500, \"cost_per_req\": 0.012,\n           \"guardrail_block_rate\": 0.007, \"judge_pass\": 0.86}\nprint(evaluate(canary, control, minutes_in_stage=240))\n# ('HOLD', 'quality drop: page release owner')\n```\n\n- **No hard trigger fires.** Errors, latency (4500 is under 1.2 × 4100 = 4920) and cost are within limits.\n- **The judge pass rate fell five points.** With 1,800 judged canary responses (10% of 18,000 requests)\n  against 5,000 on control, the standard error of the difference is just under one point, so a five-point\n  drop is more than five standard errors: very unlikely to be noise.\n- **It still holds and pages a human** rather than rolling back, because a judge-score drop can also mean\n  the judge misreads the new model's style (for example shorter answers it penalises). The human reads a\n  sample of failing judgements and decides.\n- **The judged-sample floor matters.** Had the same drop appeared after only 200 judgements, the gate\n  would have returned \"collecting evidence\".\n\n### Agent-specific hard triggers from task traces\nFor a tool-calling agent, add task-level signals computed from traces. The numbers are illustrative.\n\n```python\ndef agent_triggers(canary, control):\n    if canary[\"tasks\"] < 1000:                                   # task-level minimum sample\n        return \"HOLD\", \"collecting tasks\"\n    if canary[\"cost_per_task\"] > 1.3 * control[\"cost_per_task\"]:\n        return \"ROLLBACK\", \"cost per task\"\n    if canary[\"step_budget_hit_rate\"] > max(2 * control[\"step_budget_hit_rate\"], 0.01):\n        return \"ROLLBACK\", \"agent hitting max steps (looping)\"\n    if canary[\"tool_error_rate\"] > max(2 * control[\"tool_error_rate\"], 0.01):\n        return \"ROLLBACK\", \"tool-call errors (bad arguments or schema drift)\"\n    return \"OK\", \"no agent hard trigger\"\n\ncontrol = {\"tasks\": 9000, \"cost_per_task\": 0.062, \"steps_per_task\": 6.1,\n           \"step_budget_hit_rate\": 0.004, \"tool_error_rate\": 0.008}\ncanary  = {\"tasks\": 1400, \"cost_per_task\": 0.094, \"steps_per_task\": 9.3,\n           \"step_budget_hit_rate\": 0.021, \"tool_error_rate\": 0.009}\nprint(agent_triggers(canary, control))   # ('ROLLBACK', 'cost per task')\n```\n\n| Signal | Control | Canary | Limit | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Steps per task | 6.1 | 9.3 | (watched, not a trigger) | about 52% more steps |\n| Cost per task | $0.062 | $0.094 | 1.3 × 0.062 ≈ $0.081 | Trips: roll back |\n| Step-budget hit rate | 0.4% | 2.1% | max(0.8%, 1%) = 1% | Would also trip |\n| Tool error rate | 0.8% | 0.9% | max(1.6%, 1%) = 1.6% | Within limit |\n\n- **No HTTP error moved**, yet the new model loops more and costs about 52% more per task. Request-level\n  gates would have promoted it.\n\n### Deriving the minimum request count\nRequire the expected gap between baseline and regressed error counts to exceed *k* standard deviations\nof the baseline count: n·p·(r − 1) ≥ k·√(n·p), so n ≥ k² / (p·(r − 1)²).\n\n```python\nfrom math import ceil\n\ndef min_requests(baseline_rate, regression_ratio, k=5):\n    \"\"\"Requests needed before a regression_ratio-fold error rise stands k sigma above noise.\"\"\"\n    return ceil(k * k / (baseline_rate * (regression_ratio - 1) ** 2))\n\nprint(min_requests(0.01, 2))     # 2500  -> 3,000 is a sensible rounded-up floor\nprint(min_requests(0.004, 2))    # 6250  -> lower baselines need more traffic\nprint(min_requests(0.01, 1.5))   # 10000 -> smaller regressions cost quadratically more\n```","patterns":["**Metric-driven canary controller.** On Kubernetes, Argo Rollouts or Flagger step traffic weights and run analysis queries against Prometheus between steps, aborting when a metric fails its threshold. Use them for hard triggers (error rate, p95, 429s); monitor aborted rollouts and time spent per step.","**Flag-based percentage rollout for prompts and models.** Keep prompt version and pinned model ID in a feature-flag or config service, roll out by percentage or tenant, and roll back by flipping the flag. Monitor flag change events alongside the quality dashboard so every shift is attributable.","**Task-level agent metrics on canary and control.** Tag every trace with the release version and compute cost per task, steps per task, step-budget hits and tool-call error rate per arm. Monitor these next to request metrics; they catch looping and path regressions that HTTP metrics miss.","**Judged-sample floor on quality gates.** Route a fixed fraction of canary and control traffic to an LLM judge and refuse to promote until the judged count reaches the computed floor (about 1,600 per arm for a 3-point drop near 90%). Monitor judged count per stage, not just request count.","**Old index kept warm.** Serve the new index behind a config switch while the previous index stays deployed until the bake ends. Monitor retrieval hit-rate and judge scores per index version.","**Write-action fencing in early stages.** In internal and first-tenant stages, agent tools with side effects run in dry-run or behind human approval. Monitor how many write actions would have executed, to size the blast radius before enabling them."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most agent releases are not code: a new prompt, model version, tool\n  description or retrieval setting. They can make the agent take worse paths, loop longer or cost more\n  per task with no rise in HTTP errors, and some of its actions (emails, record updates) cannot be undone.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A feature flag or config service holding prompt version and pinned\n  model ID, a canary controller or gate script evaluated on a timer, an LLM judge on sampled traffic, and\n  per-task metrics from your agent traces.\n- **What you control.** Which agent signals are hard triggers (error rate, cost per task, step-budget\n  exhaustion, guardrail blocks), which are soft (judge score, task completion), minimum samples, and\n  whether write tools are fenced in early stages.\n- **What you can skip.** Building your own traffic-shifting controller; use the flag service or an\n  existing canary tool.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Swapping the model under a production agent.** Staged rollout with cost-per-task and step triggers\n  catches a model that loops or over-calls tools before it reaches every tenant.\n- **Shipping a prompt or tool-description change.** A judged-sample floor decides whether a few points of\n  quality loss are real before promotion.\n- **Enabling a new write tool.** Early stages run it in dry-run, counting would-be writes to size the blast\n  radius before turning it on.","mini_project":"**Canary gate for an agent release (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a gate script that returns promote, hold or roll back for a new agent version from simulated\n  task traces.\n- **Steps:** generate synthetic traces for control and canary (steps, cost, tool errors, a judge verdict\n  per task); implement hard triggers for cost per task and step-budget hits, a task minimum, and a soft\n  judge trigger with a judged-sample floor; run it every simulated 15 minutes as traffic accumulates.\n- **Done when:** a looping canary rolls back on cost, a canary with a five-point judge drop holds and\n  \"pages\", a clean canary promotes only after its floors are met, and each decision prints its reason."},"exercise":"**Goal:** write and test the rollout plan and gate for one real or simulated agent release (a model\nswap or prompt change). No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** baseline rates from your traces or a simulator: error rate, cost and steps per task,\n  step-budget hits, judge pass rate.\n- **Constraints:** every minimum sample is derived from a baseline and the smallest regression you care\n  about, and every trigger is hard or soft, with relative limits plus absolute floors.\n- **Deliver:** a stage ladder with durations and floors, a gate returning promote, hold or roll back with\n  a reason, and a replay of simulated traffic through it.\n- **Acceptance:** a canary looping 50% more rolls back on a hard trigger; a 3-point judge drop holds once\n  the judged floor is met, not before; a clean canary promotes only after every floor.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a sales agent drafts and sends outreach emails. You are swapping its model; it gets about\n400 tasks a day across 6 tenants, one tenant is half the volume, and email-send errors run near 0.5%.\n\nDesign the stages and triggers. What can the canary prove in a week, and what can it not?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- computes that a doubled 0.5% error rate needs about 5,000 tasks (`min_requests(0.005, 2)`), over 12\n  days of total traffic, so error triggers alone cannot decide in a week;\n- leans on fast hard signals (cost and steps per task), judges every canary task since volume is low,\n  and shadow-runs the new model on real tasks (draft, never send) for evidence without exposure;\n- admits a 1,600-per-arm judged floor is also over a week away and small tenants stay underpowered;\n- fences sends behind dry-run or approval in early stages, because a sent email cannot be rolled back;\n- pins the model and keeps the old version behind the flag.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Evaluation tick, every 15 min\"] --> S{\"Minimum tasks reached?\"}\n    S -->|\"yes\"| H{\"Any hard trigger tripped?\"}\n    H -->|\"yes\"| RB[\"Roll back via flag\"]\n    H -->|\"no\"| E{\"Duration and judged sample met?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| HO[\"Hold: collecting evidence\"]\n    E -->|\"no\"| HO\n    E -->|\"yes\"| Q{\"Judge pass rate dropped?\"}\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| HP[\"Hold: page release owner\"]\n    Q -->|\"no\"| P[\"Promote to next stage\"]\n    HO -.-> T\n    P -.->|\"next stage\"| T","caption":"Every evaluation tick runs hard triggers first, then the evidence floors, then the soft quality check;\nonly a clean pass promotes the new agent version."},"interview":[{"q":"Why have an explicit \"hold\" state instead of just promote or roll back?","a":"- **Evidence often is not there yet:** before the minimum sample, neither promoting nor rolling back is\n  justified by data.\n- **Soft signals need a human:** a judge-score drop may be a real regression or the judge misreading a new\n  style, so hold and page a named owner rather than automating either way."},{"q":"A prompt change rolled out green on errors and latency, then complaints arrived. What was missing?","a":"- **Quality signals:** prompt and model changes can degrade answers with no error or latency change.\n- **Fix the gate:** add sampled LLM-judge scoring on canary and control as a soft trigger, with a judged\n  sample floor (about 1,600 per arm to see 91% falling to 88%)."},{"q":"A new model makes your agent take 50% more steps per task. Which trigger should catch it?","a":"- **A hard cost or step trigger, not the judge:** cost per task and step-budget exhaustion are fast and\n  unambiguous, so compare them per arm and roll back automatically past a relative limit.\n- **Instrument at task level:** tag traces with the release version, because request-level metrics hide\n  that one task now makes nine model calls instead of six."},{"q":"What does rollback not undo for an agent, and how do you design around it?","a":"- **Side effects:** emails sent or records updated by the bad version stay in customer systems after a\n  clean revert.\n- **Design:** dry-run or approval-gated write tools in early stages, and log every external write with the\n  release version so cleanup is possible."}],"tradeoffs":["**Trustworthy decisions vs release speed.** Long bake times and large minimum samples make decisions trustworthy but slow every release; the balance flips toward speed for low-risk changes behind flags and toward caution for model swaps under an agent with tools.","**Reaction time vs thrash.** Automated rollback reacts in minutes but can thrash on noisy metrics; reserve automation for hard, low-noise signals and route noisy quality signals to a human hold.","**Robust comparison vs cost.** Relative thresholds against a live control absorb traffic shifts, but require running two versions at once, which doubles some costs and is awkward for stateful or per-tenant releases, such as agents with long-lived memory.","**Blast radius vs signal.** Rolling out by tenant gives clean blast-radius control and clearer attribution, but small tenants produce too little traffic to judge, so per-tenant stages need longer durations."],"pitfalls":["**Writing criteria after the rollout starts.** Symptom: thresholds that conveniently sit just above whatever the canary is currently showing. Diagnose: compare the commit time of the gate config with the rollout start. Fix: commit criteria with the release plan.","**Judging a stage before its minimum sample.** Symptom: releases promoted on a quiet hour and incidents that start when peak traffic arrives. Diagnose: log requests, tasks and judged count at each promotion. Fix: enforce duration and sample floors in the gate.","**Monitoring only errors and latency for a prompt or model change.** Symptom: a green rollout, then a week of complaints about worse answers, or a cost bill up by half. Diagnose: plot judge pass rate, cost and steps per task per release version. Fix: sampled quality as a soft trigger, cost and steps as hard.","**Pointing at a floating model alias.** The provider changes the model underneath you; symptom: a quality shift no release on your side explains and rollback does not fix. Diagnose: log the provider-returned model version per call. Fix: pin model versions.","**Assuming rollback undoes everything.** Symptom: a clean revert while records written or messages sent by the bad version remain in customer systems. Fix: fence write actions early."],"when":"- **Use for any behaviour-changing release:** code, prompt, model, tool definitions, retrieval\n  configuration, index, guardrail policy, tool set.\n- **Scale the ladder to risk:** a copy change behind a flag may need only a short internal stage; a model\n  swap for an agent with write tools needs staged tenants, a long bake and human-reviewed quality gates.\n- **Measure** what the customer feels (errors, latency, cost, task success) and what predicts it (judge\n  score on sampled traffic, escalations, steps per task).","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the gate logic, version pinning and flags, and wires metrics so the canary and control are comparable on the same dashboard and alert rules.","architect":"Decides which artefacts are versioned and independently rollbackable, which signals are hard triggers, and how irreversible actions are fenced during early rollout stages.","fde":"Agrees the stages and thresholds with the customer before go-live, including who on their side can call a rollback and how affected users are told."},"check":{"q":"A new model version is at 10% of traffic. Error rate and latency are fine, but the thumbs-down\nrate is 4.1% versus 3.2% on control after 900 rated responses. Roll back, hold or promote?","a":"Hold, not promote, and probably not an automatic rollback yet. Thumbs-down is a noisy soft\nsignal: 900 ratings at 4.1% is about 37 negatives against about 29 expected at the control rate. The\nstandard error of a rate near 4% on 900 ratings is roughly 0.65 points (more once the control's own\nnoise is added), so the 0.9-point gap is at most about 1.4 standard errors, well within chance, before even counting bias from which users choose to rate. I would extend the stage until the sample\nis large enough, read a sample of the negative responses to see whether they share a cause, and run the\nLLM judge on matched traffic for a less biased signal. If the failures cluster on a real regression,\nroll back; if they are spread and the gap closes with more data, continue."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should rollback triggers be written before the rollout begins?","options":["So they can be loaded into the deployment tool's configuration file","Because decisions made while watching ambiguous live numbers are biased toward continuing the release","Because thresholds cannot be changed once traffic is flowing","Because auditors require a document for every deployment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-committed thresholds move the reasoning to a calm moment. In the moment, sunk cost and schedule pressure make \"probably noise\" the default reading of a bad number, which is how 5% problems reach 100%."},{"q":"Which signal is the best candidate for an automated, immediate rollback trigger?","options":["Average user satisfaction rating from in-app surveys","LLM-judge quality score on 50 sampled responses","Error rate exceeding twice the control rate after the minimum request count","Number of support tickets opened this week"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Error rate is fast, low-noise and unambiguous once a minimum sample exists. Survey ratings, small judge samples and tickets are slow or noisy, so they should trigger a human-reviewed hold rather than automation."},{"q":"A rollout re-embedded the whole corpus with a new embedding model. What makes rollback safe?","options":["Re-running the old embedding model over the corpus if quality drops","Pinning the new embedding model version","Rolling back the application code only","Keeping the old index serving in parallel until the new one has passed its bake period"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Re-embedding takes hours, so it is not a mid-incident rollback. Keeping the old index live lets rollback be a configuration switch, while code-only rollback would query vectors from an incompatible model."}]},{"key":"deployment/runbooks","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy, 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"- **Fix-it knowledge lives in a few heads.** Which dashboard shows the real problem, which flag switches\n  to the fallback model, which log query finds the failing tenant. That works until those engineers are\n  asleep, on holiday, or on another project.\n- **Forward-deployed work fails structurally.** After handoff the customer's operations team is on call\n  for an agent they did not build, often in an environment the vendor cannot access. At 3 a.m. under\n  pressure nobody reasons well from first principles.\n- **\"The on-call will figure it out\" fails.** Every incident becomes an investigation, stretching time to\n  mitigation from minutes to hours while users are affected.\n- **An architecture document fails too.** It explains how the system works, not what to do when the\n  provider returns 429s, and nobody reads twenty pages during an outage.\n- **AI adds unfamiliar failure modes.** Provider degradation or rate limits, cost spikes from a prompt loop\n  or abusive user, guardrail-block surges, a stale or partially rebuilt index, jailbreak or\n  prompt-injection reports, confident wrong answers after an upstream change. Some responses (pausing\n  write tools, notifying security) need explicit permission, which the runbook writes down in advance.","how":"### One situation per runbook\n- **One alert or symptom, not a whole service**, because the reader arrives from a specific page and\n  needs the steps for that situation, not a tour.\n- **Every alert links to its runbook.** An alert with no runbook means the alert is unnecessary or the\n  knowledge is missing.\n\n### A fixed structure\n1. **Trigger:** the alert name and threshold, or the user-reported symptom.\n2. **Impact:** who is affected and how badly, with a severity guide, because severity decides who is\n   woken up and how fast customers are told.\n3. **Confirm:** one or two checks that tell a real problem from a false alarm, with exact dashboard links\n   or queries.\n4. **Mitigate:** the steps that stop user harm, in order, with exact commands or flag names. Mitigation\n   comes before diagnosis because restoring service is urgent and root cause can wait for the postmortem.\n5. **Verify:** what metric must return to normal, and for how long, before declaring recovery.\n6. **Escalate:** who to call if mitigation fails or the situation does not match, with names or rotas,\n   not \"the platform team\".\n7. **Afterwards:** what to record for the postmortem (timeline, traces, request IDs).\n\n### Precision over prose\n- **Imperative, copy-pasteable steps:** the flag name, the query, the expected output.\n- **Explicit decision points:** \"if 429s are from one tenant, go to step 6; otherwise continue\".\n- **No vague steps.** \"Check the logs\" is where incidents stall.\n\n### Pre-authorised actions\n- **State what the on-call may do without asking:** switch to a fallback model, disable an agent's write\n  tools, block a tenant, shed load.\n- **Why:** waiting for a manager's approval at night extends the incident.\n\n### Agent-specific runbooks to write first\n| Situation | Typical trigger | First mitigation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cost runaway or loop** | Cost per task or step-budget hits above threshold | Lower the max-step limit, disable the looping tool, cap the tenant |\n| **Tool misbehaving** | Tool-call error rate up, or a tool returning malformed data | Disable that tool; the agent answers without it or hands off to a human |\n| **Unsafe or wrong write actions** | User report, audit alert on unusual writes | Switch write tools to approval-only or read-only mode, export affected run IDs |\n| **Prompt injection reported** | Report, guardrail alert on tool results | Disable the source tool or connector, preserve the traces, notify security |\n\n### Automation is the next step, not a replacement\n- **Promote repeated steps:** a step run identically every time becomes a script, then automatic\n  remediation.\n- **Keep the runbook after automating**, describing what the automation does and what to do when it\n  fails, because automation fails in exactly the unusual situations where humans need context.\n\n### Store them where the outage is not\n- **Do not share fate with the system:** not only on a wiki behind the same single sign-on that may be\n  failing, and not only in a cloud account the customer's on-call cannot access.\n- **Keep an offline or mirrored copy** for scenarios (identity outage, network partition into an\n  air-gapped site) where the primary location goes down too.\n- **The alert carries the link**, because searching costs minutes.\n\n### Runbook vs playbook\n- **Terminology varies:** some teams say *playbook* for the broader incident-handling process (roles,\n  communication, severity) and *runbook* for the per-situation procedure described here.\n- **Have both**, and do not expect one to do the other's job.\n\n### Keep them alive\n- **Owner and last-tested date** on every runbook.\n- **Rehearse in game days** (deliberately injected failures in staging), and update after every incident\n  where the runbook was wrong or missing.\n- **Untested means broken:** a runbook that has never been executed should be assumed not to work.","example":"### An LLM provider-degraded runbook\nAn abridged runbook for an LLM-backed agent, written for a customer operations team after handoff. Flag\nnames, dashboards and thresholds are illustrative stand-ins for the team's own.\n\n```markdown\n### RB-07 LLM provider degraded (429s / 5xx / timeouts)\nTrigger: alert `llm_upstream_error_rate > 5% for 5 min` (any tenant)\nImpact: answers fail or slow down for all users. SEV2; SEV1 if > 25% for 15 min.\n\nConfirm\n1. Dashboard \"LLM upstream\" -> panel \"errors by status code\".\n   429 = rate limit, 5xx/timeout = provider outage. Check the provider status page.\n2. Panel \"requests by tenant\": is one tenant > 50% of traffic? If yes, go to step 6.\n\nMitigate (pre-authorised, no approval needed)\n3. 5xx/timeouts: set flag `llm.route=fallback` (secondary provider/region). Expect\n   slightly lower answer quality; banner `degraded_mode=on` informs users.\n4. 429s, no single hot tenant: set `llm.max_concurrency=40` (default 80) to stop retry storms.\n5. Pause batch jobs: set `jobs.summarise_nightly.enabled=false`.\n6. One tenant flooding: set `tenant.<id>.rate_limit_rps=2`; notify their admin (template T-3).\n\nVerify\n7. Upstream error rate < 1% and p95 < 8 s for 15 min. Then revert step 3 first, watch 15 min,\n   then restore concurrency, then re-enable batch jobs last.\n\nEscalate\n8. Not recovered 30 min after step 3, or fallback also failing: page vendor on-call\n   (rota \"assistant-l3\"), open a ticket with the provider quoting request IDs from the trace.\nAfterwards: export the incident timeline and five failing trace IDs to the postmortem doc.\n```\n\nWhat it encodes:\n- **A rate-limit vs outage split**, because the same alert has opposite fixes. For an outage you move\n  traffic away; for rate limiting you reduce your own pressure, and failing over a rate-limited workload\n  can simply exhaust the fallback's quota too.\n- **A hot-tenant branch** and **actions the operator may take without approval**.\n- **An explicit recovery condition** before anything is reverted.\n- **A deliberate revert order:** one change at a time with a watch period, so if errors return you know\n  which reverted change they depended on. The most load-generating change (batch jobs) comes back last,\n  when the system has proven it is stable.\n\n### An agent cost-runaway runbook\nThe agent-specific counterpart: a loop or a misbehaving tool drives cost per task up. Again, flag names\nand thresholds are illustrative stand-ins for the levers your own agent exposes.\n\n```markdown\n### RB-12 Agent cost runaway / looping\nTrigger: alert `agent_cost_per_task > 2x 7-day median for 15 min`, or step-budget hits > 2% of tasks\nImpact: spend climbs, tasks time out at the step limit. SEV2; SEV1 if daily budget will be hit today.\n\nConfirm\n1. Dashboard \"Agent tasks\" -> \"steps per task\" and \"tool calls by tool\": did one tool's call count jump?\n2. Open three runs at the step limit (trace search: status=step_limit, last 1 h).\n   Same tool called repeatedly with similar arguments = loop. Go to step 3.\n\nMitigate (pre-authorised)\n3. Disable the looping tool: set `tools.<name>.enabled=false`. The agent answers without it or hands off.\n4. If no single tool: lower `agent.max_steps` from 15 to 8 for all tenants.\n5. One tenant driving the spend: set `tenant.<id>.daily_task_cap` to its 7-day average.\n\nVerify\n6. Cost per task back within 1.2x median and step-budget hits < 0.5% for 30 min.\n   Re-enable the tool on the internal tenant first, watch 30 min, then everyone.\n\nEscalate\n7. Not recovered 30 min after step 3 or 4: page vendor on-call (rota \"assistant-l3\") with trace IDs.\nAfterwards: attach three looping traces and the tool's recent changes to the postmortem.\n```\n\n- **The confirm step reads traces, not logs**, because a loop is visible as a repeated tool call in one\n  run, not as an error line.\n- **Disabling one tool is the smallest lever**, which is why the agent must be built to degrade (answer\n  without it or hand off) rather than crash when a tool is missing.\n\n### Linking alerts to runbooks, and linting the link\nPrometheus alerting rules conventionally carry a `runbook_url` annotation, so the page that wakes the\non-call contains the link.\n\n```yaml\ngroups:\n  - name: assistant\n    rules:\n      - alert: LLMUpstreamErrorRate\n        expr: llm_upstream_error_rate > 0.05\n        for: 5m\n        labels:\n          severity: sev2\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"LLM upstream errors above 5% for 5 minutes\"\n          runbook_url: \"https://runbooks.example.internal/RB-07\"\n```\n\nA CI check fails the build when an alert has no runbook or a runbook has gone untested too long.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nalerts = [  # parsed from the alert rules file\n    {\"alert\": \"LLMUpstreamErrorRate\", \"runbook_url\": \"https://runbooks.example.internal/RB-07\"},\n    {\"alert\": \"GuardrailBlockSurge\"},                       # no runbook link\n]\nrunbooks = {\"RB-07\": {\"owner\": \"cust-ops\", \"last_tested\": date(2026, 3, 2)}}\n\ndef lint(alerts, runbooks, today, max_age_days=180):\n    problems = []\n    for a in alerts:\n        url = a.get(\"runbook_url\")\n        if not url:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: no runbook_url\")\n            continue\n        rb = runbooks.get(url.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1])\n        if rb is None:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: runbook {url} not found\")\n        elif (today - rb[\"last_tested\"]).days > max_age_days:\n            problems.append(f\"{a['alert']}: runbook last tested {rb['last_tested']}\")\n    return problems\n\nprint(lint(alerts, runbooks, date(2026, 10, 20)))\n# ['LLMUpstreamErrorRate: runbook last tested 2026-03-02', 'GuardrailBlockSurge: no runbook_url']\n```","patterns":["**Alert-embedded runbook links.** Put the runbook URL in every paging alert (for example the `runbook_url` annotation in Prometheus rules) so it travels into the pager notification. Monitor the share of paging alerts without a link; target zero.","**Levers built into the agent.** For every runbook step, the agent exposes a flag: per-tool disable, max-step override, read-only mode for write tools, fallback model, per-tenant caps. Monitor runbooks that reference a lever that does not exist (they should fail review).","**Game days before handoff.** Inject the failure in staging (block the provider endpoint, throttle a connector, corrupt an index alias, make a tool return malformed data) and have the customer's on-call run the runbook unaided. Monitor time to mitigate and every step where they hesitated.","**Runbook review in the postmortem template.** Each postmortem asks \"was the runbook right, missing or wrong?\" and opens an update task. Monitor incidents where the runbook was wrong.","**Mirrored copy outside the blast radius.** Export runbooks to a location that does not depend on the system's identity provider or network, refreshed on every change. Monitor the mirror's last sync time.","**Step-to-script promotion.** When a step is run identically in several incidents, wrap it in a script or chat-ops command and reference that command from the runbook. Monitor how often automated remediations fail and fall back to manual steps."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents fail in ways a generic service runbook never covers: a loop that\n  burns budget, a tool returning garbage the agent trusts, a prompt injection arriving through a retrieved\n  document, a write tool updating the wrong records. Each needs a lever and a written step to pull it.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A `runbook_url` on every paging alert, flags for fallback model,\n  per-tool disable and per-tenant limits, and trace queries that find the failing run by ID.\n- **What you control.** Which levers the agent exposes (kill switch per tool, max-step override, read-only\n  mode), which actions the on-call may take without approval, and the trace fields responders need.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing a runbook per code path. Write one per alert or symptom, starting with\n  provider outage, rate limits, cost runaway and unsafe actions.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Handing an agent to a customer's on-call team.** Runbooks in their tools and access model let them\n  mitigate provider outages and agent loops without escalating.\n- **Prompt injection through a connector.** A pre-authorised \"disable the connector, preserve traces,\n  notify security\" procedure removes the need for a 3 a.m. approval call.\n- **Readiness reviews.** \"Every paging alert links to a tested runbook\" is a blocking checklist item.","mini_project":"**Write and rehearse two agent runbooks (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** prove a stranger can stop an agent loop and a provider outage using only your runbooks.\n- **Steps:** add three flags to a toy agent (per-tool disable, `max_steps`, fallback model); write RB-07\n  style runbooks for \"provider degraded\" and \"agent looping\"; inject each failure (a stub tool that always\n  asks to be called again, a stub provider returning 429s); ask a colleague, or yourself a day later, to\n  follow the runbook without reading the code.\n- **Done when:** both failures are mitigated within 10 minutes using only the runbook, every step that\n  caused hesitation is rewritten, and a lint script confirms each alert links to a runbook tested in the\n  last 180 days."},"exercise":"**Goal:** write one agent runbook a stranger can execute, and prove it in a game day. No solution is\ngiven.\n- **Inputs:** an agent you have built, or a toy one with two tools; pick \"tool misbehaving\" or\n  \"prompt injection reported\" from the table above.\n- **Constraints:** every step names an exact flag, query or command that exists today; the confirm step\n  separates a real problem from a false alarm; pre-authorised actions are listed.\n- **Deliver:** the runbook, any levers you had to add to the agent, the alert rule carrying its\n  `runbook_url`, and a game-day log.\n- **Acceptance:** someone who did not build the agent mitigates the injected failure within 15 minutes\n  using only the runbook; the lint script passes; every hesitation in the log led to an edit.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** an IT-ops agent can restart services and close tickets. The alert \"guardrail block rate\nabove 3x baseline for 10 min\" fires at 2 a.m.; the customer's on-call has a read-only console and no vendor\naccess.\n\nOutline the runbook: which branches does confirm need, and which mitigations are pre-authorised?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- confirm splits an attack (one tenant or source, injected text in tool results), a guardrail or model\n  change (blocks across all tenants just after a release), and a real shift in user requests;\n- pre-authorises reversible levers exposed to that read-only console (an admin toggle or chat-ops\n  command): disable the source connector, switch restart and close tools to approval-only;\n- rolls back the guardrail policy only on the release branch, never while an attack is suspected;\n- preserves traces and notifies security for a suspected injection;\n- names the escalation rota and the recovery metric.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Alert fires with runbook link\"] --> C{\"Confirm: real problem?\"}\n    C -->|\"no\"| FA[\"Record false alarm, tune alert\"]\n    C -->|\"yes\"| B{\"Which branch matches?\"}\n    B -->|\"provider outage\"| M1[\"Fail over to fallback\"]\n    B -->|\"rate limit\"| M2[\"Cut own concurrency\"]\n    B -->|\"agent looping\"| M3[\"Lower step limit, disable tool\"]\n    B -->|\"no match\"| E[\"Escalate to named rota\"]\n    M1 --> V{\"Recovered for watch period?\"}\n    M2 --> V\n    M3 --> V\n    V -->|\"no\"| E\n    V -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Revert one change at a time\"]\n    R --> P[\"Record timeline for postmortem\"]","caption":"The responder's path through one runbook: confirm before acting, mitigate before diagnosing, and\nescalate when the steps do not match or do not work."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes a runbook different from system documentation?","a":"- **Scope:** one alert or symptom with trigger, impact, confirm, mitigate, verify and escalate steps,\n  rather than an explanation of how the system works.\n- **Why it matters:** the responder is often not the builder, and under pressure needs exact commands, not\n  a twenty-page tour."},{"q":"Your provider alert fires with 429s. Would you fail over to the fallback provider?","a":"- **Not first:** 429s mean you are being rate-limited, so the fix is reducing your own pressure (lower\n  concurrency, pause batch jobs, throttle a hot tenant).\n- **Failing over can backfire:** the same load can exhaust the fallback's quota too. Failover fits 5xx and\n  timeouts, which signal a provider outage."},{"q":"Your agent's cost per task doubled overnight. Walk me through the runbook.","a":"- **Confirm from traces:** open runs that hit the step limit and look for one tool called repeatedly with\n  similar arguments, and check per-tool call counts on the dashboard.\n- **Mitigate with the smallest lever:** disable that tool or lower the max-step limit, cap a hot tenant,\n  then verify cost and step-budget hits return to baseline before re-enabling on one tenant first."},{"q":"What must you build into an agent so its runbooks can actually be executed?","a":"- **Levers per risk:** a disable flag per tool, a read-only mode for write tools, a max-step override, a\n  fallback model route and per-tenant caps, all changeable without a redeploy.\n- **Graceful degradation and traceability:** the agent must keep working (or hand off) with a tool\n  disabled, and every run needs a searchable trace ID so the confirm step can find it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Speed for strangers vs staleness.** Detailed, command-level runbooks make incidents fast for unfamiliar operators but go stale quickly as the agent changes; the cost flips when nobody owns updates, and a wrong runbook is worse than none.","**Fast mitigation vs over-reaction.** Pre-authorising disruptive actions shortens incidents but risks over-reaction on false alarms; keep the confirm step strict so disruptive mitigation follows evidence.","**No human delay vs hidden behaviour.** Automating remediation removes human delay but hides the system's behaviour from operators; keep humans in the loop for actions with customer-visible or irreversible effects.","**Precision vs navigability.** Many narrow runbooks match alerts precisely but become hard to navigate; a short index by symptom helps when the triggering alert is not obvious."],"pitfalls":["**Explaining the architecture instead of giving steps.** Symptom: an on-call who read the page and still had to page the original engineer. Diagnose: in the incident timeline, find the step where they stopped and whether it named an exact flag or query. Fix: imperative, exact steps.","**Never testing them.** Symptom: a step references a flag, dashboard or command renamed months ago, discovered mid-incident. Diagnose: check every flag and dashboard a runbook names against the live config, plus its last-tested date. Fix: game days and a last-tested date.","**Alerts with no linked runbook.** Symptom: repeated pages that each get the same ad-hoc response and no consistent mitigation time. Diagnose: lint the alert rules for a missing `runbook_url`. Fix: fail CI on it.","**Leaving out AI-specific situations** such as cost spikes, agent loops, guardrail-block surges or prompt-injection reports. Symptom: a generic \"service degraded\" response to a problem that needed a kill switch. Fix: write the four agent runbooks in the table above before go-live.","**A runbook step with no lever behind it.** Symptom: \"disable the CRM tool\" is written down, but the agent has no per-tool flag, so the only option is a full redeploy. Fix: build the lever with the tool.","**Keeping runbooks only behind the system's own login** or on infrastructure that fails with it. Symptom: in an identity or network outage the on-call cannot open the page that tells them what to do. Fix: a mirrored or offline copy outside the blast radius.","**Writing them only for the vendor team.** Symptom: a customer operations team that escalates every page during hypercare because the runbooks assume access and context they do not have. Diagnose: track escalation rate per runbook. Fix: rewrite them in the customer's tools and access model, then rehearse with their on-call."],"when":"- **Use for:** every alert that pages a human, and every known failure mode a different team will\n  handle, before go-live and as a readiness-review item.\n- **Start with the likeliest and most harmful situations:** provider outage, rate limits, cost runaway,\n  agent loops, index staleness, unsafe output or action reports, authentication failures to customer\n  systems.\n- **Measure:** time to mitigate, how often responders escalate despite the runbook, and how often an\n  incident finds a runbook wrong.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes runbooks for the failure modes they built in, with exact flags and queries, and adds a kill switch or fallback path wherever a runbook needs one that does not exist.","architect":"Ensures the system exposes the levers runbooks need (fallback routing, per-tenant limits, tool disablement) and defines which actions are pre-authorised for on-call.","fde":"Rewrites runbooks for the customer's operators, in their tools and access model, and runs a game day with them before handoff so the procedures are proven in their environment."},"check":{"q":"During hypercare the customer's on-call escalated every one of six incidents to your team, even\nthough runbooks existed. What would you investigate and change?","a":"I would review each incident with the on-call to see where they stopped. Common causes: the\nrunbook assumed access they lack (a vendor dashboard or cloud account); steps were vague (\"check the\nmodel\") instead of exact; the confirm step did not distinguish their situation so they did not trust the\nmitigation; or disruptive actions were not explicitly pre-authorised, so they waited for permission. Fixes\nfollow from the cause: rewrite with their tools and links, add decision branches, state pre-authorisation\nin writing with their management's agreement, then run a game day so they execute each runbook once\nunder realistic conditions."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do runbooks put mitigation steps before diagnosis?","options":["Diagnosis is the vendor's responsibility, not the on-call's","Stopping user harm is urgent, while root cause can be found afterwards from preserved evidence","Diagnosis cannot be written down in advance","Mitigation steps are easier to automate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The incident's cost accrues while users are affected, so restoring service comes first. Recording timelines and trace IDs preserves what the postmortem needs, so diagnosis is deferred, not lost."},{"q":"Which runbook step is most likely to stall an incident?","options":["\"Set flag llm.route=fallback\"","\"If one tenant exceeds 50% of traffic, go to step 6\"","\"Check the logs for anything unusual\"","\"Verify upstream error rate stays below 1% for 15 minutes\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Vague instructions force the responder to reason from scratch, which is what runbooks exist to avoid. The other steps are exact actions, explicit branches or measurable recovery conditions."},{"q":"A team automated a runbook's mitigation as an auto-remediation job. What should happen to the runbook?","options":["Delete it, since the automation now handles the situation","Freeze it as historical documentation","Keep it, describing what the automation does and what to do if the automation fails or does not fire","Convert it into a postmortem template"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Automation fails in unusual conditions, which is when operators most need context. The runbook becomes the fallback for the automation and explains its behaviour so responders do not fight it."}]},{"key":"deployment/health-checks","sources":["Official docs: Kubernetes (liveness, readiness and startup probes)"],"why":"- **Infrastructure cannot see inside an instance.** Without checks, traffic keeps flowing to a process\n  that has deadlocked, run out of GPU memory, or is still loading a multi-gigabyte model, while every\n  dashboard shows \"running\".\n- **Rolling deploys depend on them.** A new version is only safe to receive traffic once it says it is\n  ready.\n- **A single shallow `/health` returning 200 is too shallow.** A server can answer HTTP while its model\n  failed to load or its connection pool is exhausted.\n- **A single deep check wired to restarts is dangerous.** Checking every dependency (database, vector\n  store, LLM provider) in the restart probe means one provider blip fails every instance at once: a\n  fleet-wide restart storm with cold model loads on top.\n- **\"Healthy\" is not one question.** Restart, route traffic, and \"does the agent work end to end\" each\n  get their own check.","how":"### The four checks\n| Check | Question | Checks | Action on failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Liveness** | Is this process irrecoverably stuck? | Event loop responds, no deadlocked worker | Restart (kubelet, after repeated failures) |\n| **Readiness** | Can it serve a request successfully right now? | Model loaded, GPU usable, not overloaded, not shutting down | Remove from Service endpoints, no restart |\n| **Startup** | Has slow initialisation finished? | Weights loaded, GPU warmed, in-memory index built | Liveness and readiness wait until it passes |\n| **Synthetic end-to-end** | Does the agent complete a known task correctly? | Golden task through the real entry point, tools included | Alert and dashboard, never restart |\n\n### Liveness: should this process be restarted?\n- **Check only what a restart would fix:** the event loop responds, a worker thread is not deadlocked.\n- **Never check external dependencies**, because restarting your service does not fix someone else's\n  outage, and fleet-scale restarts add load exactly when the system is weakest.\n\n### Readiness: should this instance receive traffic right now?\n- **Check local conditions only.** In Kubernetes a failing readiness probe removes the pod from the\n  Service's endpoints without restarting it, so traffic flows to other replicas.\n- **Shared dependencies are risky here.** If every replica checks the same LLM provider and it fails, every\n  replica goes unready and the service has zero endpoints, so even a fallback path cannot be served.\n  Instead keep serving and degrade (fallback model, cached answer, clear error) and report the\n  dependency through metrics and alerts.\n- **Drain before exiting:** stop new requests, then finish in-flight ones, because long LLM streams cut\n  mid-answer are user-visible errors on every deploy. Behind a plain load balancer, fail readiness first\n  and wait before exit.\n- **Kubernetes removes a terminating pod from endpoints itself, but asynchronously**, so the usual pattern\n  is a short pre-stop delay plus a graceful-shutdown timeout longer than the longest stream.\n- **Agent runs can outlast any sensible grace period.** A multi-minute tool-calling run should checkpoint\n  its state so another replica can resume it, rather than forcing a grace period long enough for the\n  slowest run.\n\n### Startup: has slow initialisation finished?\n- **Loading weights, warming a GPU or building an in-memory index can take minutes.** A startup probe\n  covers that window; Kubernetes does not run liveness and readiness until it succeeds.\n- **Without it** teams either set a huge liveness delay (slow to detect real hangs later) or watch pods\n  get killed mid-load in a restart loop.\n\n### Synthetic end-to-end checks\n- **Probes describe one instance, not the product.** A synthetic check runs from outside on a schedule:\n  a fixed golden request through the real entry point (auth, retrieval, model, tools, guardrails),\n  asserting on the answer, for example that it cites the expected document.\n- **It catches what probes never see:** a stale index, an expired credential to a customer system, a model\n  silently changed behind an alias, a tool whose schema changed so every call fails.\n- **For agents, assert on the path too:** the expected tool was called, the step count stayed under budget,\n  and no write tool fired on a read-only task.\n- **Results feed alerts and dashboards, not restarts.**\n\n### Probe timing is arithmetic, not taste\nEach probe acts only after `failureThreshold` consecutive failures, checked every `periodSeconds`:\n\n```\nreaction_time ≈ periodSeconds × failureThreshold   (+ up to timeoutSeconds per attempt)\n```\n\n| Probe | What the product means | How to set it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Startup** | Window for initialisation | Exceed the worst case, not the typical one. Weights load in 90 seconds normally but 4 minutes on a cold node pulling from object storage: `periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 30` gives a 300-second window; a 60-second window kills every pod on a cold node and loops forever |\n| **Liveness** | How long a hung process keeps its share of traffic before restart | Short detects hangs fast but also restarts pods merely slow under a load spike (a long GC pause, a CPU-starved event loop) |\n| **Readiness** | How long an overloaded or draining instance keeps getting new requests | The matching `successThreshold` decides how quickly it is trusted again |\n\n- **Choose from measured load times and latency, not copied defaults.** That mostly separates probes\n  that help from probes that cause outages.\n\n### Making checks cheap and honest\n- **Fast, and never calling the LLM**, because probes run often.\n- **Deeper checks on a background schedule; the endpoint returns the cached result**, because a probe that\n  waits on a slow dependency times out and reports failure for the wrong reason.\n- **Return a body saying which condition failed**, so operators following a runbook see why an instance is\n  unready.","example":"### FastAPI service with separate probes\nA FastAPI agent service that loads a model at startup, separates liveness from readiness and reports\noverload. The model loader is a stand-in for real initialisation (run with FastAPI 0.128 and uvicorn\n0.39: probes return 503 for about 5 seconds, then 200).\n\nIt runs as a background task because an `await` inside the lifespan would delay the server from\naccepting connections until loading ends: probes would see connection refused rather than a 503 with a\nreason.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Response\n\nstate = {\"model\": None, \"in_flight\": 0}\nMAX_IN_FLIGHT = 32                       # illustrative per-replica concurrency budget\n\nasync def load_model():\n    await asyncio.sleep(5)               # stand-in; real blocking loads go in asyncio.to_thread\n    state[\"model\"] = object()\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app):\n    task = asyncio.create_task(load_model())   # load in background so probes answer meanwhile\n    yield\n    task.cancel()\n\napp = FastAPI(lifespan=lifespan)\n\n@app.get(\"/livez\")                      # restart only if the process itself is stuck\nasync def livez():\n    return {\"ok\": True}                 # reaching this handler proves the event loop runs\n\n@app.get(\"/startupz\")                   # gates liveness and readiness until the model is loaded\nasync def startupz(resp: Response):\n    if state[\"model\"] is None:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"model_loaded\": state[\"model\"] is not None}\n\n@app.get(\"/readyz\")                     # route traffic here only if a request would succeed now\nasync def readyz(resp: Response):\n    reasons = []\n    if state[\"model\"] is None: reasons.append(\"model not loaded\")\n    if state[\"in_flight\"] >= MAX_IN_FLIGHT: reasons.append(\"at capacity\")\n    if reasons:\n        resp.status_code = 503\n    return {\"ready\": not reasons, \"reasons\": reasons}\n```\n\n- **No check calls the LLM provider, the vector store or any tool.** Their health is reported by metrics\n  and a separate synthetic check, so an upstream outage degrades the agent instead of emptying the load\n  balancer.\n- **A real deployment increments and decrements `in_flight`** around each agent run (including the whole\n  duration of a streamed response and every tool call in between).\n- **Draining is configured in the platform**, for example a Kubernetes pre-stop delay, rather than by\n  overriding the server's own signal handling.\n\n### Kubernetes probe configuration for the service above\nIllustrative values for a model that loads in 90 seconds typically and up to 4 minutes on a cold node.\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 120      # longer than the longest streamed answer\n  containers:\n    - name: assistant\n      image: registry.example.internal/assistant:1.4.2\n      ports:\n        - containerPort: 8000\n      startupProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /startupz, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        failureThreshold: 30              # 300 s window, above the 4-minute worst case\n      livenessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /livez, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 10\n        failureThreshold: 3               # about 30 s of hang before restart\n        timeoutSeconds: 2\n      readinessProbe:\n        httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: 8000 }\n        periodSeconds: 5\n        failureThreshold: 2               # about 10 s before routing away\n        successThreshold: 1\n      lifecycle:\n        preStop:\n          exec:\n            command: [\"sleep\", \"10\"]      # let endpoint removal propagate before SIGTERM\n            # exec needs a sleep binary in the image (distroless has none); Kubernetes 1.30+\n            # also offers the built-in handler  preStop: { sleep: { seconds: 10 } }\n```\n\n### A golden agent task as a synthetic check\nRun from outside the cluster every few minutes. It sends a fixed task through the public entry point and\nasserts on the answer and the path. The endpoint and response fields are those of your own agent API;\nthe ones here are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport time, httpx\n\nGOLDEN = {\"task\": \"What is the excess on policy P-1001 for water damage?\"}\n\ndef synthetic_check(base_url, token):\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    r = httpx.post(f\"{base_url}/v1/agent/runs\", json=GOLDEN,\n                   headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}, timeout=60)\n    took = time.monotonic() - t0\n    r.raise_for_status()\n    run = r.json()                                   # {\"answer\", \"citations\", \"tool_calls\"}\n    tools = [c[\"tool\"] for c in run[\"tool_calls\"]]\n    failures = []\n    if \"lookup_policy\" not in tools: failures.append(\"expected tool not called\")\n    if \"doc-P-1001\" not in run[\"citations\"]: failures.append(\"expected document not cited\")\n    if len(tools) > 6: failures.append(f\"{len(tools)} tool calls, budget 6\")\n    if any(t.startswith(\"update_\") for t in tools): failures.append(\"write tool on read-only task\")\n    if took > 20: failures.append(f\"slow: {took:.1f}s\")\n    return failures                                  # empty list = pass; alert after 2 in a row\n```\n\n- **Every probe can be green while this fails:** an expired credential to the policy system, a renamed tool\n  parameter, or a stale index all break the golden task without touching any instance's local state.\n- **Keep it read-only and cheap:** one fixed task per critical path, costing a handful of model calls\n  and never changing customer data.\n\n### Worked scenario: the 90-second provider blip\n| Design | What happens during a 90-second provider outage |\n|---|---|\n| Provider checked in **liveness** | Every pod fails together, all restart, each reloads weights; recovery takes many minutes after the provider is back, and every in-flight agent run is lost |\n| Provider checked in **readiness** | No restarts, but every pod goes unready; zero endpoints for roughly the outage's duration, so even the fallback cannot be served |\n| Provider in **metrics and synthetic check only** | Pods stay ready; requests use the fallback model or return a clear error; an alert links to the runbook |","patterns":["**Separate endpoints per probe.** Expose `/livez`, `/readyz` and `/startupz` with distinct logic (the first two follow the naming the Kubernetes API server uses for its own health endpoints), and wire each to the matching probe. Monitor container restart counts and ready replica count per deployment.","**Startup window from measured cold loads.** Measure model load time on a cold node (image pull plus weight download), then set startup `periodSeconds × failureThreshold` above the worst case with margin. Monitor startup duration percentiles after each model or image change.","**Pre-stop delay plus graceful timeout.** Add a short pre-stop sleep so endpoint removal propagates, and set `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` above the longest stream; the server should finish in-flight requests on SIGTERM. Monitor client-side disconnects and truncated streams during deploys.","**Golden-task synthetic check.** Run a scheduled job (every few minutes) from outside the cluster that sends a fixed task through the public entry point and asserts on a cited document ID, the tools called and the step count. Alert on consecutive failures and track synthetic latency separately.","**Capacity-aware readiness with autoscaling.** Fail readiness at a per-replica in-flight budget, and pair it with a horizontal autoscaler and a front-door rate limit so the fleet never reaches zero ready endpoints. Monitor the fraction of replicas reporting \"at capacity\"."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Your agent depends on an LLM provider, a vector store and several tools,\n  all outside the process. Wire any of them into liveness and one provider blip restarts the whole fleet,\n  killing every in-flight agent run.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `/livez`, `/readyz` and `/startupz` endpoints in the agent's API\n  server, Kubernetes probe settings, a pre-stop delay and grace period for long runs, and a scheduled\n  golden agent task from outside the cluster.\n- **What you control.** What each probe checks (local only), probe timing from measured load times, the\n  per-replica in-flight budget for agent runs, how long draining waits, and the golden task's assertions.\n- **What you can skip.** Health-checking every tool on each probe. Report tool and provider health\n  through metrics, alerts and the synthetic check, never through restart or routing probes.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Self-hosted model behind an agent.** A startup probe sized to cold weight loads stops restart loops\n  when nodes scale up.\n- **Long-running tool-calling agents.** Draining plus checkpointing keeps deploys from killing\n  multi-minute runs.\n- **RAG agents over customer content.** A golden-task synthetic check catches stale indexes, expired\n  connector credentials and broken tools that every probe misses.","mini_project":"**Health checks for a toy agent service (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent API whose probes survive a provider outage and whose synthetic check catches a broken\n  tool.\n- **Steps:** build a FastAPI agent with `/livez`, `/readyz` (model loaded, in-flight budget) and\n  `/startupz`; stub the LLM provider and one tool behind a switch; write a golden-task script asserting\n  on the tool called and a cited ID; flip the provider switch off and then break the tool's schema.\n- **Done when:** with the provider down, readiness stays green and requests get a clear degraded answer;\n  with the tool broken, every probe stays green while the synthetic check reports the exact failure two\n  runs in a row."},"exercise":"**Goal:** give a toy agent service probes that survive a provider outage, then prove it with failure\ninjection. No solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** a FastAPI (or similar) agent with a stubbed LLM provider, one stubbed tool, and a model\n  load you can make slow.\n- **Constraints:** no probe makes an outbound call; probe timings are derived from load times you\n  measured, written next to the config.\n- **Deliver:** the endpoints, a probe config (Kubernetes or your platform's), a golden-task script, and a\n  short log of each injected failure and what every check reported.\n- **Acceptance:** with the provider stubbed down, readiness stays 200 and users get a degraded answer;\n  doubling load time still fits the startup window; a renamed tool parameter fails the golden task two\n  runs in a row while every probe stays green.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** a customer's load balancer supports one health URL per pool and checks it every 5 seconds,\n3 failures to eject. Your agent runs on their VMs, calls a hosted LLM through their proxy, and runs last\nup to 8 minutes.\n\nWhich conditions go behind that URL, and how do you deploy without cutting runs?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- puts only local conditions behind it (model or client initialised, in-flight budget, draining flag),\n  never the proxy or the LLM, so one proxy blip does not eject every VM;\n- first asks whether a failed check only ejects or also replaces the VM (some autoscaling groups do);\n  if it replaces, the in-flight budget stays out;\n- notes ejection takes about 15 seconds, so draining fails the check first, waits that long, then\n  finishes runs;\n- checkpoints runs per tool step because 8 minutes exceeds any sane drain;\n- watches the proxy and provider through metrics and a golden task from inside their network.","diagram":{"code":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Starting\n    Starting --> Starting: startup probe fails, under threshold\n    Starting --> Restarted: startup window exceeded\n    Starting --> Ready: startup passes, readiness passes\n    Ready --> NotReady: readiness fails\n    NotReady --> Ready: readiness passes again\n    Ready --> Restarted: liveness fails threshold times\n    NotReady --> Restarted: liveness fails threshold times\n    Ready --> Draining: termination signal\n    Draining --> [*]: in-flight runs finished or checkpointed\n    Restarted --> Starting","caption":"Kubernetes runs the startup probe first; after it passes, liveness and readiness run independently and\ntrigger different actions. Draining lets in-flight agent runs finish or checkpoint."},"interview":[{"q":"Your model takes 90 seconds to load, sometimes 4 minutes. How do you configure the probes?","a":"- **Startup probe sized to the worst case:** reaction time is about `periodSeconds × failureThreshold`, so\n  10 × 30 gives 300 seconds; a 60-second window would loop forever on cold nodes.\n- **Then short liveness and readiness**, which Kubernetes only starts after startup passes, so hangs are\n  still detected quickly later."},{"q":"Agent runs take several minutes and are cut off during every deploy. What do you change?","a":"- **Drain properly:** a short pre-stop delay so endpoint removal propagates, then a grace period longer than\n  the longest streamed answer so in-flight requests finish.\n- **Checkpoint long runs:** persist state after each tool step so a run on a terminating pod resumes on\n  another replica; verify by counting runs resumed versus lost and truncated streams per rollout."},{"q":"All probes are green but users report the agent gives wrong answers. What check is missing?","a":"- **A synthetic end-to-end check:** probes describe local instance state, so a stale index, expired\n  customer credential, broken tool or model silently changed behind an alias leaves every probe green.\n- **Design it as a golden task:** sent through the real entry point, asserting on a cited document, the\n  tools called and the step count, feeding alerts rather than restarts."},{"q":"Should the agent's readiness probe check that its tools are reachable?","a":"- **No, for shared tools:** if every replica checks the same CRM or search API and it fails, all go unready\n  together and nothing, not even a degraded answer, can be served.\n- **Yes, for per-instance dependencies:** a local GPU or a sidecar only that pod uses, where routing away\n  from this replica actually helps. Tool health belongs in metrics, alerts and the synthetic task."}],"tradeoffs":["**Detection vs coupling.** Deep readiness checks catch more broken instances but couple every replica to shared dependencies; the balance flips toward deep checks only for dependencies that are per-instance, such as a local GPU.","**Fast detection vs flapping.** Aggressive probe intervals and low failure thresholds detect problems fast but cause flapping and needless restarts under load spikes; tolerate a few consecutive failures before acting.","**Graceful shedding vs empty endpoints.** Load-based readiness (\"at capacity\") sheds traffic gracefully, but if every replica hits capacity at once the service has no endpoints.","**End-to-end proof vs cost and drift.** Synthetic checks prove end-to-end correctness, but each costs real model and tool calls and can drift from real usage; keep them few, cheap and representative of the critical path."],"pitfalls":["**Checking the LLM provider or database in liveness.** Symptom: every pod restarts at once during a short upstream incident, followed by slow cold starts. Diagnose: line up container restart counts with the provider's error-rate graph, and read the probe handler for outbound calls. Fix: liveness is local only.","**No startup probe for a service loading large weights.** Symptom: pods killed by liveness during model loading, stuck in a restart loop. Diagnose: compare measured cold-node load time with the window `periodSeconds × failureThreshold`. Fix: a startup probe sized to the worst case.","**Exiting without draining**, so new requests still arrive during shutdown. Symptom: streamed responses and agent runs cut off mid-answer on every deploy or scale-down. Diagnose: count truncated streams and lost runs per rollout. Fix: pre-stop delay, graceful timeout, and checkpoints for long runs.","**Only shallow HTTP pings.** Symptom: instances report healthy while every request fails because the model never loaded or a GPU errored. Fix: readiness checks model and GPU state.","**Treating green probes as proof the agent works.** Symptom: all-green dashboards while users get wrong answers from a stale index or a broken tool. Diagnose: compare synthetic pass rate and user-reported failures with probe status. Fix: a golden task asserting on the answer and the tool path."],"when":"- **Every deployed agent service:** liveness and readiness.\n- **Slow initialisation:** add a startup probe.\n- **Customer-facing agent:** at least one synthetic golden task on the critical path, asserting on answer\n  and tool path.\n- **Decide scope by the action triggered:** restart, route away, or alert.\n- **Measure:** probe failure counts, restart counts, ready replica count and synthetic pass rate, with each\n  health alert linked to a runbook.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements separate liveness, readiness and startup endpoints, keeps them fast and dependency-free, and writes the golden-request synthetic check with a meaningful assertion.","architect":"Decides which failures cause restarts, which cause routing changes and which only alert, so a shared dependency outage degrades the service rather than taking it down.","fde":"Adapts checks to the customer's load balancers and monitoring, which may only support one endpoint, and makes synthetic checks run inside their network against their real integrations."},"check":{"q":"After a 90-second outage at the LLM provider, the whole fleet restarted and the service was down\nfor twelve minutes. What went wrong in the health check design, and how would you change it?","a":"The provider was almost certainly checked by the liveness probe: readiness failures only remove\npods from endpoints and never restart them, so a fleet-wide restart points at liveness (a provider check\nin readiness would instead have emptied the endpoints for roughly the outage's duration). When the\nprovider failed, every replica failed at once; liveness restarted them all, and each restart\nmeant reloading model weights or warming caches, so recovery took far longer than the upstream outage.\nI would make liveness local only (process responsive), keep readiness to per-instance conditions such as\nmodel loaded, capacity and draining, and report provider health through metrics, alerts and a synthetic\ncheck. The request path should degrade with a fallback model or clear error, and add a startup probe so\nslow loads are not killed."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens in Kubernetes when a pod fails its readiness probe but passes liveness?","options":["The container is restarted","The pod is removed from Service endpoints and stops receiving traffic, without a restart","The pod is rescheduled onto another node","Nothing, readiness is informational only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readiness controls routing: an unready pod is taken out of the Service's endpoints until it passes again. Restarts are driven by liveness failures, and rescheduling is a scheduler decision, not a probe result."},{"q":"Why should a liveness probe avoid checking the external LLM provider?","options":["Provider calls are too expensive to run every few seconds","Liveness probes cannot make outbound network calls","The provider's own status page already reports its health","Restarting your instances cannot fix the provider, and simultaneous restarts turn a brief upstream issue into a fleet-wide outage"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Cost is a real concern, but the decisive reason is the action liveness triggers. A restart only helps for failures inside the process; for shared dependencies it multiplies the damage."},{"q":"Which failure would a synthetic end-to-end check catch that liveness and readiness probes would not?","options":["A deadlocked worker thread","A pod that has not finished loading model weights","A retrieval index that stopped updating, so answers cite outdated documents","A pod that is shutting down"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Probes check one instance's local condition. A stale index leaves every instance healthy while answers are wrong, which only a check asserting on real end-to-end output can detect."}]},{"key":"deployment/hypercare","sources":[],"why":"- **Production is the full distribution; UAT only sampled it.** Every user, document type, odd workflow\n  and integration under real load arrives at once.\n- **The gap is larger for AI**, because quality depends on inputs the team has never seen: queries phrased\n  in ways the golden set did not anticipate, the scanned fax from the one branch office that still uses\n  them, users pasting whole email threads into the assistant.\n- **Adoption is fragile in the first days.** Users switching from a manual process form their opinion\n  fast. Early failures met with silence or a two-week ticket queue send them back to the old way, and\n  usage never recovers even after the bugs are fixed.\n- **\"Go-live is the finish line\" fails.** Standard support is tuned for a stable system with known issues:\n  it cannot tell a prompt regression from a user-training gap and has no route to change the prompt,\n  retrieval, tools or model.\n- **Open-ended hypercare fails the other way:** the delivery team quietly becomes the customer's permanent\n  operations team. Hypercare bridges the two: concentrated expertise for a bounded time, with a defined\n  exit.","how":"### Plan it before go-live\n- **Agree it in the readiness review:** duration, staffing, hours of coverage, escalation paths, what\n  counts as an incident versus a request, and exit criteria.\n- **Duration is a planning choice, not a standard.** A few weeks is common, scaled to risk and user count.\n- **Plan in advance** because the customer's support organisation, the vendor team and the sponsor must\n  know who answers what before the first problem arrives.\n- **Fix the mechanics:** a single intake channel so issues are not scattered across email and chat;\n  severity definitions with an agreed response time for each; coverage hours that match when users work\n  (including other time zones); who on each side is reachable out of hours for a severity-1.\n\n### Heightened monitoring\n- **Tighten dashboards and alert thresholds**, and have someone actively watch them rather than wait for\n  pages.\n- **Watch AI signals that rarely page anyone:** user feedback rates, escalation-to-human rate, guardrail\n  blocks, retrieval misses (\"no relevant documents found\"), cost per task.\n- **Add agent signals:** task completion rate, steps per task, step-budget hits, tool-call error rate, and\n  how often humans reject an action the agent asked approval for. A rising rejection rate is an early sign\n  the agent proposes wrong actions.\n- **Read a daily sample of real conversations and traces.** It is the fastest way to learn where the agent\n  fails, because metrics show that something changed while traces show why: which tool, which argument,\n  which retrieved document.\n\n### A daily triage loop\nEvery issue, ticket and piece of negative feedback is classified by cause, which decides the owner and the\nfix. A short daily review with the customer keeps both sides on the same list.\n\n| Cause | Meaning | Owner and fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Defect** | System bug, integration or tool failure | Engineers |\n| **Quality** | Retrieval, tool choice or model produced a poor result | Prompt, tool-description or retrieval tuning |\n| **Knowledge gap** | The answer is not in the indexed content | Customer's content owners |\n| **Training** | User misunderstood what the agent does | Enablement |\n| **Change request** | New scope | Sponsor |\n\n### Fast but safe fixes\n- **Faster cadence, same gates.** Every quality fix still passes the regression suite, and every real\n  failure becomes a new regression case, because a prompt change fixing today's complaint can quietly\n  break a case UAT already accepted.\n- **Same rollout criteria, shorter stages**, not around them.\n- **Freeze everything else.** A model upgrade, a new feature or tool, or an index rebuild for another team\n  waits until hypercare ends, because feedback signals are already noisy and two changes in one week make\n  it impossible to say which moved the thumbs-down rate.\n- **Batch fixes into one or two predictable releases a week**, each with a written note of what changed,\n  so every movement in the metrics stays attributable.\n\n### Exit criteria and handoff\nHypercare ends when written criteria hold for a sustained period, for example:\n1. No open severity-1 or severity-2 issues.\n2. Incident and ticket volume stable or falling over consecutive weeks, **normalised by active users**\n   (because volume also falls when people stop using the system) and read alongside usage retention.\n3. Quality metrics at or above the UAT baseline on real traffic.\n4. The customer's support team resolves issues using the runbooks without escalating.\n5. The known-issues list and backlog are handed over with owners.\n\nThe handoff is explicit: a meeting, a signed-off document and a named date when the support model changes.","example":"### Worked scenario: an HR policy agent\nAn HR policy agent for a 4,000-employee company, with retrieval over policy documents and a tool to open\nHR cases. Three weeks of planned hypercare.\n\nExit criteria agreed at the readiness review: zero open sev-1/sev-2, weekly actionable issues below 10\nfor two consecutive weeks, thumbs-down rate at or below the 6% observed in UAT, and the customer's\nservice desk resolving at least 80% of tickets without vendor escalation.\n\n| Week | Users | Actionable issues | Thumbs-down | Desk unaided |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1,900 | 41 | 11% | n/a |\n| 2 | 2,600 | 18 | 7% | 65% |\n| 3 | 2,900 | 9 | 5.8% | 78% |\n\n- **Week 1 triage:** 9 defects (most from single sign-on timeouts for one region), 14 quality, 11\n  knowledge gaps (the parental-leave policy for one country was never in the indexed content), 5\n  training, 2 change requests.\n- **Week 1 fixes:** the team fixes SSO, the customer's HR content owner uploads the missing policy, the 14\n  quality failures become regression cases, and a retrieval fix passes the suite and ships behind a flag.\n- **Week 2 near miss:** a prompt change improved answer tone but dropped citations on long answers. The\n  regression suite caught it in staging, so it never shipped and never became a production incident.\n- **Week 3 is real improvement:** per active user, issues fell from about 2.2 per 100 users in week 1 to\n  about 0.3 while the user count kept rising, so the drop is not people giving up.\n- **But exit is not met:** volume is below 10 for only one week and desk resolution is below 80%. Rather\n  than letting hypercare drift, the team extends it by one week with a single focus: a runbook session\n  with the service desk on the three ticket types they still escalate. Week 4 meets all criteria and\n  support is handed over.\n\n### Exit criteria as code\nThe same criteria, evaluated from weekly numbers so the exit decision is not argued from impressions.\n\n```python\nweeks = [  # open_sev12 values are illustrative\n    {\"week\": 1, \"users\": 1900, \"issues\": 41, \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.11,  \"desk_unaided\": None},\n    {\"week\": 2, \"users\": 2600, \"issues\": 18, \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.07,  \"desk_unaided\": 0.65},\n    {\"week\": 3, \"users\": 2900, \"issues\": 9,  \"open_sev12\": 0, \"thumbs_down\": 0.058, \"desk_unaided\": 0.78},\n]\n\ndef exit_check(weeks, max_issues=10, td_baseline=0.06, desk_min=0.80):\n    unmet = []\n    last = weeks[-1]\n    if last[\"open_sev12\"] > 0:\n        unmet.append(\"open sev-1/sev-2\")\n    if len(weeks) < 2 or any(w[\"issues\"] >= max_issues for w in weeks[-2:]):\n        unmet.append(\"issues below 10 for two consecutive weeks\")\n    if last[\"thumbs_down\"] > td_baseline:\n        unmet.append(\"thumbs-down above UAT baseline\")\n    if (last[\"desk_unaided\"] or 0) < desk_min:\n        unmet.append(\"desk resolution below 80%\")\n    return unmet\n\nfor w in weeks:\n    print(w[\"week\"], f\"{100 * w['issues'] / w['users']:.1f} issues per 100 users\")\nprint(exit_check(weeks))\n# 1 2.2 issues per 100 users\n# 2 0.7 issues per 100 users\n# 3 0.3 issues per 100 users\n# ['issues below 10 for two consecutive weeks', 'desk resolution below 80%']\n```\n\n### Daily trace sample, biased toward failures\nA uniform sample of agent runs is mostly successes. This picks the morning's reading list by\nover-sampling runs that already look wrong, while keeping some normal runs to catch silent failures.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\ndef daily_sample(runs, n=40, seed=None):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    flagged = [r for r in runs if r[\"thumbs_down\"] or r[\"hit_step_limit\"]\n               or r[\"tool_errors\"] > 0 or r[\"approval_rejected\"]]\n    normal = [r for r in runs if r not in flagged]\n    k_flag = min(len(flagged), n // 2)                 # up to half the sample from flagged runs\n    picked = rng.sample(flagged, k_flag) + rng.sample(normal, min(len(normal), n - k_flag))\n    return [r[\"trace_id\"] for r in picked]\n```\n\n| Agent signal, week 1 | Value | What the trace reading found |\n|---|---|---|\n| Step-budget hits | 3% of runs | The agent re-searched with near-identical queries when no policy matched (often a knowledge gap) |\n| Approval rejections on `open_hr_case` | 18% of proposals | Cases opened for questions the policy already answered; fixed in the tool description |\n| Tool-call errors | 1% | SSO timeouts in one region (the defect above) |\n\nThese week-1 agent signal values are illustrative; the point is that each number led to a trace, and each\ntrace to a cause and an owner.","patterns":["**Single intake channel with a cause field.** Route every report (tickets, chat, feedback buttons) into one tracker queue whose required fields include cause and severity. Monitor issues per 100 active users by cause each day.","**Daily trace sampling.** Pull a fixed sample of real agent runs each morning, over-weighting flagged runs, under the data-access rules agreed in the readiness review, and have one person read them before triage. Monitor new failure patterns found per day; when it reaches zero for several days, sampling can drop in frequency.","**Agent signal board.** Track task completion, steps per task, step-budget hits, tool errors, approval rejections and human handoffs daily, each linked to example traces. Monitor day-over-day change after each fix release.","**Change freeze with a fix train.** Only hypercare fixes ship, batched into one or two scheduled releases a week, each passing the regression suite and a shortened rollout with a written change note. Monitor metric shifts against release notes so every movement is attributable.","**Shadow-then-lead support transfer.** The customer's service desk first shadows the vendor on tickets, then leads with vendor backup, using the runbooks. Monitor the share of tickets resolved without vendor escalation against the exit threshold."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Production is the first time your agent meets the full spread of user\n  phrasing, documents and tool responses. Its failures (wrong tool, a loop, a confident answer from\n  missing content) are found fastest by reading real runs in the first weeks.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A daily sample of agent traces, dashboards for task completion,\n  steps per task, tool errors, approval rejections and handoffs to humans, one intake queue with a cause\n  field, and a regression suite that every fix must pass.\n- **What you control.** Which signals you watch, how traces are sampled, how fixes are batched and gated,\n  the change freeze, and the exit criteria that end your team's involvement.\n- **What you can skip.** Building new features. Hypercare is for fixing what real traffic exposes, and\n  anything else waits until it ends.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **First weeks of a customer-facing support or HR agent.** Daily trace review and cause triage turn real\n  failures into fixes and regression cases while adoption is still forming.\n- **Agents with approval-gated write tools.** Approval rejection rate during hypercare shows whether the\n  agent proposes the right actions before approvals are relaxed.\n- **Handing an agent to a customer's service desk.** Shadow-then-lead transfer and an unaided-resolution\n  threshold decide when the vendor steps back.\n- **Model or prompt fixes under a freeze.** A weekly fix train keeps every change attributable while\n  signals are noisy.","mini_project":"**Run a simulated hypercare week for an agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn simulated agent runs into a daily triage list, fixes and an exit decision.\n- **Steps:** generate or log 300 runs (one day) from a toy RAG agent with a write tool, including thumbs-down,\n  step-limit hits and rejected approvals; build the biased daily sample of 40; read and label each by\n  cause; add every quality failure to a regression file; compute issues per 100 users and the exit check\n  for three simulated weeks.\n- **Done when:** you have a cause breakdown with an owner per cause, at least five new regression cases,\n  and an exit check that names exactly which criteria are unmet and the single focus for an extension."},"exercise":"**Goal:** write the hypercare plan for an agent you have built and run one simulated week against it.\nNo solution is given.\n- **Inputs:** at least 300 logged or generated runs with feedback, step counts, tool errors and approval\n  outcomes, plus a user count per day.\n- **Constraints:** exit criteria are written before you look at the data; every criterion is measurable\n  from logs; volume is normalised by active users.\n- **Deliver:** the plan (duration, intake, severities, freeze rules, exit criteria), a labelled daily\n  sample of 40 traces, and a short exit memo.\n- **Acceptance:** every sampled trace has a cause and an owner; a deliberately injected loop shows up in\n  the sample and the agent signal board; the exit memo names each unmet criterion and the single focus\n  of any extension.","mastery_test":"**Problem:** week 2 of hypercare for a procurement agent that drafts purchase orders for approval. Issues\nper 100 users fell from 3.1 to 1.2, thumbs-down is flat, but approval rejections rose from 8% to 21%.\n\nMeanwhile the customer asks to slip in a model upgrade \"while you are all here\". What do you conclude\nand do?\n\n**A strong answer:**\n- reads the falling issue rate as errors now caught silently by approvers instead of reported, so\n  quality may be worse, not better;\n- checks whether the rise lines up with a week-1 fix release, a shift in request mix, or stricter\n  approvers (rejection reasons per approver);\n- samples rejected proposals, classifies the cause (tool description, retrieval of supplier data, a\n  recent fix) and adds them as regression cases;\n- refuses the model upgrade until exit, because two changes would hide which one moved rejections;\n- adds a rejection-rate threshold to the exit criteria.","diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    G[\"Agent go-live\"] --> M[\"Monitor metrics and read traces\"]\n    M --> T{\"Daily triage by cause\"}\n    T -->|\"defect or quality\"| F[\"Fix, pass regression suite\"]\n    T -->|\"knowledge gap\"| K[\"Customer content owner\"]\n    T -->|\"training\"| EN[\"Enablement\"]\n    T -->|\"change request\"| SP[\"Sponsor backlog\"]\n    F --> R[\"Batched release, shortened rollout\"]\n    R --> X{\"Exit criteria held?\"}\n    K --> X\n    EN --> X\n    X -->|\"no\"| EXT[\"Extend with one focus\"]\n    EXT --> M\n    X -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Signed handoff to support\"]","caption":"The daily loop runs until the exit criteria hold; an unmet criterion extends hypercare with a specific\nfocus rather than letting it drift."},"interview":[{"q":"What is hypercare, and why not hand straight over to standard support at go-live?","a":"- **Definition:** a bounded period of elevated support after go-live, with closer monitoring, daily triage,\n  faster fixes and named contacts, ending on written exit criteria.\n- **Why not standard support:** it is tuned for stable systems, cannot tell a prompt regression from a\n  training gap, and cannot change the prompt, retrieval, tools or model."},{"q":"What would you watch during hypercare for an agent that can take actions?","a":"- **Agent signals beyond feedback:** task completion, steps per task, step-budget hits, tool-call errors,\n  approval rejection rate and handoffs to humans, each linked to example traces.\n- **A daily trace sample biased to flagged runs:** rejected proposals and step-limit runs show wrong\n  actions and loops before users complain about them."},{"q":"A customer complaint arrives daily about answers on a topic. How do you triage it?","a":"- **Classify by cause first:** is the content indexed (knowledge gap), retrieved but answered badly\n  (quality), broken by an integration (defect), misunderstood by users (training), or new scope (change\n  request)?\n- **Route to the owner:** a knowledge gap goes to the customer's content owners, because tuning the prompt\n  for content that is not there risks hallucinated answers."},{"q":"The exit date has arrived but criteria are not met. What do you do?","a":"- **Extend with a single focus, not open-endedly:** name the unmet criterion and the work that closes it,\n  for example a runbook session on the ticket types the desk still escalates.\n- **Do not exit on the calendar:** handing over before the customer can run the system just moves the\n  escalations into normal support."}],"tradeoffs":["**Expert coverage vs customer ownership.** Longer hypercare catches more issues with experts on hand, but ties up the delivery team and delays the customer's own ownership; extend only against unmet exit criteria, never by default.","**Fast fixes vs learning.** Embedding vendor engineers with the customer gives the fastest fixes, but can stop the customer team from learning; the balance flips once routine issues are being solved by vendor staff on request.","**Cadence vs regression risk.** A faster release cadence fixes real pain quickly but raises regression risk; it is safe only when every change passes the regression suite and a shortened rollout.","**Insight vs privacy cost.** Reading many real traces finds failure patterns early, but costs time and needs privacy approval; agree data-access rules during the readiness review, not during an incident."],"pitfalls":["**No exit criteria.** Symptom: the delivery team still answers the customer's tickets months after go-live, and nobody can say when it ends. Fix: written criteria agreed before go-live.","**Prompt tweaks that skip the regression suite.** Symptom: a fix for this week's issue reopens a case accepted in UAT. Diagnose: list releases with no suite run attached and diff reopened cases against them. Fix: no fix ships without the suite.","**Lumping all feedback into \"bugs\".** Symptom: engineers spend days on missing content or user training while real defects wait. Diagnose: chart engineering hours by triage cause. Fix: classify by cause and route to the right owner.","**Measuring only incidents and uptime.** Symptom: a quiet incident queue alongside falling usage, because unhappy users silently returned to the old process. Diagnose: plot issues per 100 active users next to weekly retention. Fix: normalise by active users and track retention.","**Reading only final answers.** Symptom: \"the answer looks fine\" while the agent took 14 steps, called a write tool it did not need, or had proposals rejected. Diagnose: check steps per task and approval rejections in the daily trace sample. Fix: read traces and watch agent signals.","**Doing the support work instead of transferring it.** Symptom: a customer service desk that escalates every ticket on the first day after handoff. Fix: the desk leads tickets before exit."],"when":"- **Use when:** real users meet a new agent, the customer's own team will eventually operate it, or\n  adoption matters commercially.\n- **Size by risk and scale:** a small internal pilot may need a few days of close attention; an enterprise\n  rollout to thousands of users, or an agent with write tools, needs weeks with dedicated staff.\n- **Track:** issue volume by cause, user feedback, usage and retention, quality on real traffic versus the\n  UAT baseline, agent signals, and the share of tickets the customer resolves alone.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns each real failure into a regression case, ships fixes through shortened rollouts, and builds the daily views of feedback, escalations and sampled transcripts used in triage.","architect":"Ensures the system supports hypercare safely: feature flags for quick fixes, regression gates that cannot be skipped, and data-access rules for reviewing real conversations.","fde":"Runs the daily triage with the customer, separates defects from content, training and scope issues, and drives the handoff so their team can operate the system without escalating."},"check":{"q":"Three weeks into hypercare, incidents are near zero but weekly active users have fallen 30% from\nweek one. The sponsor says hypercare can close. Do you agree?","a":"Not yet. A quiet incident queue with falling usage often means users stopped reporting because they\nstopped using the system. I would look at who dropped off and what they last did, read a sample of their\nfinal sessions, check feedback and abandonment by task type, and talk to a few users directly. Likely\ncauses are a quality gap on a common task, missing content, or a workflow that is slower than the old\nprocess. If the cause is fixable, extend hypercare against a specific adoption criterion; if it is a\ntraining or change-management issue, hand that to the customer's enablement owner explicitly. Closing on\nincident counts alone would hide the real failure."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should normally end a hypercare period?","options":["The planned number of weeks has passed","The delivery team is needed on another project","Written exit criteria have held for a sustained period and ownership is formally handed over","The first week with zero incidents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hypercare is bounded by criteria, not the calendar or staffing. A single quiet week can be noise or reflect falling usage, and calendar-based exits hand over a system the customer cannot yet run."},{"q":"Users report that the assistant cannot answer questions about one country's leave policy. The ingestion pipeline works, but the customer's HR team never supplied that policy for indexing. How should this be classified in triage?","options":["As a model quality defect needing prompt changes","As a user training issue","As a knowledge gap owned by the customer's content owners","As a change request to the sponsor"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The system cannot answer from content it does not have. Tuning prompts would risk hallucinated answers; the right owner is whoever controls the source content, and the fix is to ingest the missing policy."},{"q":"Why must quality fixes during hypercare still pass the regression suite?","options":["Because the customer's change-management policy always requires it","Because a prompt or retrieval change that fixes one complaint can silently break cases already accepted in UAT","Because regression suites are the only way to measure latency","Because hypercare fixes are deployed to all users at once"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AI behaviour changes are global: a prompt edit affects every query. The regression suite, extended with each real failure, is what keeps a fast fix cadence from trading one problem for another."}]}]});
